    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department with a painful rash, high fever, and severe eye soreness that began 2 weeks after starting Allopurinol. Physical exam reveals confluent, atypical targetoid macules with flaccid blisters and sloughing covering approximately 8% of the total body surface area (BSA). Two mucosal sites are severely involved. Skin biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis with minimal dermal inflammation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Erythema Multiforme Major", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS), defined by fever, mucosal involvement of $\ge 2$ sites, targetoid macules, and epidermal detachment affecting <10% of the body surface area (BSA). Biopsy characteristically shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis (apoptosis of keratinocytes) driven by cytotoxic T-cells and granulysin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old woman on Phenytoin for seizures presents with fever and widespread skin tenderness. Within 24 hours, the skin begins to peel off in large sheets. Physical examination shows dusky erythema progressing to confluent blisters. Gentle lateral pressure on the erythematous skin causes the epidermis to slide off the dermis (positive Nikolsky sign). The epidermal detachment is estimated at 35% of the total body surface area. What is the primary cause of mortality in this condition?', '["High-output cardiac failure and hypothermia", "Hepatocellular failure and acute encephalopathy", "Acute kidney injury from severe myoglobinuria", "Infection, sepsis, and multi-organ failure", "Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN), characterized by epidermal detachment of >30% BSA and a positive Nikolsky sign. The loss of the skin barrier leads to massive fluid shifts, hypothermia, and high-output heart failure, but the leading cause of death in these patients is infection (sepsis) and subsequent multi-organ failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old boy presents with a painful, blistering rash and fever. They recently started Sulfasalazine for inflammatory bowel disease. Physical examination reveals atypical targetoid plaques on the trunk and extremities, with flaccid bullae that slide off under lateral pressure. The surface area of epidermal detachment is measured at exactly 18% of the total BSA. What is the clinical category of this patient''s drug reaction?', '["SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "DRESS syndrome", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The SJS/TEN spectrum is categorized by the percentage of body surface area showing epidermal detachment: SJS involves <10% BSA detachment; SJS/TEN Overlap involves 10% to 30% BSA detachment; and TEN involves >30% BSA detachment. At 18% detachment, this patient falls into the SJS/TEN Overlap category.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old woman of East Asian descent is about to start Carbamazepine for neuropathic pain. The clinician orders genetic screening for the HLA-B*1502 allele before prescribing. What is the clinical significance of this allele?', '["It is associated with rapid metabolic clearance of Carbamazepine, making it ineffective", "It correlates with an increased risk of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia", "It indicates an increased risk of severe, irreversible agranulocytosis", "It carries an extremely high risk of Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in Asian populations", "It is a marker for drug-induced phototoxicity and exaggerated sunburns"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The HLA-B*1502 allele is strongly associated with an increased risk of developing Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in individuals of East Asian and South-East Asian descent. Screening is mandatory or highly recommended prior to starting Carbamazepine in these high-risk populations.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old boy with Han Chinese ancestry is diagnosed with gout and is about to start Allopurinol therapy. Which HLA allele is strongly associated with Allopurinol-induced Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) in this population?', '["HLA-DRB1*0401", "HLA-B*5701", "HLA-B*1502", "HLA-B*2705", "HLA-B*5801"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The HLA-B*5801 allele is a potent genetic risk factor for Allopurinol-induced severe cutaneous adverse reactions, including SJS and TEN, particularly in Han Chinese, Thai, and Korean populations. HLA-B*1502 is associated with carbamazepine, and HLA-B*5701 is linked to abacavir hypersensitivity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old female with Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) involving 45% BSA is admitted to the hospital. Which of the following clinical settings is the most appropriate for the management of this patient?', '["A specialized Burn Unit or Intensive Care Unit (ICU)", "An outpatient clinic with daily nurse home visits", "A clean private room in a general medical ward", "An infectious disease isolation room", "A standard dermatology inpatient ward"]'::jsonb, 0, 'TEN is a life-threatening dermatologic emergency. Due to extensive skin loss, patients require meticulous sterile wound care, specialized non-adherent dressings, tight fluid resuscitation, and temperature regulation identical to severe burn victims. Management in a specialized Burn Unit or intensive care setting significantly reduces mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old woman began taking Carbamazepine 4 weeks ago. They now present with a high fever (39.0 C), a widespread red skin eruption, and severe facial and periorbital swelling. Physical exam reveals confluent morbilliform plaques on the trunk, palpable lymphadenopathy in the neck and axillae, and moderate tender hepatomegaly. Lab work shows a WBC of 16,500/mm3 with 18% eosinophils (absolute eosinophil count 2,970/mm3) and AST/ALT elevated at 4 times the upper limit of normal. What is the diagnosis?', '["Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Serum Sickness", "Exanthematous (Morbilliform) Drug Eruption", "Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS) syndrome", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'DRESS syndrome (also known as Drug-Induced Hypersensitivity Syndrome) is a severe, life-threatening drug reaction characterized by a long latency period (2-8 weeks), high fever, a diffuse erythematous eruption, facial/periorbital edema, lymphadenopathy, hematologic abnormalities (eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytosis), and visceral organ involvement—most commonly hepatitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is diagnosed with DRESS syndrome after starting Phenytoin 6 weeks ago. Aside from stopping the offending drug, what virus reactivation is frequently associated with the pathogenesis and flare-ups of this systemic drug hypersensitivity?', '["Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Hepatitis C Virus (HCV)", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)", "Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'DRESS syndrome is strongly associated with the sequential reactivation of latent herpesviruses, most notably Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6), as well as HHV-7, EBV, and CMV. This viral reactivation is thought to drive the prolonged, recurrent course of the disease even after the causative drug has been completely cleared.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old female is started on Cephalexin for a suspected soft tissue infection. Forty-eight hours later, they develop a high fever (39.2 C) and a burning rash that started in the groin and axillae. Physical exam reveals diffuse, bright erythema studded with hundreds of non-follicular, sterile, pinpoint pustules. Lab work shows a marked leukocytosis with neutrophilia (WBC 19,000/mm3). Pustule cultures are sterile. What is the diagnosis?', '["Generalized Pustular Psoriasis (Von Zumbusch)", "Subcorneal Pustular Dermatosis", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "DRESS syndrome", "Disseminated candidiasis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'AGEP is a rare, severe cutaneous reaction characterized by the rapid onset (typically <48 hours after drug exposure) of high fever and hundreds of small, sterile, non-follicular pustules arising on a background of edematous erythema, classically starting in flexural folds (groin/axillae). Beta-lactams and macrolides are the most common triggers. Leukocytosis with marked neutrophilia is characteristic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old man presents with a dark, round spot on their left forearm. They note that every time they take Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection, the exact same spot becomes red, swollen, and itchy, occasionally forming a small central blister. Once it heals, a persistent dusky brown patch remains. What is the mechanism of this ''fixing'' to the same anatomic site?', '["Localized somatic mutation in the keratinocytes of that area", "Localized accumulation of IgE molecules on dermal mast cells", "Deposition of circulating IgG immune complexes in the local capillary bed", "Persistence of resident memory CD8+ T-cells in the affected epidermis", "Slow lymphatic clearance of the drug from that specific site"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Fixed Drug Eruption (FDE) is characterized by recurrent erythematous plaques that appear in the exact same localized site (skin or mucosa) upon re-exposure to the offending drug. This site-specific memory is mediated by resident memory CD8+ T-cells that remain quiescent in the basal epidermis of the affected plaque and rapidly degranulate upon systemic re-exposure to the drug.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old boy presents with a single, highly circumscribed, dusky-red, itchy patch on their glans penis that developed 24 hours after taking a medication. Which of the following drugs is the most common trigger for a fixed drug eruption (FDE) affecting the genitalia?', '["Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (Sulfonamides)", "Aspirin", "Metformin", "Atorvastatin", "Amlodipine"]'::jsonb, 0, 'While FDE can be triggered by many drugs (including NSAIDs and tetracyclines), sulfonamides—most notably Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole—are the most common triggers for fixed drug eruptions located on the genitalia (glans penis or labia).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old man is started on Warfarin for a new deep vein thrombosis. Due to an oversight, no Heparin bridge is administered. On day 3 of therapy, the patient develops severe pain and dark purple, well-demarcated patches on their breasts and thighs. Within 24 hours, these lesions progress to necrotic black eschars. What is the primary molecular cause of this reaction?', '["An IgE-mediated anaphylactoid reaction causing localized vasculitis", "A drop in antithrombin III levels leading to arterial spasm", "Localized vasoconstriction from paradoxical alpha-adrenergic stimulation", "A rapid drop in Protein C levels (due to its short half-life), causing a transient hypercoagulable state and microthrombosis", "Direct toxic necrosis of adipocytes caused by accumulation of the warfarin molecule"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Warfarin-induced skin necrosis occurs early in therapy (typically days 3-5) when warfarin is initiated without a heparin bridge, especially in patients with hereditary Protein C or S deficiency. Protein C has a very short half-life (approx 8 hours) and falls rapidly, while procoagulant factors II, IX, and X fall slowly. This creates a transient, severe hypercoagulable state leading to microthrombosis in the capillaries supplying fat-rich areas (breasts, thighs, buttocks).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient on subcutaneous Unfractionated Heparin therapy for a pulmonary embolism develops painful, red-to-purple patches at their abdominal injection sites 7 days into therapy, which quickly progress to localized skin necrosis. Lab work reveals a platelet count that has dropped from 280,000/mm3 to 90,000/mm3. What is the target of the autoantibodies driving this clinical presentation?', '["Desmoglein 1 and Desmoglein 3 in the epidermis", "The G-protein coupled thrombin receptor", "Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes", "Type XVII Collagen (BP180) in the basement membrane", "Protein C and Protein S"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT) presenting with localized heparin-induced skin necrosis at the injection sites. HIT is mediated by IgG autoantibodies targeting Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes, which bind and activate platelets, causing both thrombocytopenia (clearance of platelets) and a severe, life-threatening prothrombotic state.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient develops heparin-induced skin necrosis and thrombocytopenia (HIT). Which of the following is the most critical and immediate next step in management?', '["Transfuse 2 units of platelets and continue subcutaneous heparin", "Stop heparin, administer Vitamin K, and start oral warfarin immediately", "Discontinue all heparin products immediately and initiate a non-heparin anticoagulant (e.g., Argatroban)", "Increase the heparin dose to overcome the antibody-mediated clearance", "Apply high-potency topical steroids to the necrotic injection sites"]'::jsonb, 2, 'When HIT is suspected or confirmed (due to a platelet drop >50% and/or skin necrosis), the immediate action must be to stop all heparin products (including low-molecular-weight heparin and heparin flushes) and start an alternative, non-heparin rapid anticoagulant (e.g., a direct thrombin inhibitor like Argatroban or Bivalirudin) to prevent catastrophic thromboembolism. Warfarin is contraindicated in active HIT as it can precipitate gangrene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old male being treated with Hydralazine for severe hypertension presents with joint pain, fever, and a mild malar-like rash on their face. Lab results show a positive ANA (1:640) and highly elevated Anti-Histone antibodies. Anti-dsDNA and Anti-Smith antibodies are negative. Urinalysis and serum creatinine are normal. What is the clinical significance of anti-histone antibodies in this patient?', '["They indicate severe, silent lupus nephritis requiring renal biopsy", "They are caused by a cross-reaction with the hydralazine molecule itself", "They represent a high risk of transition to Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis", "They are highly sensitive (approx 95%) for drug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE)", "They are highly specific for idiopathic systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Anti-histone antibodies are highly sensitive (present in 90-95% of cases) for Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE). Common triggers include Hydralazine, Procainamide, and Isoniazid. Unlike idiopathic SLE, DILE rarely involves the kidneys or central nervous system, and anti-dsDNA or anti-Smith antibodies are usually absent.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old woman being treated with D-Penicillamine for Wilson''s disease develops flaccid blisters on their chest and upper back, along with painful sores in their mouth. Physical exam shows a positive Nikolsky sign. A skin biopsy reveals intraepidermal acantholysis. Direct Immunofluorescence (DIF) demonstrates intercellular IgG deposition in a ''fishnet'' pattern throughout the epidermis. What is the target of the autoantibodies in this drug-induced condition?', '["Integrin alpha-6 beta-4 in the hemidesmosomes", "Type VII Collagen in the anchoring fibrils", "Laminin-332 in the lamina lucida", "BP180 (Type XVII Collagen) and BP230", "Desmogleins (Dsg1 and Dsg3)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Drug-Induced Pemphigus, characteristically triggered by thiol (sulfhydryl) group-containing drugs like D-Penicillamine, Captopril, and Gold salts. Thiol groups can biochemically disrupt desmosomes or act as haptens. The autoantibodies target desmoglein 1 and/or 3, leading to flaccid blisters, mucosal lesions, acantholysis, and a ''fishnet'' DIF pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An 82-year-old man on Linagliptin (a DPP-4 inhibitor) for type 2 diabetes presents with an intensely pruritic rash and several large, firm, tense blisters on their abdomen and lower extremities. Nikolsky sign is negative. A biopsy shows a subepidermal split with numerous eosinophils. DIF demonstrates a linear band of IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug-induced Bullous Pemphigoid", "Linear IgA Bullous Dermatosis", "Drug-induced Pemphigus Vulgaris", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita", "Pseudoporphyria"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has Drug-induced Bullous Pemphigoid (BP), characteristically presenting with tense subepidermal blisters and severe pruritus. In recent years, Dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitors (gliptins) have been recognized as a major drug trigger for BP, particularly in elderly patients. Loop diuretics (furosemide) are another classic trigger.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 26-year-old girl being treated with a Beta-blocker for hypertension presents with an extremely itchy rash on their forearms and shins. Physical examination reveals symmetric, flat-topped, polygonal, purple papules. Fine white lines (Wickham striae) are visible on the surface under magnification. How does this drug-induced lichenoid eruption typically differ from classic, idiopathic Lichen Planus?', '["It is highly infectious and spreads to close contacts", "It selectively targets the face and spares the extremities", "It is completely non-pruritic and does not show interface dermatitis on biopsy", "It presents with large, flaccid blisters and a positive Nikolsky sign", "It is often more generalized, symmetric, and frequently lacks mucosal involvement or classic Wickham striae"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Lichenoid drug eruptions (triggered by beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, thiazides, and gold) clinically mimic Lichen Planus. However, they typically differ by being more generalized, symmetric, and having a eczematous or psoriasiform appearance, while frequently sparing the oral mucosa and showing a slower resolution after drug withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old female presents with an explosive onset of acne-like bumps on their chest and upper back. They deny having any acne as a teenager. Physical exam reveals dozens of monomorphic (all at the same stage of development) erythematous inflammatory papules and pustules. No blackheads or whiteheads (comedones) are present. The patient was recently treated with a high-dose oral prednisone taper. What is the diagnosis?', '["Papulopustular rosacea", "Acne vulgaris (papulopustular subtype)", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Hot tub folliculitis", "Pityrosporum folliculitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Steroid acne is an acneiform drug eruption caused by systemic or high-potency topical corticosteroids. Clinically, it is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules on the trunk and shoulders. Unlike acne vulgaris, steroid acne lacks comedones (blackheads/whiteheads) and the lesions are all at the identical stage of development.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient receiving a rapid intravenous infusion of Vancomycin suddenly develops severe flushing, pruritus, and a bright red rash over their face, neck, and upper torso. Their heart rate is 115/min and blood pressure is 98/60 mmHg. There is no wheezing, lip swelling, or hives. What is the primary mechanism of this reaction?', '["Direct toxic effect of the vancomycin molecule on vascular smooth muscle", "T-cell mediated cytotoxic destruction of endothelial cells", "Type III hypersensitivity with deposition of IgA immune complexes in dermal vessels", "IgE-mediated Type I hypersensitivity reaction causing systemic anaphylaxis", "Direct, non-immunologic histamine release from mast cells triggered by the rapid drug infusion"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Red Man Syndrome, a pseudo-allergic (anaphylactoid) reaction caused by a rapid infusion of intravenous Vancomycin. The drug directly degranulates mast cells and basophils in a non-immunologic fashion, releasing histamine. It is not a true IgE-mediated allergy. It can be prevented by slowing the infusion rate (to over 60 minutes) and pre-treating with antihistamines.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old child presents with a widespread, hive-like rash, severe joint pain and swelling of the ankles, and a low-grade fever that began 10 days after starting Cefaclor for an ear infection. Lab work shows a normal platelet count and no proteinuria. What is the diagnosis?', '["Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura (IgA Vasculitis)", "True Serum Sickness", "Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR)", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome", "Acute Rheumatic Fever"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR) is a benign, transient reaction that classically occurs in children 1-3 weeks after starting certain drugs, most famously Cefaclor, as well as penicillins and sulfonamides. It mimics true serum sickness with fever, arthralgias, and urticarial plaques, but crucially LACKS circulating immune complexes, complement depletion, vasculitis, and renal damage (proteinuria).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old boy being treated with high-dose Capecitabine for metastatic colorectal cancer presents with painful redness, swelling, and tingling on their palms and soles. Physical examination reveals symmetric, bright erythema and edema of the palmar and plantar surfaces, with early desquamation. What is the clinical term for this condition?', '["Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (Hand-Foot Syndrome)", "Contact Dermatitis", "Fixed Drug Eruption", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Hand-Foot-and-Mouth Disease"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (also known as Hand-Foot Syndrome) is a common cutaneous side effect of several chemotherapy agents, most classically Capecitabine, 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU), and Cytarabine. It is caused by drug leaking from small capillaries into the thick skin of the palms and soles, leading to direct toxicity, pain, symmetric erythema, swelling, and peeling.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old girl being treated with Cetuximab (an EGFR inhibitor) for lung cancer develops an itchy, acne-like rash consisting of papules and pustules on their face, neck, and upper chest. Comedones are completely absent. What is the clinical significance of this rash?', '["It indicates a life-threatening drug allergy and the Cetuximab must be stopped permanently", "It is a positive prognostic indicator (the development and severity of the rash correlate with a favorable tumor response)", "It represents a deep follicular bacterial infection requiring intravenous antibiotics", "It is caused by secondary adrenal insufficiency from the chemotherapy", "It is a sign of tumor progression and therapeutic failure"]'::jsonb, 1, 'EGFR inhibitor-induced acneiform (papulopustular) eruption is a highly common and expected class-effect of drugs like Cetuximab, Erlotinib, and Panitumumab. Counter-intuitively, the development and severity of this rash act as a positive surrogate marker for drug efficacy, correlating with improved tumor response and patient survival. It is managed with topical/oral antibiotics (doxycycline) and steroids without stopping the chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old female who has been taking Minocycline for severe acne for 3 years presents with a new, muddy, blue-gray discoloration on their shins and the mucosal surface of their lower gums. A skin biopsy reveals iron-containing and melanin-containing pigment deposits within the dermis. What is the subtype and characteristic appearance of this minocycline-induced hyperpigmentation?', '["Type I Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, limited strictly to sites of prior inflammation and scarring", "Type II Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, affecting normal skin of the shins and forearms, as well as gums", "Type III Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, presenting as a diffuse muddy-brown tan in sun-exposed areas", "Slate-gray facial pigmentation induced by drug-induced lipofuscin", "Argiria, caused by silver deposition in the eccrine glands"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Minocycline can induce three distinct types of skin hyperpigmentation: Type I occurs strictly in scars and areas of prior inflammation (blue-black pigment); Type II occurs on normal skin, classically the shins, forearms, and gums (slate-blue/gray pigment containing iron and melanin); and Type III is a diffuse, muddy-brown hyperpigmentation in sun-exposed areas (pure melanin).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old girl with a history of refractory cardiac arrhythmias has been taking Amiodarone for 5 years. They present to the clinic complaining of a blue-gray discoloration of their face, nose, and ears. Biopsy of the affected skin reveals lipofuscin-like granules within dermal macrophages. What is the key environmental trigger required for the development of this specific pigmentation?', '["Frequent hot water baths", "Exposure to cold weather (Raynaud-like)", "Chronic mechanical friction", "High dietary intake of vitamin C", "Ultraviolet (UV) light exposure (sunlight)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Amiodarone-induced hyperpigmentation is a classic side effect occurring in patients on long-term, high-dose amiodarone therapy. It presents as a slate-gray to blue-violet discoloration strictly in sun-exposed areas (face, nose, ears). Ultraviolet (UV) light is required to trigger the deposition of a drug-lipid complex (lipofuscin) inside dermal macrophages.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old girl with systemic lupus erythematosus has been taking Hydroxychloroquine for 6 years. During a routine skin check, the dermatologist notes well-demarcated, slate-gray patches on the patient''s bilateral shins and a bluish-gray discoloration of the hard palate. What is the prognosis and clinical course of this pigmentation?', '["It indicates an immediate risk of acute hepatic failure", "It resolves spontaneously within 2 weeks of stopping the drug", "It is completely permanent and will continue to spread even if the drug is discontinued", "It is highly infectious and can be transmitted to others", "It is slowly reversible over months to years after stopping the hydroxychloroquine, though some areas may persist"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Skin and mucosal hyperpigmentation occurs in up to 25% of patients on long-term antimalarials (Chloroquine/Hydroxychloroquine). Classic sites are the shins (pretibial area), the hard palate, and the nail beds. The pigmentation is slowly reversible over many months or years after drug cessation, though some localized areas may remain permanently.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 32-year-old woman undergoing chemotherapy for Hodgkin''s lymphoma develops intensely pruritic, linear, criss-crossing red streaks on her back and chest that resemble ''whip marks''. She admits to scratching her skin due to dryness. Which of the following chemotherapy agents is the classic cause of this flagellate erythema?', '["Paclitaxel", "Doxorubicin", "5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)", "Bleomycin", "Cyclophosphamide"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Flagellate erythema is a virtually pathognomonic cutaneous toxicity associated with Bleomycin therapy. It presents as highly characteristic, linear, criss-crossing erythematous streaks that look like ''whip marks'' on the back and trunk. It represents a Koebner-like response where minor scratching or friction concentrates the drug in the local skin vessels.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old man presents with progressive, painless, firm, fibrous swelling of their gums that has begun to cover the crowns of their teeth. They have a history of epilepsy. Which of the following triads of medications represents the classic causes of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia?', '["Phenytoin, Cyclosporine, and Nifedipine", "Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen", "Penicillin, Doxycycline, and Cephalexin", "Prednisone, Isotretinoin, and Spironolactone", "Metformin, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Drug-induced gingival hyperplasia is a classic side effect characterized by fibrous overgrowth of the gingival papillae. The three classic, high-yield culprits are Phenytoin (anti-epileptic), Cyclosporine (immunosuppressant), and Calcium Channel Blockers (especially Nifedipine, but also Amlodipine/Diltiazem).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old male taking Naproxen for osteoarthritic pain presents with skin fragility and multiple small, tense vesicles and erosions on the dorsum of their hands. Biopsy reveals a subepidermal blister with minimal inflammatory cell infiltrate. Laboratory workup demonstrates normal levels of urinary and fecal porphyrins, ruling out Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT). What is the diagnosis?', '["Bullous Pemphigoid", "Pseudoporphyria", "Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT)", "Allergic Contact Dermatitis", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pseudoporphyria is a drug-induced condition that clinically and histologically mimics Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT), presenting with skin fragility, blistering, and scarring on sun-exposed hands. Crucially, porphyrin levels in the blood, urine, and stool are completely normal in pseudoporphyria. NSAIDs (most famously Naproxen) are the leading cause.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 25-year-old female presents with multiple extremely painful, warm, red, deep-seated nodules on her bilateral shins that have been present for 2 weeks. She reports starting oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) 2 months ago. A biopsy reveals septal panniculitis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Weber-Christian disease", "Sweet''s syndrome", "Erythema Nodosum (EN)", "Erythema Induratum (Bazin''s disease)", "Polyarteritis Nodosa (PAN)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Erythema Nodosum (EN) is a classic form of panniculitis (inflammation of subcutaneous fat) that presents as tender, warm, erythematous nodules on the pretibial shins. It represents a hypersensitivity reaction to various triggers, including infections (strep, TB) and medications—most commonly oral contraceptives (OCPs) and sulfonamides. Histology classically shows septal panniculitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old child presents with high fever, severe mucosal crusting and ulceration of the lips and mouth, and conjunctival redness with purulent discharge. Physical exam reveals less than 1% BSA of skin detachment with only a few targetoid macules on the limbs. The child was treated for ''walking pneumonia'' 1 week ago. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS)", "Primary Herpes Simplex Gingivostomatitis", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS) is triggered by Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection, most commonly in children and young adults. It is characterized by severe mucositis involving $\ge 2$ mucosal sites (typically oral and ocular) with minimal or absent cutaneous lesions (<1% BSA skin involvement). It mimics SJS but has a distinct pathogenesis and carries a significantly better prognosis with lower mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old patient presents with painful, dark purple, necrotic, star-shaped (retiform) patches on their bilateral earlobes, cheeks, and lower extremities. Lab work reveals a severe neutropenia (WBC 1,200/mm3 with an absolute neutrophil count of 350/mm3). A urine drug screen is positive for Cocaine. What is the causative agent responsible for this patient''s necrotic ear skin and bone marrow suppression?', '["Direct vasoconstrictive toxicity of the cocaine molecule itself", "Amiodarone-induced photo-vasculitis", "Levamisole (a veterinary dewormer used as a cocaine cutting agent)", "An IgE-mediated drug allergy to benzoylecgonine", "A contamination of cocaine with Streptococcus pyogenes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Levamisole-induced retiform purpura. Levamisole is a veterinary anthelmintic commonly used as an adulterant (cutting agent) in illicit cocaine. It triggers a unique, severe autoimmune vasculitis characterized by painful, retiform (star-shaped) purpura with central necrosis, characteristically involving the ears/earlobes and face. It is also a potent bone marrow suppressant, leading to severe neutropenia/agranulocytosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old girl being treated with Hydralazine for 8 years presents with fever, joint pain, weight loss, and palpable purple spots (purpura) on their lower legs. Lab results show a positive ANA, dual positivity for p-ANCA (MPO) and c-ANCA (PR3) antibodies, and urinalysis reveals proteinuria and hematuria with red blood cell casts. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura", "Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE)", "Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis is a severe necrotizing vasculitis classically triggered by long-term use of Hydralazine, Propylthiouracil (PTU), or Methimazole. Unlike simple drug-induced lupus (DILE), drug-induced ANCA vasculitis features high titers of p-ANCA (MPO) and PR3, palpable purpura, and significant internal organ involvement—most notably rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (renal failure).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old man on Captopril (an ACE inhibitor) develops a widespread scaly rash on their trunk. They report a large, oval, scaly ''herald patch'' appeared on their chest first, followed by smaller oval plaques along the skin tension lines on their back in a ''Christmas tree'' distribution. How does this drug-induced Pityriasis Rosea-like eruption typically differ from idiopathic Pityriasis Rosea?', '["It often lacks a distinct initial herald patch and takes much longer to resolve after stopping the drug", "It strictly affects the face and spares the trunk", "It presents with deep, tense bullae and a positive Nikolsky sign", "It is accompanied by severe kidney damage and proteinuria", "It is highly contagious and spreads to children via respiratory droplets"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pityriasis Rosea-like drug eruptions (triggered by ACE inhibitors, gold, beta-blockers, and metronidazole) closely mimic classic PR. However, the drug-induced form typically lacks the single large ''herald patch'' that precedes the main eruption, shows more confluent and inflammatory lesions, and is much more persistent, resolving slowly only after the offending drug is discontinued.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a history of stable, mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with bipolar disorder and started on Lithium. Two weeks later, they present with an explosive, severe flare of psoriasis, with thick, silvery plaques now covering 40% of their BSA. Which of the following lists represents the ''big four'' systemic drugs most famous for flaring or worsening psoriasis?', '["Lithium, Beta-blockers, Antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), and NSAIDs", "Statins, Fibrates, Metformin, and Lisinopril", "Isotretinoin, Spironolactone, Cyclosporine, and Prednisone", "Amlodipine, Warfarin, Heparin, and Aspirin", "Penicillins, Cephalosporins, Macrolides, and Sulfonamides"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Systemic drugs are common triggers for the onset or severe exacerbation of psoriasis. The classic, high-yield ''big four'' culprits are Lithium (bipolar therapy), Beta-blockers (cardiovascular therapy), Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine/chloroquine), and NSAIDs. Systemic steroid withdrawal can also precipitate a life-threatening rebound flare of generalized pustular psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Immediately after receiving an intramuscular (IM) injection of Penicillin G benzathine in the upper outer quadrant of the buttock, a 72-year-old boy develops excruciating, burning pain and livedo-like, blue-gray discoloration around the injection site. Within 48 hours, this area develops deep, necrotic sloughing of the skin and underlying gluteal muscle. What is this clinical entity?', '["Arthus reaction from vaccine immune complex deposition", "Nicolau Syndrome (Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa)", "An IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction", "Necrotizing fasciitis from bacterial contamination", "Warfarin-induced skin necrosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nicolau Syndrome (also known as Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa) is a rare, devastating non-allergic vascular complication of intramuscular injections. It occurs due to accidental intra-arterial injection, peri-arterial injection, or drug-induced vasospasm/thrombosis. It presents with immediate severe pain, retiform cyanosis, and subsequent deep tissue necrosis of the skin, fat, and muscle.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing chemotherapy for leukemia is treated with G-CSF (Granulocyte Colony-Stimulating Factor) to manage neutropenia. Three days later, they develop a high fever (39.5 C) and several painful, bright red, ''succulent'' or ''juicy'' plaques on their neck and upper back. Biopsy reveals a massive, dense neutrophilic infiltrate in the upper dermis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["AGEP", "DRESS syndrome", "Sweet''s Syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis)", "Pyoderma Gangrenosum", "Erysipelas"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sweet''s syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis) is characterized by high fever, leukocytosis, and tender, erythematous plaques that are clinically described as ''juicy'' or ''succulent'' due to profound upper dermal edema. Histology shows a dense dermal neutrophilic infiltrate without vasculitis. G-CSF is a classic drug trigger because it directly stimulates neutrophil production.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old female being treated with Paclitaxel for breast cancer develops bilateral, woody thickening and hardening of the skin of their lower legs and forearms. Physical exam shows marked induration, but there is an absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon or nail fold capillary changes, and systemic organ involvement is absent. ANA testing is negative. What is the diagnosis?', '["Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma)", "Stasis dermatitis", "Taxane-induced scleroderma-like changes", "Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis", "Eosinophilic Fasciitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Taxanes (Paclitaxel, Docetaxel) can induce scleroderma-like skin changes (induration, woody thickening) typically localized to the extremities. The key clinical clues to distinguish this drug-induced form from idiopathic Systemic Sclerosis are the absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon, normal nail fold capillaries, negative autoantibodies (ANA/Scl-70), and lack of visceral sclerosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old man taking Vemurafenib (a BRAF inhibitor) for metastatic melanoma develops several fast-growing, dome-shaped nodules with central keratinous craters on their arms within 3 weeks of starting the drug. Biopsy of one lesion shows a well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma/keratoacanthoma (SCC/KA). What is the mechanism driving these secondary skin malignancies?', '["A metastatic spread of the primary melanoma to the skin", "Upregulation of the Hedgehog pathway in follicular basal cells", "Systemic immunosuppression leading to explosive reactivation of HPV-16", "Direct mutagenic damage to epidermal stem cell DNA by the drug molecule", "Paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do NOT have the BRAF mutation"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Vemurafenib and other BRAF inhibitors cause paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal cells that do not harbor the BRAF V600E mutation. The drug binds to wild-type BRAF, causing it to homodimerize and hyper-activate downstream MEK/ERK signaling, driving rapid keratinocyte proliferation and leading to eruptive Keratoacanthomas and low-grade Squamous Cell Carcinomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old boy on Sorafenib (a multi-kinase inhibitor) for renal cell carcinoma develops thick, painful, yellow calluses surrounded by a bright red halo on the heels and lateral aspects of their feet. How does this Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) clinically differ from Capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS)?', '["HFSR presents as localized hyperkeratosis and thick calluses on pressure points, whereas classic HFS is characterized by diffuse, symmetric erythema and peeling", "HFSR strictly affects the fingernails and spares the skin", "HFSR is completely painless and resolves within 24 hours of starting the drug", "Classic HFS is caused by direct exposure to sunlight", "There is no clinical difference between the two reactions"]'::jsonb, 0, 'While both affect the hands/feet, Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) is clinically distinct from capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS). HFSR is characterized by localized, hyperkeratotic, thick yellow plaques and blisters on high-friction pressure points (heels/lateral sole), while classic HFS (capecitabine) presents as diffuse, confluent erythema, edema, and desquamation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old man with chronic renal insufficiency receives intravenous iodinated contrast for a CT scan. Two days later, they develop painful, rapidly expanding, pustular, vegetating plaques on their face and lower extremities. Pustule culture is negative. Biopsy shows massive epidermal hyperplasia (pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia) and dense neutrophilic microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Sweet''s syndrome", "Pyoderma Gangrenosum", "Iododerma (Halogenoderma)", "Blastomycosis", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Halogenodermas (Iododerma and Bromoderma) are rare cutaneous toxic accumulations of halogens, most commonly occurring in patients with impaired renal clearance. Clinically, they present as highly inflammatory, pustular, vegetating plaques that can closely mimic Pyoderma Gangrenosum, deep fungal infections (like Blastomycosis), or Squamous Cell Carcinoma. Histology shows marked pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department with a painful rash, high fever, and severe eye soreness that began 2 weeks after starting Allopurinol. Physical exam reveals confluent, atypical targetoid macules with flaccid blisters and sloughing covering approximately 8% of the total body surface area (BSA). Two mucosal sites are severely involved. Skin biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis with minimal dermal inflammation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Erythema Multiforme Major"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS), defined by fever, mucosal involvement of $\ge 2$ sites, targetoid macules, and epidermal detachment affecting <10% of the body surface area (BSA). Biopsy characteristically shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis (apoptosis of keratinocytes) driven by cytotoxic T-cells and granulysin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old man on Phenytoin for seizures presents with fever and widespread skin tenderness. Within 24 hours, the skin begins to peel off in large sheets. Physical examination shows dusky erythema progressing to confluent blisters. Gentle lateral pressure on the erythematous skin causes the epidermis to slide off the dermis (positive Nikolsky sign). The epidermal detachment is estimated at 35% of the total body surface area. What is the primary cause of mortality in this condition?', '["Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "Acute kidney injury from severe myoglobinuria", "Infection, sepsis, and multi-organ failure", "Hepatocellular failure and acute encephalopathy", "High-output cardiac failure and hypothermia"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN), characterized by epidermal detachment of >30% BSA and a positive Nikolsky sign. The loss of the skin barrier leads to massive fluid shifts, hypothermia, and high-output heart failure, but the leading cause of death in these patients is infection (sepsis) and subsequent multi-organ failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old woman presents with a painful, blistering rash and fever. They recently started Sulfasalazine for inflammatory bowel disease. Physical examination reveals atypical targetoid plaques on the trunk and extremities, with flaccid bullae that slide off under lateral pressure. The surface area of epidermal detachment is measured at exactly 18% of the total BSA. What is the clinical category of this patient''s drug reaction?', '["Erythema Multiforme Major", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "DRESS syndrome"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The SJS/TEN spectrum is categorized by the percentage of body surface area showing epidermal detachment: SJS involves <10% BSA detachment; SJS/TEN Overlap involves 10% to 30% BSA detachment; and TEN involves >30% BSA detachment. At 18% detachment, this patient falls into the SJS/TEN Overlap category.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old male of East Asian descent is about to start Carbamazepine for neuropathic pain. The clinician orders genetic screening for the HLA-B*1502 allele before prescribing. What is the clinical significance of this allele?', '["It correlates with an increased risk of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia", "It is a marker for drug-induced phototoxicity and exaggerated sunburns", "It is associated with rapid metabolic clearance of Carbamazepine, making it ineffective", "It indicates an increased risk of severe, irreversible agranulocytosis", "It carries an extremely high risk of Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in Asian populations"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The HLA-B*1502 allele is strongly associated with an increased risk of developing Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in individuals of East Asian and South-East Asian descent. Screening is mandatory or highly recommended prior to starting Carbamazepine in these high-risk populations.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old man with Han Chinese ancestry is diagnosed with gout and is about to start Allopurinol therapy. Which HLA allele is strongly associated with Allopurinol-induced Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) in this population?', '["HLA-DRB1*0401", "HLA-B*5801", "HLA-B*1502", "HLA-B*5701", "HLA-B*2705"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The HLA-B*5801 allele is a potent genetic risk factor for Allopurinol-induced severe cutaneous adverse reactions, including SJS and TEN, particularly in Han Chinese, Thai, and Korean populations. HLA-B*1502 is associated with carbamazepine, and HLA-B*5701 is linked to abacavir hypersensitivity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old female with Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) involving 45% BSA is admitted to the hospital. Which of the following clinical settings is the most appropriate for the management of this patient?', '["A standard dermatology inpatient ward", "An outpatient clinic with daily nurse home visits", "A specialized Burn Unit or Intensive Care Unit (ICU)", "An infectious disease isolation room", "A clean private room in a general medical ward"]'::jsonb, 2, 'TEN is a life-threatening dermatologic emergency. Due to extensive skin loss, patients require meticulous sterile wound care, specialized non-adherent dressings, tight fluid resuscitation, and temperature regulation identical to severe burn victims. Management in a specialized Burn Unit or intensive care setting significantly reduces mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old girl began taking Carbamazepine 4 weeks ago. They now present with a high fever (39.0 C), a widespread red skin eruption, and severe facial and periorbital swelling. Physical exam reveals confluent morbilliform plaques on the trunk, palpable lymphadenopathy in the neck and axillae, and moderate tender hepatomegaly. Lab work shows a WBC of 16,500/mm3 with 18% eosinophils (absolute eosinophil count 2,970/mm3) and AST/ALT elevated at 4 times the upper limit of normal. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS) syndrome", "Serum Sickness", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "Exanthematous (Morbilliform) Drug Eruption", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'DRESS syndrome (also known as Drug-Induced Hypersensitivity Syndrome) is a severe, life-threatening drug reaction characterized by a long latency period (2-8 weeks), high fever, a diffuse erythematous eruption, facial/periorbital edema, lymphadenopathy, hematologic abnormalities (eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytosis), and visceral organ involvement—most commonly hepatitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is diagnosed with DRESS syndrome after starting Phenytoin 6 weeks ago. Aside from stopping the offending drug, what virus reactivation is frequently associated with the pathogenesis and flare-ups of this systemic drug hypersensitivity?', '["Hepatitis C Virus (HCV)", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'DRESS syndrome is strongly associated with the sequential reactivation of latent herpesviruses, most notably Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6), as well as HHV-7, EBV, and CMV. This viral reactivation is thought to drive the prolonged, recurrent course of the disease even after the causative drug has been completely cleared.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old woman is started on Cephalexin for a suspected soft tissue infection. Forty-eight hours later, they develop a high fever (39.2 C) and a burning rash that started in the groin and axillae. Physical exam reveals diffuse, bright erythema studded with hundreds of non-follicular, sterile, pinpoint pustules. Lab work shows a marked leukocytosis with neutrophilia (WBC 19,000/mm3). Pustule cultures are sterile. What is the diagnosis?', '["Subcorneal Pustular Dermatosis", "Disseminated candidiasis", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "Generalized Pustular Psoriasis (Von Zumbusch)", "DRESS syndrome"]'::jsonb, 2, 'AGEP is a rare, severe cutaneous reaction characterized by the rapid onset (typically <48 hours after drug exposure) of high fever and hundreds of small, sterile, non-follicular pustules arising on a background of edematous erythema, classically starting in flexural folds (groin/axillae). Beta-lactams and macrolides are the most common triggers. Leukocytosis with marked neutrophilia is characteristic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old man presents with a dark, round spot on their left forearm. They note that every time they take Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection, the exact same spot becomes red, swollen, and itchy, occasionally forming a small central blister. Once it heals, a persistent dusky brown patch remains. What is the mechanism of this ''fixing'' to the same anatomic site?', '["Localized accumulation of IgE molecules on dermal mast cells", "Slow lymphatic clearance of the drug from that specific site", "Persistence of resident memory CD8+ T-cells in the affected epidermis", "Deposition of circulating IgG immune complexes in the local capillary bed", "Localized somatic mutation in the keratinocytes of that area"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fixed Drug Eruption (FDE) is characterized by recurrent erythematous plaques that appear in the exact same localized site (skin or mucosa) upon re-exposure to the offending drug. This site-specific memory is mediated by resident memory CD8+ T-cells that remain quiescent in the basal epidermis of the affected plaque and rapidly degranulate upon systemic re-exposure to the drug.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female presents with a single, highly circumscribed, dusky-red, itchy patch on their glans penis that developed 24 hours after taking a medication. Which of the following drugs is the most common trigger for a fixed drug eruption (FDE) affecting the genitalia?', '["Metformin", "Aspirin", "Amlodipine", "Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (Sulfonamides)", "Atorvastatin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'While FDE can be triggered by many drugs (including NSAIDs and tetracyclines), sulfonamides—most notably Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole—are the most common triggers for fixed drug eruptions located on the genitalia (glans penis or labia).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old boy is started on Warfarin for a new deep vein thrombosis. Due to an oversight, no Heparin bridge is administered. On day 3 of therapy, the patient develops severe pain and dark purple, well-demarcated patches on their breasts and thighs. Within 24 hours, these lesions progress to necrotic black eschars. What is the primary molecular cause of this reaction?', '["Direct toxic necrosis of adipocytes caused by accumulation of the warfarin molecule", "A drop in antithrombin III levels leading to arterial spasm", "A rapid drop in Protein C levels (due to its short half-life), causing a transient hypercoagulable state and microthrombosis", "An IgE-mediated anaphylactoid reaction causing localized vasculitis", "Localized vasoconstriction from paradoxical alpha-adrenergic stimulation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Warfarin-induced skin necrosis occurs early in therapy (typically days 3-5) when warfarin is initiated without a heparin bridge, especially in patients with hereditary Protein C or S deficiency. Protein C has a very short half-life (approx 8 hours) and falls rapidly, while procoagulant factors II, IX, and X fall slowly. This creates a transient, severe hypercoagulable state leading to microthrombosis in the capillaries supplying fat-rich areas (breasts, thighs, buttocks).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient on subcutaneous Unfractionated Heparin therapy for a pulmonary embolism develops painful, red-to-purple patches at their abdominal injection sites 7 days into therapy, which quickly progress to localized skin necrosis. Lab work reveals a platelet count that has dropped from 280,000/mm3 to 90,000/mm3. What is the target of the autoantibodies driving this clinical presentation?', '["Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes", "Type XVII Collagen (BP180) in the basement membrane", "Desmoglein 1 and Desmoglein 3 in the epidermis", "Protein C and Protein S", "The G-protein coupled thrombin receptor"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT) presenting with localized heparin-induced skin necrosis at the injection sites. HIT is mediated by IgG autoantibodies targeting Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes, which bind and activate platelets, causing both thrombocytopenia (clearance of platelets) and a severe, life-threatening prothrombotic state.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient develops heparin-induced skin necrosis and thrombocytopenia (HIT). Which of the following is the most critical and immediate next step in management?', '["Apply high-potency topical steroids to the necrotic injection sites", "Increase the heparin dose to overcome the antibody-mediated clearance", "Discontinue all heparin products immediately and initiate a non-heparin anticoagulant (e.g., Argatroban)", "Stop heparin, administer Vitamin K, and start oral warfarin immediately", "Transfuse 2 units of platelets and continue subcutaneous heparin"]'::jsonb, 2, 'When HIT is suspected or confirmed (due to a platelet drop >50% and/or skin necrosis), the immediate action must be to stop all heparin products (including low-molecular-weight heparin and heparin flushes) and start an alternative, non-heparin rapid anticoagulant (e.g., a direct thrombin inhibitor like Argatroban or Bivalirudin) to prevent catastrophic thromboembolism. Warfarin is contraindicated in active HIT as it can precipitate gangrene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old woman being treated with Hydralazine for severe hypertension presents with joint pain, fever, and a mild malar-like rash on their face. Lab results show a positive ANA (1:640) and highly elevated Anti-Histone antibodies. Anti-dsDNA and Anti-Smith antibodies are negative. Urinalysis and serum creatinine are normal. What is the clinical significance of anti-histone antibodies in this patient?', '["They are caused by a cross-reaction with the hydralazine molecule itself", "They are highly specific for idiopathic systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)", "They represent a high risk of transition to Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis", "They indicate severe, silent lupus nephritis requiring renal biopsy", "They are highly sensitive (approx 95%) for drug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Anti-histone antibodies are highly sensitive (present in 90-95% of cases) for Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE). Common triggers include Hydralazine, Procainamide, and Isoniazid. Unlike idiopathic SLE, DILE rarely involves the kidneys or central nervous system, and anti-dsDNA or anti-Smith antibodies are usually absent.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old woman being treated with D-Penicillamine for Wilson''s disease develops flaccid blisters on their chest and upper back, along with painful sores in their mouth. Physical exam shows a positive Nikolsky sign. A skin biopsy reveals intraepidermal acantholysis. Direct Immunofluorescence (DIF) demonstrates intercellular IgG deposition in a ''fishnet'' pattern throughout the epidermis. What is the target of the autoantibodies in this drug-induced condition?', '["Laminin-332 in the lamina lucida", "Integrin alpha-6 beta-4 in the hemidesmosomes", "BP180 (Type XVII Collagen) and BP230", "Type VII Collagen in the anchoring fibrils", "Desmogleins (Dsg1 and Dsg3)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Drug-Induced Pemphigus, characteristically triggered by thiol (sulfhydryl) group-containing drugs like D-Penicillamine, Captopril, and Gold salts. Thiol groups can biochemically disrupt desmosomes or act as haptens. The autoantibodies target desmoglein 1 and/or 3, leading to flaccid blisters, mucosal lesions, acantholysis, and a ''fishnet'' DIF pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An 82-year-old man on Linagliptin (a DPP-4 inhibitor) for type 2 diabetes presents with an intensely pruritic rash and several large, firm, tense blisters on their abdomen and lower extremities. Nikolsky sign is negative. A biopsy shows a subepidermal split with numerous eosinophils. DIF demonstrates a linear band of IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug-induced Bullous Pemphigoid", "Linear IgA Bullous Dermatosis", "Pseudoporphyria", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita", "Drug-induced Pemphigus Vulgaris"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has Drug-induced Bullous Pemphigoid (BP), characteristically presenting with tense subepidermal blisters and severe pruritus. In recent years, Dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitors (gliptins) have been recognized as a major drug trigger for BP, particularly in elderly patients. Loop diuretics (furosemide) are another classic trigger.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old girl being treated with a Beta-blocker for hypertension presents with an extremely itchy rash on their forearms and shins. Physical examination reveals symmetric, flat-topped, polygonal, purple papules. Fine white lines (Wickham striae) are visible on the surface under magnification. How does this drug-induced lichenoid eruption typically differ from classic, idiopathic Lichen Planus?', '["It is completely non-pruritic and does not show interface dermatitis on biopsy", "It presents with large, flaccid blisters and a positive Nikolsky sign", "It selectively targets the face and spares the extremities", "It is highly infectious and spreads to close contacts", "It is often more generalized, symmetric, and frequently lacks mucosal involvement or classic Wickham striae"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Lichenoid drug eruptions (triggered by beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, thiazides, and gold) clinically mimic Lichen Planus. However, they typically differ by being more generalized, symmetric, and having a eczematous or psoriasiform appearance, while frequently sparing the oral mucosa and showing a slower resolution after drug withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old male presents with an explosive onset of acne-like bumps on their chest and upper back. They deny having any acne as a teenager. Physical exam reveals dozens of monomorphic (all at the same stage of development) erythematous inflammatory papules and pustules. No blackheads or whiteheads (comedones) are present. The patient was recently treated with a high-dose oral prednisone taper. What is the diagnosis?', '["Acne vulgaris (papulopustular subtype)", "Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Hot tub folliculitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Steroid acne is an acneiform drug eruption caused by systemic or high-potency topical corticosteroids. Clinically, it is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules on the trunk and shoulders. Unlike acne vulgaris, steroid acne lacks comedones (blackheads/whiteheads) and the lesions are all at the identical stage of development.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient receiving a rapid intravenous infusion of Vancomycin suddenly develops severe flushing, pruritus, and a bright red rash over their face, neck, and upper torso. Their heart rate is 115/min and blood pressure is 98/60 mmHg. There is no wheezing, lip swelling, or hives. What is the primary mechanism of this reaction?', '["Type III hypersensitivity with deposition of IgA immune complexes in dermal vessels", "IgE-mediated Type I hypersensitivity reaction causing systemic anaphylaxis", "Direct toxic effect of the vancomycin molecule on vascular smooth muscle", "Direct, non-immunologic histamine release from mast cells triggered by the rapid drug infusion", "T-cell mediated cytotoxic destruction of endothelial cells"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is Red Man Syndrome, a pseudo-allergic (anaphylactoid) reaction caused by a rapid infusion of intravenous Vancomycin. The drug directly degranulates mast cells and basophils in a non-immunologic fashion, releasing histamine. It is not a true IgE-mediated allergy. It can be prevented by slowing the infusion rate (to over 60 minutes) and pre-treating with antihistamines.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old child presents with a widespread, hive-like rash, severe joint pain and swelling of the ankles, and a low-grade fever that began 10 days after starting Cefaclor for an ear infection. Lab work shows a normal platelet count and no proteinuria. What is the diagnosis?', '["Stevens-Johnson Syndrome", "Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR)", "Acute Rheumatic Fever", "True Serum Sickness", "Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura (IgA Vasculitis)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR) is a benign, transient reaction that classically occurs in children 1-3 weeks after starting certain drugs, most famously Cefaclor, as well as penicillins and sulfonamides. It mimics true serum sickness with fever, arthralgias, and urticarial plaques, but crucially LACKS circulating immune complexes, complement depletion, vasculitis, and renal damage (proteinuria).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female being treated with high-dose Capecitabine for metastatic colorectal cancer presents with painful redness, swelling, and tingling on their palms and soles. Physical examination reveals symmetric, bright erythema and edema of the palmar and plantar surfaces, with early desquamation. What is the clinical term for this condition?', '["Fixed Drug Eruption", "Contact Dermatitis", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (Hand-Foot Syndrome)", "Hand-Foot-and-Mouth Disease"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (also known as Hand-Foot Syndrome) is a common cutaneous side effect of several chemotherapy agents, most classically Capecitabine, 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU), and Cytarabine. It is caused by drug leaking from small capillaries into the thick skin of the palms and soles, leading to direct toxicity, pain, symmetric erythema, swelling, and peeling.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old male being treated with Cetuximab (an EGFR inhibitor) for lung cancer develops an itchy, acne-like rash consisting of papules and pustules on their face, neck, and upper chest. Comedones are completely absent. What is the clinical significance of this rash?', '["It is a sign of tumor progression and therapeutic failure", "It is caused by secondary adrenal insufficiency from the chemotherapy", "It indicates a life-threatening drug allergy and the Cetuximab must be stopped permanently", "It is a positive prognostic indicator (the development and severity of the rash correlate with a favorable tumor response)", "It represents a deep follicular bacterial infection requiring intravenous antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 3, 'EGFR inhibitor-induced acneiform (papulopustular) eruption is a highly common and expected class-effect of drugs like Cetuximab, Erlotinib, and Panitumumab. Counter-intuitively, the development and severity of this rash act as a positive surrogate marker for drug efficacy, correlating with improved tumor response and patient survival. It is managed with topical/oral antibiotics (doxycycline) and steroids without stopping the chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old boy who has been taking Minocycline for severe acne for 3 years presents with a new, muddy, blue-gray discoloration on their shins and the mucosal surface of their lower gums. A skin biopsy reveals iron-containing and melanin-containing pigment deposits within the dermis. What is the subtype and characteristic appearance of this minocycline-induced hyperpigmentation?', '["Type I Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, limited strictly to sites of prior inflammation and scarring", "Slate-gray facial pigmentation induced by drug-induced lipofuscin", "Argiria, caused by silver deposition in the eccrine glands", "Type III Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, presenting as a diffuse muddy-brown tan in sun-exposed areas", "Type II Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, affecting normal skin of the shins and forearms, as well as gums"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Minocycline can induce three distinct types of skin hyperpigmentation: Type I occurs strictly in scars and areas of prior inflammation (blue-black pigment); Type II occurs on normal skin, classically the shins, forearms, and gums (slate-blue/gray pigment containing iron and melanin); and Type III is a diffuse, muddy-brown hyperpigmentation in sun-exposed areas (pure melanin).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old girl with a history of refractory cardiac arrhythmias has been taking Amiodarone for 5 years. They present to the clinic complaining of a blue-gray discoloration of their face, nose, and ears. Biopsy of the affected skin reveals lipofuscin-like granules within dermal macrophages. What is the key environmental trigger required for the development of this specific pigmentation?', '["High dietary intake of vitamin C", "Exposure to cold weather (Raynaud-like)", "Ultraviolet (UV) light exposure (sunlight)", "Chronic mechanical friction", "Frequent hot water baths"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Amiodarone-induced hyperpigmentation is a classic side effect occurring in patients on long-term, high-dose amiodarone therapy. It presents as a slate-gray to blue-violet discoloration strictly in sun-exposed areas (face, nose, ears). Ultraviolet (UV) light is required to trigger the deposition of a drug-lipid complex (lipofuscin) inside dermal macrophages.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old man with systemic lupus erythematosus has been taking Hydroxychloroquine for 6 years. During a routine skin check, the dermatologist notes well-demarcated, slate-gray patches on the patient''s bilateral shins and a bluish-gray discoloration of the hard palate. What is the prognosis and clinical course of this pigmentation?', '["It is slowly reversible over months to years after stopping the hydroxychloroquine, though some areas may persist", "It is completely permanent and will continue to spread even if the drug is discontinued", "It indicates an immediate risk of acute hepatic failure", "It is highly infectious and can be transmitted to others", "It resolves spontaneously within 2 weeks of stopping the drug"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Skin and mucosal hyperpigmentation occurs in up to 25% of patients on long-term antimalarials (Chloroquine/Hydroxychloroquine). Classic sites are the shins (pretibial area), the hard palate, and the nail beds. The pigmentation is slowly reversible over many months or years after drug cessation, though some localized areas may remain permanently.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 32-year-old woman undergoing chemotherapy for Hodgkin''s lymphoma develops intensely pruritic, linear, criss-crossing red streaks on her back and chest that resemble ''whip marks''. She admits to scratching her skin due to dryness. Which of the following chemotherapy agents is the classic cause of this flagellate erythema?', '["Bleomycin", "5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)", "Paclitaxel", "Cyclophosphamide", "Doxorubicin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Flagellate erythema is a virtually pathognomonic cutaneous toxicity associated with Bleomycin therapy. It presents as highly characteristic, linear, criss-crossing erythematous streaks that look like ''whip marks'' on the back and trunk. It represents a Koebner-like response where minor scratching or friction concentrates the drug in the local skin vessels.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old female presents with progressive, painless, firm, fibrous swelling of their gums that has begun to cover the crowns of their teeth. They have a history of epilepsy. Which of the following triads of medications represents the classic causes of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia?', '["Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen", "Prednisone, Isotretinoin, and Spironolactone", "Metformin, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Phenytoin, Cyclosporine, and Nifedipine", "Penicillin, Doxycycline, and Cephalexin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Drug-induced gingival hyperplasia is a classic side effect characterized by fibrous overgrowth of the gingival papillae. The three classic, high-yield culprits are Phenytoin (anti-epileptic), Cyclosporine (immunosuppressant), and Calcium Channel Blockers (especially Nifedipine, but also Amlodipine/Diltiazem).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old girl taking Naproxen for osteoarthritic pain presents with skin fragility and multiple small, tense vesicles and erosions on the dorsum of their hands. Biopsy reveals a subepidermal blister with minimal inflammatory cell infiltrate. Laboratory workup demonstrates normal levels of urinary and fecal porphyrins, ruling out Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT). What is the diagnosis?', '["Bullous Pemphigoid", "Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT)", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita", "Allergic Contact Dermatitis", "Pseudoporphyria"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Pseudoporphyria is a drug-induced condition that clinically and histologically mimics Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT), presenting with skin fragility, blistering, and scarring on sun-exposed hands. Crucially, porphyrin levels in the blood, urine, and stool are completely normal in pseudoporphyria. NSAIDs (most famously Naproxen) are the leading cause.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 25-year-old female presents with multiple extremely painful, warm, red, deep-seated nodules on her bilateral shins that have been present for 2 weeks. She reports starting oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) 2 months ago. A biopsy reveals septal panniculitis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Polyarteritis Nodosa (PAN)", "Sweet''s syndrome", "Erythema Induratum (Bazin''s disease)", "Erythema Nodosum (EN)", "Weber-Christian disease"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Erythema Nodosum (EN) is a classic form of panniculitis (inflammation of subcutaneous fat) that presents as tender, warm, erythematous nodules on the pretibial shins. It represents a hypersensitivity reaction to various triggers, including infections (strep, TB) and medications—most commonly oral contraceptives (OCPs) and sulfonamides. Histology classically shows septal panniculitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old child presents with high fever, severe mucosal crusting and ulceration of the lips and mouth, and conjunctival redness with purulent discharge. Physical exam reveals less than 1% BSA of skin detachment with only a few targetoid macules on the limbs. The child was treated for ''walking pneumonia'' 1 week ago. What is the diagnosis?', '["Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Primary Herpes Simplex Gingivostomatitis", "Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS) is triggered by Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection, most commonly in children and young adults. It is characterized by severe mucositis involving $\ge 2$ mucosal sites (typically oral and ocular) with minimal or absent cutaneous lesions (<1% BSA skin involvement). It mimics SJS but has a distinct pathogenesis and carries a significantly better prognosis with lower mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old patient presents with painful, dark purple, necrotic, star-shaped (retiform) patches on their bilateral earlobes, cheeks, and lower extremities. Lab work reveals a severe neutropenia (WBC 1,200/mm3 with an absolute neutrophil count of 350/mm3). A urine drug screen is positive for Cocaine. What is the causative agent responsible for this patient''s necrotic ear skin and bone marrow suppression?', '["Direct vasoconstrictive toxicity of the cocaine molecule itself", "Levamisole (a veterinary dewormer used as a cocaine cutting agent)", "An IgE-mediated drug allergy to benzoylecgonine", "Amiodarone-induced photo-vasculitis", "A contamination of cocaine with Streptococcus pyogenes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has Levamisole-induced retiform purpura. Levamisole is a veterinary anthelmintic commonly used as an adulterant (cutting agent) in illicit cocaine. It triggers a unique, severe autoimmune vasculitis characterized by painful, retiform (star-shaped) purpura with central necrosis, characteristically involving the ears/earlobes and face. It is also a potent bone marrow suppressant, leading to severe neutropenia/agranulocytosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old male being treated with Hydralazine for 8 years presents with fever, joint pain, weight loss, and palpable purple spots (purpura) on their lower legs. Lab results show a positive ANA, dual positivity for p-ANCA (MPO) and c-ANCA (PR3) antibodies, and urinalysis reveals proteinuria and hematuria with red blood cell casts. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura", "Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE)", "Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis is a severe necrotizing vasculitis classically triggered by long-term use of Hydralazine, Propylthiouracil (PTU), or Methimazole. Unlike simple drug-induced lupus (DILE), drug-induced ANCA vasculitis features high titers of p-ANCA (MPO) and PR3, palpable purpura, and significant internal organ involvement—most notably rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (renal failure).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old female on Captopril (an ACE inhibitor) develops a widespread scaly rash on their trunk. They report a large, oval, scaly ''herald patch'' appeared on their chest first, followed by smaller oval plaques along the skin tension lines on their back in a ''Christmas tree'' distribution. How does this drug-induced Pityriasis Rosea-like eruption typically differ from idiopathic Pityriasis Rosea?', '["It presents with deep, tense bullae and a positive Nikolsky sign", "It is highly contagious and spreads to children via respiratory droplets", "It strictly affects the face and spares the trunk", "It is accompanied by severe kidney damage and proteinuria", "It often lacks a distinct initial herald patch and takes much longer to resolve after stopping the drug"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Pityriasis Rosea-like drug eruptions (triggered by ACE inhibitors, gold, beta-blockers, and metronidazole) closely mimic classic PR. However, the drug-induced form typically lacks the single large ''herald patch'' that precedes the main eruption, shows more confluent and inflammatory lesions, and is much more persistent, resolving slowly only after the offending drug is discontinued.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a history of stable, mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with bipolar disorder and started on Lithium. Two weeks later, they present with an explosive, severe flare of psoriasis, with thick, silvery plaques now covering 40% of their BSA. Which of the following lists represents the ''big four'' systemic drugs most famous for flaring or worsening psoriasis?', '["Statins, Fibrates, Metformin, and Lisinopril", "Lithium, Beta-blockers, Antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), and NSAIDs", "Amlodipine, Warfarin, Heparin, and Aspirin", "Penicillins, Cephalosporins, Macrolides, and Sulfonamides", "Isotretinoin, Spironolactone, Cyclosporine, and Prednisone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Systemic drugs are common triggers for the onset or severe exacerbation of psoriasis. The classic, high-yield ''big four'' culprits are Lithium (bipolar therapy), Beta-blockers (cardiovascular therapy), Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine/chloroquine), and NSAIDs. Systemic steroid withdrawal can also precipitate a life-threatening rebound flare of generalized pustular psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Immediately after receiving an intramuscular (IM) injection of Penicillin G benzathine in the upper outer quadrant of the buttock, a 45-year-old female develops excruciating, burning pain and livedo-like, blue-gray discoloration around the injection site. Within 48 hours, this area develops deep, necrotic sloughing of the skin and underlying gluteal muscle. What is this clinical entity?', '["Necrotizing fasciitis from bacterial contamination", "Warfarin-induced skin necrosis", "An IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction", "Arthus reaction from vaccine immune complex deposition", "Nicolau Syndrome (Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Nicolau Syndrome (also known as Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa) is a rare, devastating non-allergic vascular complication of intramuscular injections. It occurs due to accidental intra-arterial injection, peri-arterial injection, or drug-induced vasospasm/thrombosis. It presents with immediate severe pain, retiform cyanosis, and subsequent deep tissue necrosis of the skin, fat, and muscle.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing chemotherapy for leukemia is treated with G-CSF (Granulocyte Colony-Stimulating Factor) to manage neutropenia. Three days later, they develop a high fever (39.5 C) and several painful, bright red, ''succulent'' or ''juicy'' plaques on their neck and upper back. Biopsy reveals a massive, dense neutrophilic infiltrate in the upper dermis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pyoderma Gangrenosum", "Sweet''s Syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis)", "AGEP", "Erysipelas", "DRESS syndrome"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sweet''s syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis) is characterized by high fever, leukocytosis, and tender, erythematous plaques that are clinically described as ''juicy'' or ''succulent'' due to profound upper dermal edema. Histology shows a dense dermal neutrophilic infiltrate without vasculitis. G-CSF is a classic drug trigger because it directly stimulates neutrophil production.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old woman being treated with Paclitaxel for breast cancer develops bilateral, woody thickening and hardening of the skin of their lower legs and forearms. Physical exam shows marked induration, but there is an absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon or nail fold capillary changes, and systemic organ involvement is absent. ANA testing is negative. What is the diagnosis?', '["Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma)", "Taxane-induced scleroderma-like changes", "Eosinophilic Fasciitis", "Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis", "Stasis dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Taxanes (Paclitaxel, Docetaxel) can induce scleroderma-like skin changes (induration, woody thickening) typically localized to the extremities. The key clinical clues to distinguish this drug-induced form from idiopathic Systemic Sclerosis are the absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon, normal nail fold capillaries, negative autoantibodies (ANA/Scl-70), and lack of visceral sclerosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old woman taking Vemurafenib (a BRAF inhibitor) for metastatic melanoma develops several fast-growing, dome-shaped nodules with central keratinous craters on their arms within 3 weeks of starting the drug. Biopsy of one lesion shows a well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma/keratoacanthoma (SCC/KA). What is the mechanism driving these secondary skin malignancies?', '["A metastatic spread of the primary melanoma to the skin", "Direct mutagenic damage to epidermal stem cell DNA by the drug molecule", "Systemic immunosuppression leading to explosive reactivation of HPV-16", "Upregulation of the Hedgehog pathway in follicular basal cells", "Paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do NOT have the BRAF mutation"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Vemurafenib and other BRAF inhibitors cause paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal cells that do not harbor the BRAF V600E mutation. The drug binds to wild-type BRAF, causing it to homodimerize and hyper-activate downstream MEK/ERK signaling, driving rapid keratinocyte proliferation and leading to eruptive Keratoacanthomas and low-grade Squamous Cell Carcinomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old man on Sorafenib (a multi-kinase inhibitor) for renal cell carcinoma develops thick, painful, yellow calluses surrounded by a bright red halo on the heels and lateral aspects of their feet. How does this Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) clinically differ from Capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS)?', '["Classic HFS is caused by direct exposure to sunlight", "HFSR presents as localized hyperkeratosis and thick calluses on pressure points, whereas classic HFS is characterized by diffuse, symmetric erythema and peeling", "There is no clinical difference between the two reactions", "HFSR strictly affects the fingernails and spares the skin", "HFSR is completely painless and resolves within 24 hours of starting the drug"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While both affect the hands/feet, Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) is clinically distinct from capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS). HFSR is characterized by localized, hyperkeratotic, thick yellow plaques and blisters on high-friction pressure points (heels/lateral sole), while classic HFS (capecitabine) presents as diffuse, confluent erythema, edema, and desquamation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old female with chronic renal insufficiency receives intravenous iodinated contrast for a CT scan. Two days later, they develop painful, rapidly expanding, pustular, vegetating plaques on their face and lower extremities. Pustule culture is negative. Biopsy shows massive epidermal hyperplasia (pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia) and dense neutrophilic microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Sweet''s syndrome", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Pyoderma Gangrenosum", "Iododerma (Halogenoderma)", "Blastomycosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Halogenodermas (Iododerma and Bromoderma) are rare cutaneous toxic accumulations of halogens, most commonly occurring in patients with impaired renal clearance. Clinically, they present as highly inflammatory, pustular, vegetating plaques that can closely mimic Pyoderma Gangrenosum, deep fungal infections (like Blastomycosis), or Squamous Cell Carcinoma. Histology shows marked pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department with a painful rash, high fever, and severe eye soreness that began 2 weeks after starting Allopurinol. Physical exam reveals confluent, atypical targetoid macules with flaccid blisters and sloughing covering approximately 8% of the total body surface area (BSA). Two mucosal sites are severely involved. Skin biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis with minimal dermal inflammation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Erythema Multiforme Major", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS), defined by fever, mucosal involvement of $\ge 2$ sites, targetoid macules, and epidermal detachment affecting <10% of the body surface area (BSA). Biopsy characteristically shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis (apoptosis of keratinocytes) driven by cytotoxic T-cells and granulysin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old male on Phenytoin for seizures presents with fever and widespread skin tenderness. Within 24 hours, the skin begins to peel off in large sheets. Physical examination shows dusky erythema progressing to confluent blisters. Gentle lateral pressure on the erythematous skin causes the epidermis to slide off the dermis (positive Nikolsky sign). The epidermal detachment is estimated at 35% of the total body surface area. What is the primary cause of mortality in this condition?', '["Acute kidney injury from severe myoglobinuria", "Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "Infection, sepsis, and multi-organ failure", "Hepatocellular failure and acute encephalopathy", "High-output cardiac failure and hypothermia"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN), characterized by epidermal detachment of >30% BSA and a positive Nikolsky sign. The loss of the skin barrier leads to massive fluid shifts, hypothermia, and high-output heart failure, but the leading cause of death in these patients is infection (sepsis) and subsequent multi-organ failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 26-year-old girl presents with a painful, blistering rash and fever. They recently started Sulfasalazine for inflammatory bowel disease. Physical examination reveals atypical targetoid plaques on the trunk and extremities, with flaccid bullae that slide off under lateral pressure. The surface area of epidermal detachment is measured at exactly 18% of the total BSA. What is the clinical category of this patient''s drug reaction?', '["Erythema Multiforme Major", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "DRESS syndrome"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The SJS/TEN spectrum is categorized by the percentage of body surface area showing epidermal detachment: SJS involves <10% BSA detachment; SJS/TEN Overlap involves 10% to 30% BSA detachment; and TEN involves >30% BSA detachment. At 18% detachment, this patient falls into the SJS/TEN Overlap category.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old woman of East Asian descent is about to start Carbamazepine for neuropathic pain. The clinician orders genetic screening for the HLA-B*1502 allele before prescribing. What is the clinical significance of this allele?', '["It carries an extremely high risk of Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in Asian populations", "It indicates an increased risk of severe, irreversible agranulocytosis", "It correlates with an increased risk of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia", "It is a marker for drug-induced phototoxicity and exaggerated sunburns", "It is associated with rapid metabolic clearance of Carbamazepine, making it ineffective"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The HLA-B*1502 allele is strongly associated with an increased risk of developing Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in individuals of East Asian and South-East Asian descent. Screening is mandatory or highly recommended prior to starting Carbamazepine in these high-risk populations.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old boy with Han Chinese ancestry is diagnosed with gout and is about to start Allopurinol therapy. Which HLA allele is strongly associated with Allopurinol-induced Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) in this population?', '["HLA-DRB1*0401", "HLA-B*1502", "HLA-B*5701", "HLA-B*2705", "HLA-B*5801"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The HLA-B*5801 allele is a potent genetic risk factor for Allopurinol-induced severe cutaneous adverse reactions, including SJS and TEN, particularly in Han Chinese, Thai, and Korean populations. HLA-B*1502 is associated with carbamazepine, and HLA-B*5701 is linked to abacavir hypersensitivity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old girl with Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) involving 45% BSA is admitted to the hospital. Which of the following clinical settings is the most appropriate for the management of this patient?', '["A specialized Burn Unit or Intensive Care Unit (ICU)", "A standard dermatology inpatient ward", "A clean private room in a general medical ward", "An outpatient clinic with daily nurse home visits", "An infectious disease isolation room"]'::jsonb, 0, 'TEN is a life-threatening dermatologic emergency. Due to extensive skin loss, patients require meticulous sterile wound care, specialized non-adherent dressings, tight fluid resuscitation, and temperature regulation identical to severe burn victims. Management in a specialized Burn Unit or intensive care setting significantly reduces mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old man began taking Carbamazepine 4 weeks ago. They now present with a high fever (39.0 C), a widespread red skin eruption, and severe facial and periorbital swelling. Physical exam reveals confluent morbilliform plaques on the trunk, palpable lymphadenopathy in the neck and axillae, and moderate tender hepatomegaly. Lab work shows a WBC of 16,500/mm3 with 18% eosinophils (absolute eosinophil count 2,970/mm3) and AST/ALT elevated at 4 times the upper limit of normal. What is the diagnosis?', '["Exanthematous (Morbilliform) Drug Eruption", "Serum Sickness", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS) syndrome", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'DRESS syndrome (also known as Drug-Induced Hypersensitivity Syndrome) is a severe, life-threatening drug reaction characterized by a long latency period (2-8 weeks), high fever, a diffuse erythematous eruption, facial/periorbital edema, lymphadenopathy, hematologic abnormalities (eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytosis), and visceral organ involvement—most commonly hepatitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is diagnosed with DRESS syndrome after starting Phenytoin 6 weeks ago. Aside from stopping the offending drug, what virus reactivation is frequently associated with the pathogenesis and flare-ups of this systemic drug hypersensitivity?', '["Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)", "Hepatitis C Virus (HCV)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'DRESS syndrome is strongly associated with the sequential reactivation of latent herpesviruses, most notably Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6), as well as HHV-7, EBV, and CMV. This viral reactivation is thought to drive the prolonged, recurrent course of the disease even after the causative drug has been completely cleared.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old female is started on Cephalexin for a suspected soft tissue infection. Forty-eight hours later, they develop a high fever (39.2 C) and a burning rash that started in the groin and axillae. Physical exam reveals diffuse, bright erythema studded with hundreds of non-follicular, sterile, pinpoint pustules. Lab work shows a marked leukocytosis with neutrophilia (WBC 19,000/mm3). Pustule cultures are sterile. What is the diagnosis?', '["DRESS syndrome", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "Subcorneal Pustular Dermatosis", "Generalized Pustular Psoriasis (Von Zumbusch)", "Disseminated candidiasis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AGEP is a rare, severe cutaneous reaction characterized by the rapid onset (typically <48 hours after drug exposure) of high fever and hundreds of small, sterile, non-follicular pustules arising on a background of edematous erythema, classically starting in flexural folds (groin/axillae). Beta-lactams and macrolides are the most common triggers. Leukocytosis with marked neutrophilia is characteristic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female presents with a dark, round spot on their left forearm. They note that every time they take Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection, the exact same spot becomes red, swollen, and itchy, occasionally forming a small central blister. Once it heals, a persistent dusky brown patch remains. What is the mechanism of this ''fixing'' to the same anatomic site?', '["Localized accumulation of IgE molecules on dermal mast cells", "Deposition of circulating IgG immune complexes in the local capillary bed", "Localized somatic mutation in the keratinocytes of that area", "Slow lymphatic clearance of the drug from that specific site", "Persistence of resident memory CD8+ T-cells in the affected epidermis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Fixed Drug Eruption (FDE) is characterized by recurrent erythematous plaques that appear in the exact same localized site (skin or mucosa) upon re-exposure to the offending drug. This site-specific memory is mediated by resident memory CD8+ T-cells that remain quiescent in the basal epidermis of the affected plaque and rapidly degranulate upon systemic re-exposure to the drug.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old man presents with a single, highly circumscribed, dusky-red, itchy patch on their glans penis that developed 24 hours after taking a medication. Which of the following drugs is the most common trigger for a fixed drug eruption (FDE) affecting the genitalia?', '["Atorvastatin", "Amlodipine", "Aspirin", "Metformin", "Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (Sulfonamides)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'While FDE can be triggered by many drugs (including NSAIDs and tetracyclines), sulfonamides—most notably Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole—are the most common triggers for fixed drug eruptions located on the genitalia (glans penis or labia).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old male is started on Warfarin for a new deep vein thrombosis. Due to an oversight, no Heparin bridge is administered. On day 3 of therapy, the patient develops severe pain and dark purple, well-demarcated patches on their breasts and thighs. Within 24 hours, these lesions progress to necrotic black eschars. What is the primary molecular cause of this reaction?', '["Direct toxic necrosis of adipocytes caused by accumulation of the warfarin molecule", "An IgE-mediated anaphylactoid reaction causing localized vasculitis", "A rapid drop in Protein C levels (due to its short half-life), causing a transient hypercoagulable state and microthrombosis", "A drop in antithrombin III levels leading to arterial spasm", "Localized vasoconstriction from paradoxical alpha-adrenergic stimulation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Warfarin-induced skin necrosis occurs early in therapy (typically days 3-5) when warfarin is initiated without a heparin bridge, especially in patients with hereditary Protein C or S deficiency. Protein C has a very short half-life (approx 8 hours) and falls rapidly, while procoagulant factors II, IX, and X fall slowly. This creates a transient, severe hypercoagulable state leading to microthrombosis in the capillaries supplying fat-rich areas (breasts, thighs, buttocks).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient on subcutaneous Unfractionated Heparin therapy for a pulmonary embolism develops painful, red-to-purple patches at their abdominal injection sites 7 days into therapy, which quickly progress to localized skin necrosis. Lab work reveals a platelet count that has dropped from 280,000/mm3 to 90,000/mm3. What is the target of the autoantibodies driving this clinical presentation?', '["Protein C and Protein S", "Type XVII Collagen (BP180) in the basement membrane", "Desmoglein 1 and Desmoglein 3 in the epidermis", "The G-protein coupled thrombin receptor", "Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT) presenting with localized heparin-induced skin necrosis at the injection sites. HIT is mediated by IgG autoantibodies targeting Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes, which bind and activate platelets, causing both thrombocytopenia (clearance of platelets) and a severe, life-threatening prothrombotic state.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient develops heparin-induced skin necrosis and thrombocytopenia (HIT). Which of the following is the most critical and immediate next step in management?', '["Increase the heparin dose to overcome the antibody-mediated clearance", "Transfuse 2 units of platelets and continue subcutaneous heparin", "Stop heparin, administer Vitamin K, and start oral warfarin immediately", "Discontinue all heparin products immediately and initiate a non-heparin anticoagulant (e.g., Argatroban)", "Apply high-potency topical steroids to the necrotic injection sites"]'::jsonb, 3, 'When HIT is suspected or confirmed (due to a platelet drop >50% and/or skin necrosis), the immediate action must be to stop all heparin products (including low-molecular-weight heparin and heparin flushes) and start an alternative, non-heparin rapid anticoagulant (e.g., a direct thrombin inhibitor like Argatroban or Bivalirudin) to prevent catastrophic thromboembolism. Warfarin is contraindicated in active HIT as it can precipitate gangrene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old woman being treated with Hydralazine for severe hypertension presents with joint pain, fever, and a mild malar-like rash on their face. Lab results show a positive ANA (1:640) and highly elevated Anti-Histone antibodies. Anti-dsDNA and Anti-Smith antibodies are negative. Urinalysis and serum creatinine are normal. What is the clinical significance of anti-histone antibodies in this patient?', '["They represent a high risk of transition to Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis", "They are caused by a cross-reaction with the hydralazine molecule itself", "They are highly sensitive (approx 95%) for drug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE)", "They are highly specific for idiopathic systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)", "They indicate severe, silent lupus nephritis requiring renal biopsy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Anti-histone antibodies are highly sensitive (present in 90-95% of cases) for Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE). Common triggers include Hydralazine, Procainamide, and Isoniazid. Unlike idiopathic SLE, DILE rarely involves the kidneys or central nervous system, and anti-dsDNA or anti-Smith antibodies are usually absent.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old woman being treated with D-Penicillamine for Wilson''s disease develops flaccid blisters on their chest and upper back, along with painful sores in their mouth. Physical exam shows a positive Nikolsky sign. A skin biopsy reveals intraepidermal acantholysis. Direct Immunofluorescence (DIF) demonstrates intercellular IgG deposition in a ''fishnet'' pattern throughout the epidermis. What is the target of the autoantibodies in this drug-induced condition?', '["Type VII Collagen in the anchoring fibrils", "BP180 (Type XVII Collagen) and BP230", "Desmogleins (Dsg1 and Dsg3)", "Integrin alpha-6 beta-4 in the hemidesmosomes", "Laminin-332 in the lamina lucida"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Drug-Induced Pemphigus, characteristically triggered by thiol (sulfhydryl) group-containing drugs like D-Penicillamine, Captopril, and Gold salts. Thiol groups can biochemically disrupt desmosomes or act as haptens. The autoantibodies target desmoglein 1 and/or 3, leading to flaccid blisters, mucosal lesions, acantholysis, and a ''fishnet'' DIF pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An 82-year-old male on Linagliptin (a DPP-4 inhibitor) for type 2 diabetes presents with an intensely pruritic rash and several large, firm, tense blisters on their abdomen and lower extremities. Nikolsky sign is negative. A biopsy shows a subepidermal split with numerous eosinophils. DIF demonstrates a linear band of IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug-induced Bullous Pemphigoid", "Drug-induced Pemphigus Vulgaris", "Linear IgA Bullous Dermatosis", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita", "Pseudoporphyria"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has Drug-induced Bullous Pemphigoid (BP), characteristically presenting with tense subepidermal blisters and severe pruritus. In recent years, Dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitors (gliptins) have been recognized as a major drug trigger for BP, particularly in elderly patients. Loop diuretics (furosemide) are another classic trigger.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old woman being treated with a Beta-blocker for hypertension presents with an extremely itchy rash on their forearms and shins. Physical examination reveals symmetric, flat-topped, polygonal, purple papules. Fine white lines (Wickham striae) are visible on the surface under magnification. How does this drug-induced lichenoid eruption typically differ from classic, idiopathic Lichen Planus?', '["It presents with large, flaccid blisters and a positive Nikolsky sign", "It selectively targets the face and spares the extremities", "It is completely non-pruritic and does not show interface dermatitis on biopsy", "It is often more generalized, symmetric, and frequently lacks mucosal involvement or classic Wickham striae", "It is highly infectious and spreads to close contacts"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Lichenoid drug eruptions (triggered by beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, thiazides, and gold) clinically mimic Lichen Planus. However, they typically differ by being more generalized, symmetric, and having a eczematous or psoriasiform appearance, while frequently sparing the oral mucosa and showing a slower resolution after drug withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old boy presents with an explosive onset of acne-like bumps on their chest and upper back. They deny having any acne as a teenager. Physical exam reveals dozens of monomorphic (all at the same stage of development) erythematous inflammatory papules and pustules. No blackheads or whiteheads (comedones) are present. The patient was recently treated with a high-dose oral prednisone taper. What is the diagnosis?', '["Hot tub folliculitis", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Acne vulgaris (papulopustular subtype)", "Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Papulopustular rosacea"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Steroid acne is an acneiform drug eruption caused by systemic or high-potency topical corticosteroids. Clinically, it is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules on the trunk and shoulders. Unlike acne vulgaris, steroid acne lacks comedones (blackheads/whiteheads) and the lesions are all at the identical stage of development.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old child presents with a widespread, hive-like rash, severe joint pain and swelling of the ankles, and a low-grade fever that began 10 days after starting Cefaclor for an ear infection. Lab work shows a normal platelet count and no proteinuria. What is the diagnosis?', '["Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura (IgA Vasculitis)", "Acute Rheumatic Fever", "Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR)", "True Serum Sickness", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR) is a benign, transient reaction that classically occurs in children 1-3 weeks after starting certain drugs, most famously Cefaclor, as well as penicillins and sulfonamides. It mimics true serum sickness with fever, arthralgias, and urticarial plaques, but crucially LACKS circulating immune complexes, complement depletion, vasculitis, and renal damage (proteinuria).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old woman being treated with high-dose Capecitabine for metastatic colorectal cancer presents with painful redness, swelling, and tingling on their palms and soles. Physical examination reveals symmetric, bright erythema and edema of the palmar and plantar surfaces, with early desquamation. What is the clinical term for this condition?', '["Fixed Drug Eruption", "Hand-Foot-and-Mouth Disease", "Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (Hand-Foot Syndrome)", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Contact Dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (also known as Hand-Foot Syndrome) is a common cutaneous side effect of several chemotherapy agents, most classically Capecitabine, 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU), and Cytarabine. It is caused by drug leaking from small capillaries into the thick skin of the palms and soles, leading to direct toxicity, pain, symmetric erythema, swelling, and peeling.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old female being treated with Cetuximab (an EGFR inhibitor) for lung cancer develops an itchy, acne-like rash consisting of papules and pustules on their face, neck, and upper chest. Comedones are completely absent. What is the clinical significance of this rash?', '["It is a sign of tumor progression and therapeutic failure", "It indicates a life-threatening drug allergy and the Cetuximab must be stopped permanently", "It is caused by secondary adrenal insufficiency from the chemotherapy", "It is a positive prognostic indicator (the development and severity of the rash correlate with a favorable tumor response)", "It represents a deep follicular bacterial infection requiring intravenous antibiotics"]'::jsonb, 3, 'EGFR inhibitor-induced acneiform (papulopustular) eruption is a highly common and expected class-effect of drugs like Cetuximab, Erlotinib, and Panitumumab. Counter-intuitively, the development and severity of this rash act as a positive surrogate marker for drug efficacy, correlating with improved tumor response and patient survival. It is managed with topical/oral antibiotics (doxycycline) and steroids without stopping the chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old man who has been taking Minocycline for severe acne for 3 years presents with a new, muddy, blue-gray discoloration on their shins and the mucosal surface of their lower gums. A skin biopsy reveals iron-containing and melanin-containing pigment deposits within the dermis. What is the subtype and characteristic appearance of this minocycline-induced hyperpigmentation?', '["Argiria, caused by silver deposition in the eccrine glands", "Type I Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, limited strictly to sites of prior inflammation and scarring", "Type III Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, presenting as a diffuse muddy-brown tan in sun-exposed areas", "Type II Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, affecting normal skin of the shins and forearms, as well as gums", "Slate-gray facial pigmentation induced by drug-induced lipofuscin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Minocycline can induce three distinct types of skin hyperpigmentation: Type I occurs strictly in scars and areas of prior inflammation (blue-black pigment); Type II occurs on normal skin, classically the shins, forearms, and gums (slate-blue/gray pigment containing iron and melanin); and Type III is a diffuse, muddy-brown hyperpigmentation in sun-exposed areas (pure melanin).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old female with a history of refractory cardiac arrhythmias has been taking Amiodarone for 5 years. They present to the clinic complaining of a blue-gray discoloration of their face, nose, and ears. Biopsy of the affected skin reveals lipofuscin-like granules within dermal macrophages. What is the key environmental trigger required for the development of this specific pigmentation?', '["High dietary intake of vitamin C", "Chronic mechanical friction", "Exposure to cold weather (Raynaud-like)", "Frequent hot water baths", "Ultraviolet (UV) light exposure (sunlight)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Amiodarone-induced hyperpigmentation is a classic side effect occurring in patients on long-term, high-dose amiodarone therapy. It presents as a slate-gray to blue-violet discoloration strictly in sun-exposed areas (face, nose, ears). Ultraviolet (UV) light is required to trigger the deposition of a drug-lipid complex (lipofuscin) inside dermal macrophages.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has been taking Hydroxychloroquine for 6 years. During a routine skin check, the dermatologist notes well-demarcated, slate-gray patches on the patient''s bilateral shins and a bluish-gray discoloration of the hard palate. What is the prognosis and clinical course of this pigmentation?', '["It is highly infectious and can be transmitted to others", "It indicates an immediate risk of acute hepatic failure", "It is completely permanent and will continue to spread even if the drug is discontinued", "It resolves spontaneously within 2 weeks of stopping the drug", "It is slowly reversible over months to years after stopping the hydroxychloroquine, though some areas may persist"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Skin and mucosal hyperpigmentation occurs in up to 25% of patients on long-term antimalarials (Chloroquine/Hydroxychloroquine). Classic sites are the shins (pretibial area), the hard palate, and the nail beds. The pigmentation is slowly reversible over many months or years after drug cessation, though some localized areas may remain permanently.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 32-year-old woman undergoing chemotherapy for Hodgkin''s lymphoma develops intensely pruritic, linear, criss-crossing red streaks on her back and chest that resemble ''whip marks''. She admits to scratching her skin due to dryness. Which of the following chemotherapy agents is the classic cause of this flagellate erythema?', '["Paclitaxel", "Doxorubicin", "5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)", "Cyclophosphamide", "Bleomycin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Flagellate erythema is a virtually pathognomonic cutaneous toxicity associated with Bleomycin therapy. It presents as highly characteristic, linear, criss-crossing erythematous streaks that look like ''whip marks'' on the back and trunk. It represents a Koebner-like response where minor scratching or friction concentrates the drug in the local skin vessels.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old girl presents with progressive, painless, firm, fibrous swelling of their gums that has begun to cover the crowns of their teeth. They have a history of epilepsy. Which of the following triads of medications represents the classic causes of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia?', '["Phenytoin, Cyclosporine, and Nifedipine", "Prednisone, Isotretinoin, and Spironolactone", "Metformin, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen", "Penicillin, Doxycycline, and Cephalexin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Drug-induced gingival hyperplasia is a classic side effect characterized by fibrous overgrowth of the gingival papillae. The three classic, high-yield culprits are Phenytoin (anti-epileptic), Cyclosporine (immunosuppressant), and Calcium Channel Blockers (especially Nifedipine, but also Amlodipine/Diltiazem).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female taking Naproxen for osteoarthritic pain presents with skin fragility and multiple small, tense vesicles and erosions on the dorsum of their hands. Biopsy reveals a subepidermal blister with minimal inflammatory cell infiltrate. Laboratory workup demonstrates normal levels of urinary and fecal porphyrins, ruling out Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT). What is the diagnosis?', '["Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT)", "Allergic Contact Dermatitis", "Pseudoporphyria", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita", "Bullous Pemphigoid"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pseudoporphyria is a drug-induced condition that clinically and histologically mimics Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT), presenting with skin fragility, blistering, and scarring on sun-exposed hands. Crucially, porphyrin levels in the blood, urine, and stool are completely normal in pseudoporphyria. NSAIDs (most famously Naproxen) are the leading cause.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 25-year-old female presents with multiple extremely painful, warm, red, deep-seated nodules on her bilateral shins that have been present for 2 weeks. She reports starting oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) 2 months ago. A biopsy reveals septal panniculitis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Weber-Christian disease", "Erythema Induratum (Bazin''s disease)", "Sweet''s syndrome", "Erythema Nodosum (EN)", "Polyarteritis Nodosa (PAN)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Erythema Nodosum (EN) is a classic form of panniculitis (inflammation of subcutaneous fat) that presents as tender, warm, erythematous nodules on the pretibial shins. It represents a hypersensitivity reaction to various triggers, including infections (strep, TB) and medications—most commonly oral contraceptives (OCPs) and sulfonamides. Histology classically shows septal panniculitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old child presents with high fever, severe mucosal crusting and ulceration of the lips and mouth, and conjunctival redness with purulent discharge. Physical exam reveals less than 1% BSA of skin detachment with only a few targetoid macules on the limbs. The child was treated for ''walking pneumonia'' 1 week ago. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS)", "Primary Herpes Simplex Gingivostomatitis", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS) is triggered by Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection, most commonly in children and young adults. It is characterized by severe mucositis involving $\ge 2$ mucosal sites (typically oral and ocular) with minimal or absent cutaneous lesions (<1% BSA skin involvement). It mimics SJS but has a distinct pathogenesis and carries a significantly better prognosis with lower mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old patient presents with painful, dark purple, necrotic, star-shaped (retiform) patches on their bilateral earlobes, cheeks, and lower extremities. Lab work reveals a severe neutropenia (WBC 1,200/mm3 with an absolute neutrophil count of 350/mm3). A urine drug screen is positive for Cocaine. What is the causative agent responsible for this patient''s necrotic ear skin and bone marrow suppression?', '["An IgE-mediated drug allergy to benzoylecgonine", "A contamination of cocaine with Streptococcus pyogenes", "Amiodarone-induced photo-vasculitis", "Direct vasoconstrictive toxicity of the cocaine molecule itself", "Levamisole (a veterinary dewormer used as a cocaine cutting agent)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Levamisole-induced retiform purpura. Levamisole is a veterinary anthelmintic commonly used as an adulterant (cutting agent) in illicit cocaine. It triggers a unique, severe autoimmune vasculitis characterized by painful, retiform (star-shaped) purpura with central necrosis, characteristically involving the ears/earlobes and face. It is also a potent bone marrow suppressant, leading to severe neutropenia/agranulocytosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old woman being treated with Hydralazine for 8 years presents with fever, joint pain, weight loss, and palpable purple spots (purpura) on their lower legs. Lab results show a positive ANA, dual positivity for p-ANCA (MPO) and c-ANCA (PR3) antibodies, and urinalysis reveals proteinuria and hematuria with red blood cell casts. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE)", "Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis", "Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis is a severe necrotizing vasculitis classically triggered by long-term use of Hydralazine, Propylthiouracil (PTU), or Methimazole. Unlike simple drug-induced lupus (DILE), drug-induced ANCA vasculitis features high titers of p-ANCA (MPO) and PR3, palpable purpura, and significant internal organ involvement—most notably rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (renal failure).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old boy on Captopril (an ACE inhibitor) develops a widespread scaly rash on their trunk. They report a large, oval, scaly ''herald patch'' appeared on their chest first, followed by smaller oval plaques along the skin tension lines on their back in a ''Christmas tree'' distribution. How does this drug-induced Pityriasis Rosea-like eruption typically differ from idiopathic Pityriasis Rosea?', '["It presents with deep, tense bullae and a positive Nikolsky sign", "It often lacks a distinct initial herald patch and takes much longer to resolve after stopping the drug", "It is accompanied by severe kidney damage and proteinuria", "It strictly affects the face and spares the trunk", "It is highly contagious and spreads to children via respiratory droplets"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pityriasis Rosea-like drug eruptions (triggered by ACE inhibitors, gold, beta-blockers, and metronidazole) closely mimic classic PR. However, the drug-induced form typically lacks the single large ''herald patch'' that precedes the main eruption, shows more confluent and inflammatory lesions, and is much more persistent, resolving slowly only after the offending drug is discontinued.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a history of stable, mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with bipolar disorder and started on Lithium. Two weeks later, they present with an explosive, severe flare of psoriasis, with thick, silvery plaques now covering 40% of their BSA. Which of the following lists represents the ''big four'' systemic drugs most famous for flaring or worsening psoriasis?', '["Isotretinoin, Spironolactone, Cyclosporine, and Prednisone", "Statins, Fibrates, Metformin, and Lisinopril", "Penicillins, Cephalosporins, Macrolides, and Sulfonamides", "Lithium, Beta-blockers, Antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), and NSAIDs", "Amlodipine, Warfarin, Heparin, and Aspirin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Systemic drugs are common triggers for the onset or severe exacerbation of psoriasis. The classic, high-yield ''big four'' culprits are Lithium (bipolar therapy), Beta-blockers (cardiovascular therapy), Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine/chloroquine), and NSAIDs. Systemic steroid withdrawal can also precipitate a life-threatening rebound flare of generalized pustular psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Immediately after receiving an intramuscular (IM) injection of Penicillin G benzathine in the upper outer quadrant of the buttock, a 19-year-old woman develops excruciating, burning pain and livedo-like, blue-gray discoloration around the injection site. Within 48 hours, this area develops deep, necrotic sloughing of the skin and underlying gluteal muscle. What is this clinical entity?', '["Arthus reaction from vaccine immune complex deposition", "Warfarin-induced skin necrosis", "Nicolau Syndrome (Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa)", "An IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction", "Necrotizing fasciitis from bacterial contamination"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nicolau Syndrome (also known as Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa) is a rare, devastating non-allergic vascular complication of intramuscular injections. It occurs due to accidental intra-arterial injection, peri-arterial injection, or drug-induced vasospasm/thrombosis. It presents with immediate severe pain, retiform cyanosis, and subsequent deep tissue necrosis of the skin, fat, and muscle.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing chemotherapy for leukemia is treated with G-CSF (Granulocyte Colony-Stimulating Factor) to manage neutropenia. Three days later, they develop a high fever (39.5 C) and several painful, bright red, ''succulent'' or ''juicy'' plaques on their neck and upper back. Biopsy reveals a massive, dense neutrophilic infiltrate in the upper dermis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Sweet''s Syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis)", "Erysipelas", "Pyoderma Gangrenosum", "AGEP", "DRESS syndrome"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sweet''s syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis) is characterized by high fever, leukocytosis, and tender, erythematous plaques that are clinically described as ''juicy'' or ''succulent'' due to profound upper dermal edema. Histology shows a dense dermal neutrophilic infiltrate without vasculitis. G-CSF is a classic drug trigger because it directly stimulates neutrophil production.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old man being treated with Paclitaxel for breast cancer develops bilateral, woody thickening and hardening of the skin of their lower legs and forearms. Physical exam shows marked induration, but there is an absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon or nail fold capillary changes, and systemic organ involvement is absent. ANA testing is negative. What is the diagnosis?', '["Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma)", "Stasis dermatitis", "Eosinophilic Fasciitis", "Taxane-induced scleroderma-like changes", "Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Taxanes (Paclitaxel, Docetaxel) can induce scleroderma-like skin changes (induration, woody thickening) typically localized to the extremities. The key clinical clues to distinguish this drug-induced form from idiopathic Systemic Sclerosis are the absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon, normal nail fold capillaries, negative autoantibodies (ANA/Scl-70), and lack of visceral sclerosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old man taking Vemurafenib (a BRAF inhibitor) for metastatic melanoma develops several fast-growing, dome-shaped nodules with central keratinous craters on their arms within 3 weeks of starting the drug. Biopsy of one lesion shows a well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma/keratoacanthoma (SCC/KA). What is the mechanism driving these secondary skin malignancies?', '["Upregulation of the Hedgehog pathway in follicular basal cells", "Paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do NOT have the BRAF mutation", "A metastatic spread of the primary melanoma to the skin", "Systemic immunosuppression leading to explosive reactivation of HPV-16", "Direct mutagenic damage to epidermal stem cell DNA by the drug molecule"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Vemurafenib and other BRAF inhibitors cause paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal cells that do not harbor the BRAF V600E mutation. The drug binds to wild-type BRAF, causing it to homodimerize and hyper-activate downstream MEK/ERK signaling, driving rapid keratinocyte proliferation and leading to eruptive Keratoacanthomas and low-grade Squamous Cell Carcinomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 26-year-old girl on Sorafenib (a multi-kinase inhibitor) for renal cell carcinoma develops thick, painful, yellow calluses surrounded by a bright red halo on the heels and lateral aspects of their feet. How does this Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) clinically differ from Capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS)?', '["There is no clinical difference between the two reactions", "HFSR is completely painless and resolves within 24 hours of starting the drug", "HFSR presents as localized hyperkeratosis and thick calluses on pressure points, whereas classic HFS is characterized by diffuse, symmetric erythema and peeling", "HFSR strictly affects the fingernails and spares the skin", "Classic HFS is caused by direct exposure to sunlight"]'::jsonb, 2, 'While both affect the hands/feet, Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) is clinically distinct from capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS). HFSR is characterized by localized, hyperkeratotic, thick yellow plaques and blisters on high-friction pressure points (heels/lateral sole), while classic HFS (capecitabine) presents as diffuse, confluent erythema, edema, and desquamation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old female with chronic renal insufficiency receives intravenous iodinated contrast for a CT scan. Two days later, they develop painful, rapidly expanding, pustular, vegetating plaques on their face and lower extremities. Pustule culture is negative. Biopsy shows massive epidermal hyperplasia (pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia) and dense neutrophilic microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pyoderma Gangrenosum", "Iododerma (Halogenoderma)", "Blastomycosis", "Sweet''s syndrome", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Halogenodermas (Iododerma and Bromoderma) are rare cutaneous toxic accumulations of halogens, most commonly occurring in patients with impaired renal clearance. Clinically, they present as highly inflammatory, pustular, vegetating plaques that can closely mimic Pyoderma Gangrenosum, deep fungal infections (like Blastomycosis), or Squamous Cell Carcinoma. Histology shows marked pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old boy is brought to the emergency department with a painful rash, high fever, and severe eye soreness that began 2 weeks after starting Allopurinol. Physical exam reveals confluent, atypical targetoid macules with flaccid blisters and sloughing covering approximately 8% of the total body surface area (BSA). Two mucosal sites are severely involved. Skin biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis with minimal dermal inflammation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS), defined by fever, mucosal involvement of $\ge 2$ sites, targetoid macules, and epidermal detachment affecting <10% of the body surface area (BSA). Biopsy characteristically shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis (apoptosis of keratinocytes) driven by cytotoxic T-cells and granulysin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old female on Phenytoin for seizures presents with fever and widespread skin tenderness. Within 24 hours, the skin begins to peel off in large sheets. Physical examination shows dusky erythema progressing to confluent blisters. Gentle lateral pressure on the erythematous skin causes the epidermis to slide off the dermis (positive Nikolsky sign). The epidermal detachment is estimated at 35% of the total body surface area. What is the primary cause of mortality in this condition?', '["High-output cardiac failure and hypothermia", "Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "Hepatocellular failure and acute encephalopathy", "Infection, sepsis, and multi-organ failure", "Acute kidney injury from severe myoglobinuria"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN), characterized by epidermal detachment of >30% BSA and a positive Nikolsky sign. The loss of the skin barrier leads to massive fluid shifts, hypothermia, and high-output heart failure, but the leading cause of death in these patients is infection (sepsis) and subsequent multi-organ failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old woman presents with a painful, blistering rash and fever. They recently started Sulfasalazine for inflammatory bowel disease. Physical examination reveals atypical targetoid plaques on the trunk and extremities, with flaccid bullae that slide off under lateral pressure. The surface area of epidermal detachment is measured at exactly 18% of the total BSA. What is the clinical category of this patient''s drug reaction?', '["Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "DRESS syndrome"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The SJS/TEN spectrum is categorized by the percentage of body surface area showing epidermal detachment: SJS involves <10% BSA detachment; SJS/TEN Overlap involves 10% to 30% BSA detachment; and TEN involves >30% BSA detachment. At 18% detachment, this patient falls into the SJS/TEN Overlap category.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old woman of East Asian descent is about to start Carbamazepine for neuropathic pain. The clinician orders genetic screening for the HLA-B*1502 allele before prescribing. What is the clinical significance of this allele?', '["It correlates with an increased risk of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia", "It carries an extremely high risk of Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in Asian populations", "It is associated with rapid metabolic clearance of Carbamazepine, making it ineffective", "It indicates an increased risk of severe, irreversible agranulocytosis", "It is a marker for drug-induced phototoxicity and exaggerated sunburns"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The HLA-B*1502 allele is strongly associated with an increased risk of developing Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in individuals of East Asian and South-East Asian descent. Screening is mandatory or highly recommended prior to starting Carbamazepine in these high-risk populations.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old girl with Han Chinese ancestry is diagnosed with gout and is about to start Allopurinol therapy. Which HLA allele is strongly associated with Allopurinol-induced Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) in this population?', '["HLA-DRB1*0401", "HLA-B*5701", "HLA-B*5801", "HLA-B*2705", "HLA-B*1502"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The HLA-B*5801 allele is a potent genetic risk factor for Allopurinol-induced severe cutaneous adverse reactions, including SJS and TEN, particularly in Han Chinese, Thai, and Korean populations. HLA-B*1502 is associated with carbamazepine, and HLA-B*5701 is linked to abacavir hypersensitivity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old boy with Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) involving 45% BSA is admitted to the hospital. Which of the following clinical settings is the most appropriate for the management of this patient?', '["An outpatient clinic with daily nurse home visits", "A clean private room in a general medical ward", "A specialized Burn Unit or Intensive Care Unit (ICU)", "An infectious disease isolation room", "A standard dermatology inpatient ward"]'::jsonb, 2, 'TEN is a life-threatening dermatologic emergency. Due to extensive skin loss, patients require meticulous sterile wound care, specialized non-adherent dressings, tight fluid resuscitation, and temperature regulation identical to severe burn victims. Management in a specialized Burn Unit or intensive care setting significantly reduces mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old boy began taking Carbamazepine 4 weeks ago. They now present with a high fever (39.0 C), a widespread red skin eruption, and severe facial and periorbital swelling. Physical exam reveals confluent morbilliform plaques on the trunk, palpable lymphadenopathy in the neck and axillae, and moderate tender hepatomegaly. Lab work shows a WBC of 16,500/mm3 with 18% eosinophils (absolute eosinophil count 2,970/mm3) and AST/ALT elevated at 4 times the upper limit of normal. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS) syndrome", "Serum Sickness", "Exanthematous (Morbilliform) Drug Eruption", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'DRESS syndrome (also known as Drug-Induced Hypersensitivity Syndrome) is a severe, life-threatening drug reaction characterized by a long latency period (2-8 weeks), high fever, a diffuse erythematous eruption, facial/periorbital edema, lymphadenopathy, hematologic abnormalities (eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytosis), and visceral organ involvement—most commonly hepatitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is diagnosed with DRESS syndrome after starting Phenytoin 6 weeks ago. Aside from stopping the offending drug, what virus reactivation is frequently associated with the pathogenesis and flare-ups of this systemic drug hypersensitivity?', '["Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Hepatitis C Virus (HCV)", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'DRESS syndrome is strongly associated with the sequential reactivation of latent herpesviruses, most notably Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6), as well as HHV-7, EBV, and CMV. This viral reactivation is thought to drive the prolonged, recurrent course of the disease even after the causative drug has been completely cleared.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old woman is started on Cephalexin for a suspected soft tissue infection. Forty-eight hours later, they develop a high fever (39.2 C) and a burning rash that started in the groin and axillae. Physical exam reveals diffuse, bright erythema studded with hundreds of non-follicular, sterile, pinpoint pustules. Lab work shows a marked leukocytosis with neutrophilia (WBC 19,000/mm3). Pustule cultures are sterile. What is the diagnosis?', '["Generalized Pustular Psoriasis (Von Zumbusch)", "Disseminated candidiasis", "DRESS syndrome", "Subcorneal Pustular Dermatosis", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'AGEP is a rare, severe cutaneous reaction characterized by the rapid onset (typically <48 hours after drug exposure) of high fever and hundreds of small, sterile, non-follicular pustules arising on a background of edematous erythema, classically starting in flexural folds (groin/axillae). Beta-lactams and macrolides are the most common triggers. Leukocytosis with marked neutrophilia is characteristic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old male presents with a dark, round spot on their left forearm. They note that every time they take Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection, the exact same spot becomes red, swollen, and itchy, occasionally forming a small central blister. Once it heals, a persistent dusky brown patch remains. What is the mechanism of this ''fixing'' to the same anatomic site?', '["Localized accumulation of IgE molecules on dermal mast cells", "Deposition of circulating IgG immune complexes in the local capillary bed", "Slow lymphatic clearance of the drug from that specific site", "Localized somatic mutation in the keratinocytes of that area", "Persistence of resident memory CD8+ T-cells in the affected epidermis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Fixed Drug Eruption (FDE) is characterized by recurrent erythematous plaques that appear in the exact same localized site (skin or mucosa) upon re-exposure to the offending drug. This site-specific memory is mediated by resident memory CD8+ T-cells that remain quiescent in the basal epidermis of the affected plaque and rapidly degranulate upon systemic re-exposure to the drug.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old man presents with a single, highly circumscribed, dusky-red, itchy patch on their glans penis that developed 24 hours after taking a medication. Which of the following drugs is the most common trigger for a fixed drug eruption (FDE) affecting the genitalia?', '["Metformin", "Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (Sulfonamides)", "Atorvastatin", "Aspirin", "Amlodipine"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While FDE can be triggered by many drugs (including NSAIDs and tetracyclines), sulfonamides—most notably Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole—are the most common triggers for fixed drug eruptions located on the genitalia (glans penis or labia).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old girl is started on Warfarin for a new deep vein thrombosis. Due to an oversight, no Heparin bridge is administered. On day 3 of therapy, the patient develops severe pain and dark purple, well-demarcated patches on their breasts and thighs. Within 24 hours, these lesions progress to necrotic black eschars. What is the primary molecular cause of this reaction?', '["Direct toxic necrosis of adipocytes caused by accumulation of the warfarin molecule", "A rapid drop in Protein C levels (due to its short half-life), causing a transient hypercoagulable state and microthrombosis", "A drop in antithrombin III levels leading to arterial spasm", "An IgE-mediated anaphylactoid reaction causing localized vasculitis", "Localized vasoconstriction from paradoxical alpha-adrenergic stimulation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Warfarin-induced skin necrosis occurs early in therapy (typically days 3-5) when warfarin is initiated without a heparin bridge, especially in patients with hereditary Protein C or S deficiency. Protein C has a very short half-life (approx 8 hours) and falls rapidly, while procoagulant factors II, IX, and X fall slowly. This creates a transient, severe hypercoagulable state leading to microthrombosis in the capillaries supplying fat-rich areas (breasts, thighs, buttocks).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient on subcutaneous Unfractionated Heparin therapy for a pulmonary embolism develops painful, red-to-purple patches at their abdominal injection sites 7 days into therapy, which quickly progress to localized skin necrosis. Lab work reveals a platelet count that has dropped from 280,000/mm3 to 90,000/mm3. What is the target of the autoantibodies driving this clinical presentation?', '["Protein C and Protein S", "The G-protein coupled thrombin receptor", "Type XVII Collagen (BP180) in the basement membrane", "Desmoglein 1 and Desmoglein 3 in the epidermis", "Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT) presenting with localized heparin-induced skin necrosis at the injection sites. HIT is mediated by IgG autoantibodies targeting Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes, which bind and activate platelets, causing both thrombocytopenia (clearance of platelets) and a severe, life-threatening prothrombotic state.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient develops heparin-induced skin necrosis and thrombocytopenia (HIT). Which of the following is the most critical and immediate next step in management?', '["Discontinue all heparin products immediately and initiate a non-heparin anticoagulant (e.g., Argatroban)", "Transfuse 2 units of platelets and continue subcutaneous heparin", "Increase the heparin dose to overcome the antibody-mediated clearance", "Apply high-potency topical steroids to the necrotic injection sites", "Stop heparin, administer Vitamin K, and start oral warfarin immediately"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When HIT is suspected or confirmed (due to a platelet drop >50% and/or skin necrosis), the immediate action must be to stop all heparin products (including low-molecular-weight heparin and heparin flushes) and start an alternative, non-heparin rapid anticoagulant (e.g., a direct thrombin inhibitor like Argatroban or Bivalirudin) to prevent catastrophic thromboembolism. Warfarin is contraindicated in active HIT as it can precipitate gangrene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old girl being treated with Hydralazine for severe hypertension presents with joint pain, fever, and a mild malar-like rash on their face. Lab results show a positive ANA (1:640) and highly elevated Anti-Histone antibodies. Anti-dsDNA and Anti-Smith antibodies are negative. Urinalysis and serum creatinine are normal. What is the clinical significance of anti-histone antibodies in this patient?', '["They are highly sensitive (approx 95%) for drug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE)", "They are highly specific for idiopathic systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)", "They are caused by a cross-reaction with the hydralazine molecule itself", "They indicate severe, silent lupus nephritis requiring renal biopsy", "They represent a high risk of transition to Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Anti-histone antibodies are highly sensitive (present in 90-95% of cases) for Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE). Common triggers include Hydralazine, Procainamide, and Isoniazid. Unlike idiopathic SLE, DILE rarely involves the kidneys or central nervous system, and anti-dsDNA or anti-Smith antibodies are usually absent.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old boy being treated with D-Penicillamine for Wilson''s disease develops flaccid blisters on their chest and upper back, along with painful sores in their mouth. Physical exam shows a positive Nikolsky sign. A skin biopsy reveals intraepidermal acantholysis. Direct Immunofluorescence (DIF) demonstrates intercellular IgG deposition in a ''fishnet'' pattern throughout the epidermis. What is the target of the autoantibodies in this drug-induced condition?', '["Type VII Collagen in the anchoring fibrils", "Desmogleins (Dsg1 and Dsg3)", "BP180 (Type XVII Collagen) and BP230", "Laminin-332 in the lamina lucida", "Integrin alpha-6 beta-4 in the hemidesmosomes"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has Drug-Induced Pemphigus, characteristically triggered by thiol (sulfhydryl) group-containing drugs like D-Penicillamine, Captopril, and Gold salts. Thiol groups can biochemically disrupt desmosomes or act as haptens. The autoantibodies target desmoglein 1 and/or 3, leading to flaccid blisters, mucosal lesions, acantholysis, and a ''fishnet'' DIF pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An 82-year-old man on Linagliptin (a DPP-4 inhibitor) for type 2 diabetes presents with an intensely pruritic rash and several large, firm, tense blisters on their abdomen and lower extremities. Nikolsky sign is negative. A biopsy shows a subepidermal split with numerous eosinophils. DIF demonstrates a linear band of IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug-induced Pemphigus Vulgaris", "Pseudoporphyria", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita", "Drug-induced Bullous Pemphigoid", "Linear IgA Bullous Dermatosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Drug-induced Bullous Pemphigoid (BP), characteristically presenting with tense subepidermal blisters and severe pruritus. In recent years, Dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitors (gliptins) have been recognized as a major drug trigger for BP, particularly in elderly patients. Loop diuretics (furosemide) are another classic trigger.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old boy being treated with a Beta-blocker for hypertension presents with an extremely itchy rash on their forearms and shins. Physical examination reveals symmetric, flat-topped, polygonal, purple papules. Fine white lines (Wickham striae) are visible on the surface under magnification. How does this drug-induced lichenoid eruption typically differ from classic, idiopathic Lichen Planus?', '["It is often more generalized, symmetric, and frequently lacks mucosal involvement or classic Wickham striae", "It is highly infectious and spreads to close contacts", "It selectively targets the face and spares the extremities", "It presents with large, flaccid blisters and a positive Nikolsky sign", "It is completely non-pruritic and does not show interface dermatitis on biopsy"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Lichenoid drug eruptions (triggered by beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, thiazides, and gold) clinically mimic Lichen Planus. However, they typically differ by being more generalized, symmetric, and having a eczematous or psoriasiform appearance, while frequently sparing the oral mucosa and showing a slower resolution after drug withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old boy presents with an explosive onset of acne-like bumps on their chest and upper back. They deny having any acne as a teenager. Physical exam reveals dozens of monomorphic (all at the same stage of development) erythematous inflammatory papules and pustules. No blackheads or whiteheads (comedones) are present. The patient was recently treated with a high-dose oral prednisone taper. What is the diagnosis?', '["Hot tub folliculitis", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Acne vulgaris (papulopustular subtype)", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Steroid acne is an acneiform drug eruption caused by systemic or high-potency topical corticosteroids. Clinically, it is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules on the trunk and shoulders. Unlike acne vulgaris, steroid acne lacks comedones (blackheads/whiteheads) and the lesions are all at the identical stage of development.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient receiving a rapid intravenous infusion of Vancomycin suddenly develops severe flushing, pruritus, and a bright red rash over their face, neck, and upper torso. Their heart rate is 115/min and blood pressure is 98/60 mmHg. There is no wheezing, lip swelling, or hives. What is the primary mechanism of this reaction?', '["T-cell mediated cytotoxic destruction of endothelial cells", "Direct toxic effect of the vancomycin molecule on vascular smooth muscle", "IgE-mediated Type I hypersensitivity reaction causing systemic anaphylaxis", "Direct, non-immunologic histamine release from mast cells triggered by the rapid drug infusion", "Type III hypersensitivity with deposition of IgA immune complexes in dermal vessels"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is Red Man Syndrome, a pseudo-allergic (anaphylactoid) reaction caused by a rapid infusion of intravenous Vancomycin. The drug directly degranulates mast cells and basophils in a non-immunologic fashion, releasing histamine. It is not a true IgE-mediated allergy. It can be prevented by slowing the infusion rate (to over 60 minutes) and pre-treating with antihistamines.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old child presents with a widespread, hive-like rash, severe joint pain and swelling of the ankles, and a low-grade fever that began 10 days after starting Cefaclor for an ear infection. Lab work shows a normal platelet count and no proteinuria. What is the diagnosis?', '["Acute Rheumatic Fever", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome", "Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR)", "Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura (IgA Vasculitis)", "True Serum Sickness"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR) is a benign, transient reaction that classically occurs in children 1-3 weeks after starting certain drugs, most famously Cefaclor, as well as penicillins and sulfonamides. It mimics true serum sickness with fever, arthralgias, and urticarial plaques, but crucially LACKS circulating immune complexes, complement depletion, vasculitis, and renal damage (proteinuria).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old man being treated with high-dose Capecitabine for metastatic colorectal cancer presents with painful redness, swelling, and tingling on their palms and soles. Physical examination reveals symmetric, bright erythema and edema of the palmar and plantar surfaces, with early desquamation. What is the clinical term for this condition?', '["Fixed Drug Eruption", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (Hand-Foot Syndrome)", "Contact Dermatitis", "Hand-Foot-and-Mouth Disease"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (also known as Hand-Foot Syndrome) is a common cutaneous side effect of several chemotherapy agents, most classically Capecitabine, 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU), and Cytarabine. It is caused by drug leaking from small capillaries into the thick skin of the palms and soles, leading to direct toxicity, pain, symmetric erythema, swelling, and peeling.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old boy being treated with Cetuximab (an EGFR inhibitor) for lung cancer develops an itchy, acne-like rash consisting of papules and pustules on their face, neck, and upper chest. Comedones are completely absent. What is the clinical significance of this rash?', '["It is a positive prognostic indicator (the development and severity of the rash correlate with a favorable tumor response)", "It is a sign of tumor progression and therapeutic failure", "It represents a deep follicular bacterial infection requiring intravenous antibiotics", "It is caused by secondary adrenal insufficiency from the chemotherapy", "It indicates a life-threatening drug allergy and the Cetuximab must be stopped permanently"]'::jsonb, 0, 'EGFR inhibitor-induced acneiform (papulopustular) eruption is a highly common and expected class-effect of drugs like Cetuximab, Erlotinib, and Panitumumab. Counter-intuitively, the development and severity of this rash act as a positive surrogate marker for drug efficacy, correlating with improved tumor response and patient survival. It is managed with topical/oral antibiotics (doxycycline) and steroids without stopping the chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old man who has been taking Minocycline for severe acne for 3 years presents with a new, muddy, blue-gray discoloration on their shins and the mucosal surface of their lower gums. A skin biopsy reveals iron-containing and melanin-containing pigment deposits within the dermis. What is the subtype and characteristic appearance of this minocycline-induced hyperpigmentation?', '["Slate-gray facial pigmentation induced by drug-induced lipofuscin", "Type II Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, affecting normal skin of the shins and forearms, as well as gums", "Argiria, caused by silver deposition in the eccrine glands", "Type I Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, limited strictly to sites of prior inflammation and scarring", "Type III Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, presenting as a diffuse muddy-brown tan in sun-exposed areas"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Minocycline can induce three distinct types of skin hyperpigmentation: Type I occurs strictly in scars and areas of prior inflammation (blue-black pigment); Type II occurs on normal skin, classically the shins, forearms, and gums (slate-blue/gray pigment containing iron and melanin); and Type III is a diffuse, muddy-brown hyperpigmentation in sun-exposed areas (pure melanin).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old girl with a history of refractory cardiac arrhythmias has been taking Amiodarone for 5 years. They present to the clinic complaining of a blue-gray discoloration of their face, nose, and ears. Biopsy of the affected skin reveals lipofuscin-like granules within dermal macrophages. What is the key environmental trigger required for the development of this specific pigmentation?', '["Exposure to cold weather (Raynaud-like)", "Chronic mechanical friction", "Frequent hot water baths", "High dietary intake of vitamin C", "Ultraviolet (UV) light exposure (sunlight)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Amiodarone-induced hyperpigmentation is a classic side effect occurring in patients on long-term, high-dose amiodarone therapy. It presents as a slate-gray to blue-violet discoloration strictly in sun-exposed areas (face, nose, ears). Ultraviolet (UV) light is required to trigger the deposition of a drug-lipid complex (lipofuscin) inside dermal macrophages.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old male with systemic lupus erythematosus has been taking Hydroxychloroquine for 6 years. During a routine skin check, the dermatologist notes well-demarcated, slate-gray patches on the patient''s bilateral shins and a bluish-gray discoloration of the hard palate. What is the prognosis and clinical course of this pigmentation?', '["It indicates an immediate risk of acute hepatic failure", "It is completely permanent and will continue to spread even if the drug is discontinued", "It is highly infectious and can be transmitted to others", "It resolves spontaneously within 2 weeks of stopping the drug", "It is slowly reversible over months to years after stopping the hydroxychloroquine, though some areas may persist"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Skin and mucosal hyperpigmentation occurs in up to 25% of patients on long-term antimalarials (Chloroquine/Hydroxychloroquine). Classic sites are the shins (pretibial area), the hard palate, and the nail beds. The pigmentation is slowly reversible over many months or years after drug cessation, though some localized areas may remain permanently.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 32-year-old woman undergoing chemotherapy for Hodgkin''s lymphoma develops intensely pruritic, linear, criss-crossing red streaks on her back and chest that resemble ''whip marks''. She admits to scratching her skin due to dryness. Which of the following chemotherapy agents is the classic cause of this flagellate erythema?', '["Cyclophosphamide", "Doxorubicin", "5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)", "Paclitaxel", "Bleomycin"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Flagellate erythema is a virtually pathognomonic cutaneous toxicity associated with Bleomycin therapy. It presents as highly characteristic, linear, criss-crossing erythematous streaks that look like ''whip marks'' on the back and trunk. It represents a Koebner-like response where minor scratching or friction concentrates the drug in the local skin vessels.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old male presents with progressive, painless, firm, fibrous swelling of their gums that has begun to cover the crowns of their teeth. They have a history of epilepsy. Which of the following triads of medications represents the classic causes of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia?', '["Phenytoin, Cyclosporine, and Nifedipine", "Metformin, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Prednisone, Isotretinoin, and Spironolactone", "Penicillin, Doxycycline, and Cephalexin", "Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Drug-induced gingival hyperplasia is a classic side effect characterized by fibrous overgrowth of the gingival papillae. The three classic, high-yield culprits are Phenytoin (anti-epileptic), Cyclosporine (immunosuppressant), and Calcium Channel Blockers (especially Nifedipine, but also Amlodipine/Diltiazem).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male taking Naproxen for osteoarthritic pain presents with skin fragility and multiple small, tense vesicles and erosions on the dorsum of their hands. Biopsy reveals a subepidermal blister with minimal inflammatory cell infiltrate. Laboratory workup demonstrates normal levels of urinary and fecal porphyrins, ruling out Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT). What is the diagnosis?', '["Bullous Pemphigoid", "Allergic Contact Dermatitis", "Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT)", "Pseudoporphyria", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pseudoporphyria is a drug-induced condition that clinically and histologically mimics Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT), presenting with skin fragility, blistering, and scarring on sun-exposed hands. Crucially, porphyrin levels in the blood, urine, and stool are completely normal in pseudoporphyria. NSAIDs (most famously Naproxen) are the leading cause.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 25-year-old female presents with multiple extremely painful, warm, red, deep-seated nodules on her bilateral shins that have been present for 2 weeks. She reports starting oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) 2 months ago. A biopsy reveals septal panniculitis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Weber-Christian disease", "Polyarteritis Nodosa (PAN)", "Erythema Induratum (Bazin''s disease)", "Sweet''s syndrome", "Erythema Nodosum (EN)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Erythema Nodosum (EN) is a classic form of panniculitis (inflammation of subcutaneous fat) that presents as tender, warm, erythematous nodules on the pretibial shins. It represents a hypersensitivity reaction to various triggers, including infections (strep, TB) and medications—most commonly oral contraceptives (OCPs) and sulfonamides. Histology classically shows septal panniculitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old child presents with high fever, severe mucosal crusting and ulceration of the lips and mouth, and conjunctival redness with purulent discharge. Physical exam reveals less than 1% BSA of skin detachment with only a few targetoid macules on the limbs. The child was treated for ''walking pneumonia'' 1 week ago. What is the diagnosis?', '["Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS)", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Primary Herpes Simplex Gingivostomatitis", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS) is triggered by Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection, most commonly in children and young adults. It is characterized by severe mucositis involving $\ge 2$ mucosal sites (typically oral and ocular) with minimal or absent cutaneous lesions (<1% BSA skin involvement). It mimics SJS but has a distinct pathogenesis and carries a significantly better prognosis with lower mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old patient presents with painful, dark purple, necrotic, star-shaped (retiform) patches on their bilateral earlobes, cheeks, and lower extremities. Lab work reveals a severe neutropenia (WBC 1,200/mm3 with an absolute neutrophil count of 350/mm3). A urine drug screen is positive for Cocaine. What is the causative agent responsible for this patient''s necrotic ear skin and bone marrow suppression?', '["A contamination of cocaine with Streptococcus pyogenes", "An IgE-mediated drug allergy to benzoylecgonine", "Direct vasoconstrictive toxicity of the cocaine molecule itself", "Levamisole (a veterinary dewormer used as a cocaine cutting agent)", "Amiodarone-induced photo-vasculitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Levamisole-induced retiform purpura. Levamisole is a veterinary anthelmintic commonly used as an adulterant (cutting agent) in illicit cocaine. It triggers a unique, severe autoimmune vasculitis characterized by painful, retiform (star-shaped) purpura with central necrosis, characteristically involving the ears/earlobes and face. It is also a potent bone marrow suppressant, leading to severe neutropenia/agranulocytosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old girl being treated with Hydralazine for 8 years presents with fever, joint pain, weight loss, and palpable purple spots (purpura) on their lower legs. Lab results show a positive ANA, dual positivity for p-ANCA (MPO) and c-ANCA (PR3) antibodies, and urinalysis reveals proteinuria and hematuria with red blood cell casts. What is the diagnosis?', '["SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA)", "Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE)", "Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura", "Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis is a severe necrotizing vasculitis classically triggered by long-term use of Hydralazine, Propylthiouracil (PTU), or Methimazole. Unlike simple drug-induced lupus (DILE), drug-induced ANCA vasculitis features high titers of p-ANCA (MPO) and PR3, palpable purpura, and significant internal organ involvement—most notably rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (renal failure).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old female on Captopril (an ACE inhibitor) develops a widespread scaly rash on their trunk. They report a large, oval, scaly ''herald patch'' appeared on their chest first, followed by smaller oval plaques along the skin tension lines on their back in a ''Christmas tree'' distribution. How does this drug-induced Pityriasis Rosea-like eruption typically differ from idiopathic Pityriasis Rosea?', '["It often lacks a distinct initial herald patch and takes much longer to resolve after stopping the drug", "It is accompanied by severe kidney damage and proteinuria", "It presents with deep, tense bullae and a positive Nikolsky sign", "It is highly contagious and spreads to children via respiratory droplets", "It strictly affects the face and spares the trunk"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pityriasis Rosea-like drug eruptions (triggered by ACE inhibitors, gold, beta-blockers, and metronidazole) closely mimic classic PR. However, the drug-induced form typically lacks the single large ''herald patch'' that precedes the main eruption, shows more confluent and inflammatory lesions, and is much more persistent, resolving slowly only after the offending drug is discontinued.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a history of stable, mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with bipolar disorder and started on Lithium. Two weeks later, they present with an explosive, severe flare of psoriasis, with thick, silvery plaques now covering 40% of their BSA. Which of the following lists represents the ''big four'' systemic drugs most famous for flaring or worsening psoriasis?', '["Penicillins, Cephalosporins, Macrolides, and Sulfonamides", "Amlodipine, Warfarin, Heparin, and Aspirin", "Isotretinoin, Spironolactone, Cyclosporine, and Prednisone", "Statins, Fibrates, Metformin, and Lisinopril", "Lithium, Beta-blockers, Antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), and NSAIDs"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Systemic drugs are common triggers for the onset or severe exacerbation of psoriasis. The classic, high-yield ''big four'' culprits are Lithium (bipolar therapy), Beta-blockers (cardiovascular therapy), Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine/chloroquine), and NSAIDs. Systemic steroid withdrawal can also precipitate a life-threatening rebound flare of generalized pustular psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Immediately after receiving an intramuscular (IM) injection of Penicillin G benzathine in the upper outer quadrant of the buttock, a 78-year-old boy develops excruciating, burning pain and livedo-like, blue-gray discoloration around the injection site. Within 48 hours, this area develops deep, necrotic sloughing of the skin and underlying gluteal muscle. What is this clinical entity?', '["An IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction", "Necrotizing fasciitis from bacterial contamination", "Arthus reaction from vaccine immune complex deposition", "Warfarin-induced skin necrosis", "Nicolau Syndrome (Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Nicolau Syndrome (also known as Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa) is a rare, devastating non-allergic vascular complication of intramuscular injections. It occurs due to accidental intra-arterial injection, peri-arterial injection, or drug-induced vasospasm/thrombosis. It presents with immediate severe pain, retiform cyanosis, and subsequent deep tissue necrosis of the skin, fat, and muscle.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing chemotherapy for leukemia is treated with G-CSF (Granulocyte Colony-Stimulating Factor) to manage neutropenia. Three days later, they develop a high fever (39.5 C) and several painful, bright red, ''succulent'' or ''juicy'' plaques on their neck and upper back. Biopsy reveals a massive, dense neutrophilic infiltrate in the upper dermis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["AGEP", "DRESS syndrome", "Erysipelas", "Sweet''s Syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis)", "Pyoderma Gangrenosum"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Sweet''s syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis) is characterized by high fever, leukocytosis, and tender, erythematous plaques that are clinically described as ''juicy'' or ''succulent'' due to profound upper dermal edema. Histology shows a dense dermal neutrophilic infiltrate without vasculitis. G-CSF is a classic drug trigger because it directly stimulates neutrophil production.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old girl being treated with Paclitaxel for breast cancer develops bilateral, woody thickening and hardening of the skin of their lower legs and forearms. Physical exam shows marked induration, but there is an absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon or nail fold capillary changes, and systemic organ involvement is absent. ANA testing is negative. What is the diagnosis?', '["Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma)", "Taxane-induced scleroderma-like changes", "Stasis dermatitis", "Eosinophilic Fasciitis", "Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Taxanes (Paclitaxel, Docetaxel) can induce scleroderma-like skin changes (induration, woody thickening) typically localized to the extremities. The key clinical clues to distinguish this drug-induced form from idiopathic Systemic Sclerosis are the absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon, normal nail fold capillaries, negative autoantibodies (ANA/Scl-70), and lack of visceral sclerosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old girl taking Vemurafenib (a BRAF inhibitor) for metastatic melanoma develops several fast-growing, dome-shaped nodules with central keratinous craters on their arms within 3 weeks of starting the drug. Biopsy of one lesion shows a well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma/keratoacanthoma (SCC/KA). What is the mechanism driving these secondary skin malignancies?', '["Systemic immunosuppression leading to explosive reactivation of HPV-16", "A metastatic spread of the primary melanoma to the skin", "Direct mutagenic damage to epidermal stem cell DNA by the drug molecule", "Paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do NOT have the BRAF mutation", "Upregulation of the Hedgehog pathway in follicular basal cells"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Vemurafenib and other BRAF inhibitors cause paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal cells that do not harbor the BRAF V600E mutation. The drug binds to wild-type BRAF, causing it to homodimerize and hyper-activate downstream MEK/ERK signaling, driving rapid keratinocyte proliferation and leading to eruptive Keratoacanthomas and low-grade Squamous Cell Carcinomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 38-year-old female on Sorafenib (a multi-kinase inhibitor) for renal cell carcinoma develops thick, painful, yellow calluses surrounded by a bright red halo on the heels and lateral aspects of their feet. How does this Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) clinically differ from Capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS)?', '["HFSR presents as localized hyperkeratosis and thick calluses on pressure points, whereas classic HFS is characterized by diffuse, symmetric erythema and peeling", "Classic HFS is caused by direct exposure to sunlight", "HFSR is completely painless and resolves within 24 hours of starting the drug", "There is no clinical difference between the two reactions", "HFSR strictly affects the fingernails and spares the skin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'While both affect the hands/feet, Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) is clinically distinct from capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS). HFSR is characterized by localized, hyperkeratotic, thick yellow plaques and blisters on high-friction pressure points (heels/lateral sole), while classic HFS (capecitabine) presents as diffuse, confluent erythema, edema, and desquamation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old woman with chronic renal insufficiency receives intravenous iodinated contrast for a CT scan. Two days later, they develop painful, rapidly expanding, pustular, vegetating plaques on their face and lower extremities. Pustule culture is negative. Biopsy shows massive epidermal hyperplasia (pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia) and dense neutrophilic microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Sweet''s syndrome", "Pyoderma Gangrenosum", "Blastomycosis", "Iododerma (Halogenoderma)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Halogenodermas (Iododerma and Bromoderma) are rare cutaneous toxic accumulations of halogens, most commonly occurring in patients with impaired renal clearance. Clinically, they present as highly inflammatory, pustular, vegetating plaques that can closely mimic Pyoderma Gangrenosum, deep fungal infections (like Blastomycosis), or Squamous Cell Carcinoma. Histology shows marked pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old man is brought to the emergency department with a painful rash, high fever, and severe eye soreness that began 2 weeks after starting Allopurinol. Physical exam reveals confluent, atypical targetoid macules with flaccid blisters and sloughing covering approximately 8% of the total body surface area (BSA). Two mucosal sites are severely involved. Skin biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis with minimal dermal inflammation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Erythema Multiforme Major", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS), defined by fever, mucosal involvement of $\ge 2$ sites, targetoid macules, and epidermal detachment affecting <10% of the body surface area (BSA). Biopsy characteristically shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis (apoptosis of keratinocytes) driven by cytotoxic T-cells and granulysin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 26-year-old boy on Phenytoin for seizures presents with fever and widespread skin tenderness. Within 24 hours, the skin begins to peel off in large sheets. Physical examination shows dusky erythema progressing to confluent blisters. Gentle lateral pressure on the erythematous skin causes the epidermis to slide off the dermis (positive Nikolsky sign). The epidermal detachment is estimated at 35% of the total body surface area. What is the primary cause of mortality in this condition?', '["Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "High-output cardiac failure and hypothermia", "Infection, sepsis, and multi-organ failure", "Hepatocellular failure and acute encephalopathy", "Acute kidney injury from severe myoglobinuria"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN), characterized by epidermal detachment of >30% BSA and a positive Nikolsky sign. The loss of the skin barrier leads to massive fluid shifts, hypothermia, and high-output heart failure, but the leading cause of death in these patients is infection (sepsis) and subsequent multi-organ failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old female presents with a painful, blistering rash and fever. They recently started Sulfasalazine for inflammatory bowel disease. Physical examination reveals atypical targetoid plaques on the trunk and extremities, with flaccid bullae that slide off under lateral pressure. The surface area of epidermal detachment is measured at exactly 18% of the total BSA. What is the clinical category of this patient''s drug reaction?', '["Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "DRESS syndrome", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The SJS/TEN spectrum is categorized by the percentage of body surface area showing epidermal detachment: SJS involves <10% BSA detachment; SJS/TEN Overlap involves 10% to 30% BSA detachment; and TEN involves >30% BSA detachment. At 18% detachment, this patient falls into the SJS/TEN Overlap category.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old man of East Asian descent is about to start Carbamazepine for neuropathic pain. The clinician orders genetic screening for the HLA-B*1502 allele before prescribing. What is the clinical significance of this allele?', '["It correlates with an increased risk of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia", "It carries an extremely high risk of Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in Asian populations", "It is associated with rapid metabolic clearance of Carbamazepine, making it ineffective", "It is a marker for drug-induced phototoxicity and exaggerated sunburns", "It indicates an increased risk of severe, irreversible agranulocytosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The HLA-B*1502 allele is strongly associated with an increased risk of developing Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in individuals of East Asian and South-East Asian descent. Screening is mandatory or highly recommended prior to starting Carbamazepine in these high-risk populations.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old woman with Han Chinese ancestry is diagnosed with gout and is about to start Allopurinol therapy. Which HLA allele is strongly associated with Allopurinol-induced Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) in this population?', '["HLA-DRB1*0401", "HLA-B*5801", "HLA-B*2705", "HLA-B*1502", "HLA-B*5701"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The HLA-B*5801 allele is a potent genetic risk factor for Allopurinol-induced severe cutaneous adverse reactions, including SJS and TEN, particularly in Han Chinese, Thai, and Korean populations. HLA-B*1502 is associated with carbamazepine, and HLA-B*5701 is linked to abacavir hypersensitivity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old girl with Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) involving 45% BSA is admitted to the hospital. Which of the following clinical settings is the most appropriate for the management of this patient?', '["A standard dermatology inpatient ward", "A specialized Burn Unit or Intensive Care Unit (ICU)", "A clean private room in a general medical ward", "An outpatient clinic with daily nurse home visits", "An infectious disease isolation room"]'::jsonb, 1, 'TEN is a life-threatening dermatologic emergency. Due to extensive skin loss, patients require meticulous sterile wound care, specialized non-adherent dressings, tight fluid resuscitation, and temperature regulation identical to severe burn victims. Management in a specialized Burn Unit or intensive care setting significantly reduces mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old male began taking Carbamazepine 4 weeks ago. They now present with a high fever (39.0 C), a widespread red skin eruption, and severe facial and periorbital swelling. Physical exam reveals confluent morbilliform plaques on the trunk, palpable lymphadenopathy in the neck and axillae, and moderate tender hepatomegaly. Lab work shows a WBC of 16,500/mm3 with 18% eosinophils (absolute eosinophil count 2,970/mm3) and AST/ALT elevated at 4 times the upper limit of normal. What is the diagnosis?', '["Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS) syndrome", "Exanthematous (Morbilliform) Drug Eruption", "Serum Sickness", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'DRESS syndrome (also known as Drug-Induced Hypersensitivity Syndrome) is a severe, life-threatening drug reaction characterized by a long latency period (2-8 weeks), high fever, a diffuse erythematous eruption, facial/periorbital edema, lymphadenopathy, hematologic abnormalities (eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytosis), and visceral organ involvement—most commonly hepatitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is diagnosed with DRESS syndrome after starting Phenytoin 6 weeks ago. Aside from stopping the offending drug, what virus reactivation is frequently associated with the pathogenesis and flare-ups of this systemic drug hypersensitivity?', '["Hepatitis C Virus (HCV)", "Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'DRESS syndrome is strongly associated with the sequential reactivation of latent herpesviruses, most notably Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6), as well as HHV-7, EBV, and CMV. This viral reactivation is thought to drive the prolonged, recurrent course of the disease even after the causative drug has been completely cleared.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old boy is started on Cephalexin for a suspected soft tissue infection. Forty-eight hours later, they develop a high fever (39.2 C) and a burning rash that started in the groin and axillae. Physical exam reveals diffuse, bright erythema studded with hundreds of non-follicular, sterile, pinpoint pustules. Lab work shows a marked leukocytosis with neutrophilia (WBC 19,000/mm3). Pustule cultures are sterile. What is the diagnosis?', '["Generalized Pustular Psoriasis (Von Zumbusch)", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "Subcorneal Pustular Dermatosis", "DRESS syndrome", "Disseminated candidiasis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AGEP is a rare, severe cutaneous reaction characterized by the rapid onset (typically <48 hours after drug exposure) of high fever and hundreds of small, sterile, non-follicular pustules arising on a background of edematous erythema, classically starting in flexural folds (groin/axillae). Beta-lactams and macrolides are the most common triggers. Leukocytosis with marked neutrophilia is characteristic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old woman presents with a dark, round spot on their left forearm. They note that every time they take Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection, the exact same spot becomes red, swollen, and itchy, occasionally forming a small central blister. Once it heals, a persistent dusky brown patch remains. What is the mechanism of this ''fixing'' to the same anatomic site?', '["Localized somatic mutation in the keratinocytes of that area", "Slow lymphatic clearance of the drug from that specific site", "Persistence of resident memory CD8+ T-cells in the affected epidermis", "Deposition of circulating IgG immune complexes in the local capillary bed", "Localized accumulation of IgE molecules on dermal mast cells"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fixed Drug Eruption (FDE) is characterized by recurrent erythematous plaques that appear in the exact same localized site (skin or mucosa) upon re-exposure to the offending drug. This site-specific memory is mediated by resident memory CD8+ T-cells that remain quiescent in the basal epidermis of the affected plaque and rapidly degranulate upon systemic re-exposure to the drug.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old woman presents with a single, highly circumscribed, dusky-red, itchy patch on their glans penis that developed 24 hours after taking a medication. Which of the following drugs is the most common trigger for a fixed drug eruption (FDE) affecting the genitalia?', '["Atorvastatin", "Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (Sulfonamides)", "Amlodipine", "Metformin", "Aspirin"]'::jsonb, 1, 'While FDE can be triggered by many drugs (including NSAIDs and tetracyclines), sulfonamides—most notably Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole—are the most common triggers for fixed drug eruptions located on the genitalia (glans penis or labia).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old boy is started on Warfarin for a new deep vein thrombosis. Due to an oversight, no Heparin bridge is administered. On day 3 of therapy, the patient develops severe pain and dark purple, well-demarcated patches on their breasts and thighs. Within 24 hours, these lesions progress to necrotic black eschars. What is the primary molecular cause of this reaction?', '["Direct toxic necrosis of adipocytes caused by accumulation of the warfarin molecule", "An IgE-mediated anaphylactoid reaction causing localized vasculitis", "Localized vasoconstriction from paradoxical alpha-adrenergic stimulation", "A rapid drop in Protein C levels (due to its short half-life), causing a transient hypercoagulable state and microthrombosis", "A drop in antithrombin III levels leading to arterial spasm"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Warfarin-induced skin necrosis occurs early in therapy (typically days 3-5) when warfarin is initiated without a heparin bridge, especially in patients with hereditary Protein C or S deficiency. Protein C has a very short half-life (approx 8 hours) and falls rapidly, while procoagulant factors II, IX, and X fall slowly. This creates a transient, severe hypercoagulable state leading to microthrombosis in the capillaries supplying fat-rich areas (breasts, thighs, buttocks).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient on subcutaneous Unfractionated Heparin therapy for a pulmonary embolism develops painful, red-to-purple patches at their abdominal injection sites 7 days into therapy, which quickly progress to localized skin necrosis. Lab work reveals a platelet count that has dropped from 280,000/mm3 to 90,000/mm3. What is the target of the autoantibodies driving this clinical presentation?', '["The G-protein coupled thrombin receptor", "Protein C and Protein S", "Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes", "Type XVII Collagen (BP180) in the basement membrane", "Desmoglein 1 and Desmoglein 3 in the epidermis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT) presenting with localized heparin-induced skin necrosis at the injection sites. HIT is mediated by IgG autoantibodies targeting Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes, which bind and activate platelets, causing both thrombocytopenia (clearance of platelets) and a severe, life-threatening prothrombotic state.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient develops heparin-induced skin necrosis and thrombocytopenia (HIT). Which of the following is the most critical and immediate next step in management?', '["Apply high-potency topical steroids to the necrotic injection sites", "Increase the heparin dose to overcome the antibody-mediated clearance", "Discontinue all heparin products immediately and initiate a non-heparin anticoagulant (e.g., Argatroban)", "Transfuse 2 units of platelets and continue subcutaneous heparin", "Stop heparin, administer Vitamin K, and start oral warfarin immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'When HIT is suspected or confirmed (due to a platelet drop >50% and/or skin necrosis), the immediate action must be to stop all heparin products (including low-molecular-weight heparin and heparin flushes) and start an alternative, non-heparin rapid anticoagulant (e.g., a direct thrombin inhibitor like Argatroban or Bivalirudin) to prevent catastrophic thromboembolism. Warfarin is contraindicated in active HIT as it can precipitate gangrene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old girl being treated with Hydralazine for severe hypertension presents with joint pain, fever, and a mild malar-like rash on their face. Lab results show a positive ANA (1:640) and highly elevated Anti-Histone antibodies. Anti-dsDNA and Anti-Smith antibodies are negative. Urinalysis and serum creatinine are normal. What is the clinical significance of anti-histone antibodies in this patient?', '["They represent a high risk of transition to Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis", "They are highly specific for idiopathic systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)", "They are caused by a cross-reaction with the hydralazine molecule itself", "They are highly sensitive (approx 95%) for drug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE)", "They indicate severe, silent lupus nephritis requiring renal biopsy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Anti-histone antibodies are highly sensitive (present in 90-95% of cases) for Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE). Common triggers include Hydralazine, Procainamide, and Isoniazid. Unlike idiopathic SLE, DILE rarely involves the kidneys or central nervous system, and anti-dsDNA or anti-Smith antibodies are usually absent.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old man being treated with D-Penicillamine for Wilson''s disease develops flaccid blisters on their chest and upper back, along with painful sores in their mouth. Physical exam shows a positive Nikolsky sign. A skin biopsy reveals intraepidermal acantholysis. Direct Immunofluorescence (DIF) demonstrates intercellular IgG deposition in a ''fishnet'' pattern throughout the epidermis. What is the target of the autoantibodies in this drug-induced condition?', '["Integrin alpha-6 beta-4 in the hemidesmosomes", "Laminin-332 in the lamina lucida", "BP180 (Type XVII Collagen) and BP230", "Desmogleins (Dsg1 and Dsg3)", "Type VII Collagen in the anchoring fibrils"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Drug-Induced Pemphigus, characteristically triggered by thiol (sulfhydryl) group-containing drugs like D-Penicillamine, Captopril, and Gold salts. Thiol groups can biochemically disrupt desmosomes or act as haptens. The autoantibodies target desmoglein 1 and/or 3, leading to flaccid blisters, mucosal lesions, acantholysis, and a ''fishnet'' DIF pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An 82-year-old man on Linagliptin (a DPP-4 inhibitor) for type 2 diabetes presents with an intensely pruritic rash and several large, firm, tense blisters on their abdomen and lower extremities. Nikolsky sign is negative. A biopsy shows a subepidermal split with numerous eosinophils. DIF demonstrates a linear band of IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pseudoporphyria", "Linear IgA Bullous Dermatosis", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita", "Drug-induced Bullous Pemphigoid", "Drug-induced Pemphigus Vulgaris"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Drug-induced Bullous Pemphigoid (BP), characteristically presenting with tense subepidermal blisters and severe pruritus. In recent years, Dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitors (gliptins) have been recognized as a major drug trigger for BP, particularly in elderly patients. Loop diuretics (furosemide) are another classic trigger.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old woman being treated with a Beta-blocker for hypertension presents with an extremely itchy rash on their forearms and shins. Physical examination reveals symmetric, flat-topped, polygonal, purple papules. Fine white lines (Wickham striae) are visible on the surface under magnification. How does this drug-induced lichenoid eruption typically differ from classic, idiopathic Lichen Planus?', '["It presents with large, flaccid blisters and a positive Nikolsky sign", "It is often more generalized, symmetric, and frequently lacks mucosal involvement or classic Wickham striae", "It is completely non-pruritic and does not show interface dermatitis on biopsy", "It is highly infectious and spreads to close contacts", "It selectively targets the face and spares the extremities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Lichenoid drug eruptions (triggered by beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, thiazides, and gold) clinically mimic Lichen Planus. However, they typically differ by being more generalized, symmetric, and having a eczematous or psoriasiform appearance, while frequently sparing the oral mucosa and showing a slower resolution after drug withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old male presents with an explosive onset of acne-like bumps on their chest and upper back. They deny having any acne as a teenager. Physical exam reveals dozens of monomorphic (all at the same stage of development) erythematous inflammatory papules and pustules. No blackheads or whiteheads (comedones) are present. The patient was recently treated with a high-dose oral prednisone taper. What is the diagnosis?', '["Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Acne vulgaris (papulopustular subtype)", "Hot tub folliculitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Steroid acne is an acneiform drug eruption caused by systemic or high-potency topical corticosteroids. Clinically, it is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules on the trunk and shoulders. Unlike acne vulgaris, steroid acne lacks comedones (blackheads/whiteheads) and the lesions are all at the identical stage of development.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient receiving a rapid intravenous infusion of Vancomycin suddenly develops severe flushing, pruritus, and a bright red rash over their face, neck, and upper torso. Their heart rate is 115/min and blood pressure is 98/60 mmHg. There is no wheezing, lip swelling, or hives. What is the primary mechanism of this reaction?', '["Direct, non-immunologic histamine release from mast cells triggered by the rapid drug infusion", "T-cell mediated cytotoxic destruction of endothelial cells", "Direct toxic effect of the vancomycin molecule on vascular smooth muscle", "Type III hypersensitivity with deposition of IgA immune complexes in dermal vessels", "IgE-mediated Type I hypersensitivity reaction causing systemic anaphylaxis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This is Red Man Syndrome, a pseudo-allergic (anaphylactoid) reaction caused by a rapid infusion of intravenous Vancomycin. The drug directly degranulates mast cells and basophils in a non-immunologic fashion, releasing histamine. It is not a true IgE-mediated allergy. It can be prevented by slowing the infusion rate (to over 60 minutes) and pre-treating with antihistamines.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old child presents with a widespread, hive-like rash, severe joint pain and swelling of the ankles, and a low-grade fever that began 10 days after starting Cefaclor for an ear infection. Lab work shows a normal platelet count and no proteinuria. What is the diagnosis?', '["Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR)", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome", "True Serum Sickness", "Acute Rheumatic Fever", "Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura (IgA Vasculitis)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR) is a benign, transient reaction that classically occurs in children 1-3 weeks after starting certain drugs, most famously Cefaclor, as well as penicillins and sulfonamides. It mimics true serum sickness with fever, arthralgias, and urticarial plaques, but crucially LACKS circulating immune complexes, complement depletion, vasculitis, and renal damage (proteinuria).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old female being treated with high-dose Capecitabine for metastatic colorectal cancer presents with painful redness, swelling, and tingling on their palms and soles. Physical examination reveals symmetric, bright erythema and edema of the palmar and plantar surfaces, with early desquamation. What is the clinical term for this condition?', '["Hand-Foot-and-Mouth Disease", "Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (Hand-Foot Syndrome)", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Fixed Drug Eruption", "Contact Dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (also known as Hand-Foot Syndrome) is a common cutaneous side effect of several chemotherapy agents, most classically Capecitabine, 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU), and Cytarabine. It is caused by drug leaking from small capillaries into the thick skin of the palms and soles, leading to direct toxicity, pain, symmetric erythema, swelling, and peeling.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old male being treated with Cetuximab (an EGFR inhibitor) for lung cancer develops an itchy, acne-like rash consisting of papules and pustules on their face, neck, and upper chest. Comedones are completely absent. What is the clinical significance of this rash?', '["It is a positive prognostic indicator (the development and severity of the rash correlate with a favorable tumor response)", "It represents a deep follicular bacterial infection requiring intravenous antibiotics", "It is caused by secondary adrenal insufficiency from the chemotherapy", "It indicates a life-threatening drug allergy and the Cetuximab must be stopped permanently", "It is a sign of tumor progression and therapeutic failure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'EGFR inhibitor-induced acneiform (papulopustular) eruption is a highly common and expected class-effect of drugs like Cetuximab, Erlotinib, and Panitumumab. Counter-intuitively, the development and severity of this rash act as a positive surrogate marker for drug efficacy, correlating with improved tumor response and patient survival. It is managed with topical/oral antibiotics (doxycycline) and steroids without stopping the chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old girl who has been taking Minocycline for severe acne for 3 years presents with a new, muddy, blue-gray discoloration on their shins and the mucosal surface of their lower gums. A skin biopsy reveals iron-containing and melanin-containing pigment deposits within the dermis. What is the subtype and characteristic appearance of this minocycline-induced hyperpigmentation?', '["Argiria, caused by silver deposition in the eccrine glands", "Type III Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, presenting as a diffuse muddy-brown tan in sun-exposed areas", "Slate-gray facial pigmentation induced by drug-induced lipofuscin", "Type I Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, limited strictly to sites of prior inflammation and scarring", "Type II Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, affecting normal skin of the shins and forearms, as well as gums"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Minocycline can induce three distinct types of skin hyperpigmentation: Type I occurs strictly in scars and areas of prior inflammation (blue-black pigment); Type II occurs on normal skin, classically the shins, forearms, and gums (slate-blue/gray pigment containing iron and melanin); and Type III is a diffuse, muddy-brown hyperpigmentation in sun-exposed areas (pure melanin).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old man with a history of refractory cardiac arrhythmias has been taking Amiodarone for 5 years. They present to the clinic complaining of a blue-gray discoloration of their face, nose, and ears. Biopsy of the affected skin reveals lipofuscin-like granules within dermal macrophages. What is the key environmental trigger required for the development of this specific pigmentation?', '["Frequent hot water baths", "Exposure to cold weather (Raynaud-like)", "Ultraviolet (UV) light exposure (sunlight)", "High dietary intake of vitamin C", "Chronic mechanical friction"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Amiodarone-induced hyperpigmentation is a classic side effect occurring in patients on long-term, high-dose amiodarone therapy. It presents as a slate-gray to blue-violet discoloration strictly in sun-exposed areas (face, nose, ears). Ultraviolet (UV) light is required to trigger the deposition of a drug-lipid complex (lipofuscin) inside dermal macrophages.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old man with systemic lupus erythematosus has been taking Hydroxychloroquine for 6 years. During a routine skin check, the dermatologist notes well-demarcated, slate-gray patches on the patient''s bilateral shins and a bluish-gray discoloration of the hard palate. What is the prognosis and clinical course of this pigmentation?', '["It is completely permanent and will continue to spread even if the drug is discontinued", "It resolves spontaneously within 2 weeks of stopping the drug", "It indicates an immediate risk of acute hepatic failure", "It is slowly reversible over months to years after stopping the hydroxychloroquine, though some areas may persist", "It is highly infectious and can be transmitted to others"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Skin and mucosal hyperpigmentation occurs in up to 25% of patients on long-term antimalarials (Chloroquine/Hydroxychloroquine). Classic sites are the shins (pretibial area), the hard palate, and the nail beds. The pigmentation is slowly reversible over many months or years after drug cessation, though some localized areas may remain permanently.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 32-year-old woman undergoing chemotherapy for Hodgkin''s lymphoma develops intensely pruritic, linear, criss-crossing red streaks on her back and chest that resemble ''whip marks''. She admits to scratching her skin due to dryness. Which of the following chemotherapy agents is the classic cause of this flagellate erythema?', '["Cyclophosphamide", "5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)", "Bleomycin", "Paclitaxel", "Doxorubicin"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Flagellate erythema is a virtually pathognomonic cutaneous toxicity associated with Bleomycin therapy. It presents as highly characteristic, linear, criss-crossing erythematous streaks that look like ''whip marks'' on the back and trunk. It represents a Koebner-like response where minor scratching or friction concentrates the drug in the local skin vessels.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old male presents with progressive, painless, firm, fibrous swelling of their gums that has begun to cover the crowns of their teeth. They have a history of epilepsy. Which of the following triads of medications represents the classic causes of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia?', '["Prednisone, Isotretinoin, and Spironolactone", "Metformin, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Penicillin, Doxycycline, and Cephalexin", "Phenytoin, Cyclosporine, and Nifedipine", "Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Drug-induced gingival hyperplasia is a classic side effect characterized by fibrous overgrowth of the gingival papillae. The three classic, high-yield culprits are Phenytoin (anti-epileptic), Cyclosporine (immunosuppressant), and Calcium Channel Blockers (especially Nifedipine, but also Amlodipine/Diltiazem).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old boy taking Naproxen for osteoarthritic pain presents with skin fragility and multiple small, tense vesicles and erosions on the dorsum of their hands. Biopsy reveals a subepidermal blister with minimal inflammatory cell infiltrate. Laboratory workup demonstrates normal levels of urinary and fecal porphyrins, ruling out Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT). What is the diagnosis?', '["Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT)", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita", "Pseudoporphyria", "Bullous Pemphigoid", "Allergic Contact Dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pseudoporphyria is a drug-induced condition that clinically and histologically mimics Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT), presenting with skin fragility, blistering, and scarring on sun-exposed hands. Crucially, porphyrin levels in the blood, urine, and stool are completely normal in pseudoporphyria. NSAIDs (most famously Naproxen) are the leading cause.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 25-year-old female presents with multiple extremely painful, warm, red, deep-seated nodules on her bilateral shins that have been present for 2 weeks. She reports starting oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) 2 months ago. A biopsy reveals septal panniculitis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Erythema Induratum (Bazin''s disease)", "Weber-Christian disease", "Sweet''s syndrome", "Polyarteritis Nodosa (PAN)", "Erythema Nodosum (EN)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Erythema Nodosum (EN) is a classic form of panniculitis (inflammation of subcutaneous fat) that presents as tender, warm, erythematous nodules on the pretibial shins. It represents a hypersensitivity reaction to various triggers, including infections (strep, TB) and medications—most commonly oral contraceptives (OCPs) and sulfonamides. Histology classically shows septal panniculitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old child presents with high fever, severe mucosal crusting and ulceration of the lips and mouth, and conjunctival redness with purulent discharge. Physical exam reveals less than 1% BSA of skin detachment with only a few targetoid macules on the limbs. The child was treated for ''walking pneumonia'' 1 week ago. What is the diagnosis?', '["Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS)", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Primary Herpes Simplex Gingivostomatitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS) is triggered by Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection, most commonly in children and young adults. It is characterized by severe mucositis involving $\ge 2$ mucosal sites (typically oral and ocular) with minimal or absent cutaneous lesions (<1% BSA skin involvement). It mimics SJS but has a distinct pathogenesis and carries a significantly better prognosis with lower mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old patient presents with painful, dark purple, necrotic, star-shaped (retiform) patches on their bilateral earlobes, cheeks, and lower extremities. Lab work reveals a severe neutropenia (WBC 1,200/mm3 with an absolute neutrophil count of 350/mm3). A urine drug screen is positive for Cocaine. What is the causative agent responsible for this patient''s necrotic ear skin and bone marrow suppression?', '["Amiodarone-induced photo-vasculitis", "Direct vasoconstrictive toxicity of the cocaine molecule itself", "A contamination of cocaine with Streptococcus pyogenes", "An IgE-mediated drug allergy to benzoylecgonine", "Levamisole (a veterinary dewormer used as a cocaine cutting agent)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Levamisole-induced retiform purpura. Levamisole is a veterinary anthelmintic commonly used as an adulterant (cutting agent) in illicit cocaine. It triggers a unique, severe autoimmune vasculitis characterized by painful, retiform (star-shaped) purpura with central necrosis, characteristically involving the ears/earlobes and face. It is also a potent bone marrow suppressant, leading to severe neutropenia/agranulocytosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old girl being treated with Hydralazine for 8 years presents with fever, joint pain, weight loss, and palpable purple spots (purpura) on their lower legs. Lab results show a positive ANA, dual positivity for p-ANCA (MPO) and c-ANCA (PR3) antibodies, and urinalysis reveals proteinuria and hematuria with red blood cell casts. What is the diagnosis?', '["Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA)", "Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura", "Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis is a severe necrotizing vasculitis classically triggered by long-term use of Hydralazine, Propylthiouracil (PTU), or Methimazole. Unlike simple drug-induced lupus (DILE), drug-induced ANCA vasculitis features high titers of p-ANCA (MPO) and PR3, palpable purpura, and significant internal organ involvement—most notably rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (renal failure).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old boy on Captopril (an ACE inhibitor) develops a widespread scaly rash on their trunk. They report a large, oval, scaly ''herald patch'' appeared on their chest first, followed by smaller oval plaques along the skin tension lines on their back in a ''Christmas tree'' distribution. How does this drug-induced Pityriasis Rosea-like eruption typically differ from idiopathic Pityriasis Rosea?', '["It is accompanied by severe kidney damage and proteinuria", "It presents with deep, tense bullae and a positive Nikolsky sign", "It often lacks a distinct initial herald patch and takes much longer to resolve after stopping the drug", "It is highly contagious and spreads to children via respiratory droplets", "It strictly affects the face and spares the trunk"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Pityriasis Rosea-like drug eruptions (triggered by ACE inhibitors, gold, beta-blockers, and metronidazole) closely mimic classic PR. However, the drug-induced form typically lacks the single large ''herald patch'' that precedes the main eruption, shows more confluent and inflammatory lesions, and is much more persistent, resolving slowly only after the offending drug is discontinued.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a history of stable, mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with bipolar disorder and started on Lithium. Two weeks later, they present with an explosive, severe flare of psoriasis, with thick, silvery plaques now covering 40% of their BSA. Which of the following lists represents the ''big four'' systemic drugs most famous for flaring or worsening psoriasis?', '["Amlodipine, Warfarin, Heparin, and Aspirin", "Penicillins, Cephalosporins, Macrolides, and Sulfonamides", "Isotretinoin, Spironolactone, Cyclosporine, and Prednisone", "Lithium, Beta-blockers, Antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), and NSAIDs", "Statins, Fibrates, Metformin, and Lisinopril"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Systemic drugs are common triggers for the onset or severe exacerbation of psoriasis. The classic, high-yield ''big four'' culprits are Lithium (bipolar therapy), Beta-blockers (cardiovascular therapy), Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine/chloroquine), and NSAIDs. Systemic steroid withdrawal can also precipitate a life-threatening rebound flare of generalized pustular psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Immediately after receiving an intramuscular (IM) injection of Penicillin G benzathine in the upper outer quadrant of the buttock, a 35-year-old man develops excruciating, burning pain and livedo-like, blue-gray discoloration around the injection site. Within 48 hours, this area develops deep, necrotic sloughing of the skin and underlying gluteal muscle. What is this clinical entity?', '["An IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction", "Nicolau Syndrome (Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa)", "Warfarin-induced skin necrosis", "Arthus reaction from vaccine immune complex deposition", "Necrotizing fasciitis from bacterial contamination"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nicolau Syndrome (also known as Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa) is a rare, devastating non-allergic vascular complication of intramuscular injections. It occurs due to accidental intra-arterial injection, peri-arterial injection, or drug-induced vasospasm/thrombosis. It presents with immediate severe pain, retiform cyanosis, and subsequent deep tissue necrosis of the skin, fat, and muscle.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing chemotherapy for leukemia is treated with G-CSF (Granulocyte Colony-Stimulating Factor) to manage neutropenia. Three days later, they develop a high fever (39.5 C) and several painful, bright red, ''succulent'' or ''juicy'' plaques on their neck and upper back. Biopsy reveals a massive, dense neutrophilic infiltrate in the upper dermis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["DRESS syndrome", "Erysipelas", "Sweet''s Syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis)", "Pyoderma Gangrenosum", "AGEP"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sweet''s syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis) is characterized by high fever, leukocytosis, and tender, erythematous plaques that are clinically described as ''juicy'' or ''succulent'' due to profound upper dermal edema. Histology shows a dense dermal neutrophilic infiltrate without vasculitis. G-CSF is a classic drug trigger because it directly stimulates neutrophil production.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old man being treated with Paclitaxel for breast cancer develops bilateral, woody thickening and hardening of the skin of their lower legs and forearms. Physical exam shows marked induration, but there is an absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon or nail fold capillary changes, and systemic organ involvement is absent. ANA testing is negative. What is the diagnosis?', '["Taxane-induced scleroderma-like changes", "Stasis dermatitis", "Eosinophilic Fasciitis", "Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma)", "Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Taxanes (Paclitaxel, Docetaxel) can induce scleroderma-like skin changes (induration, woody thickening) typically localized to the extremities. The key clinical clues to distinguish this drug-induced form from idiopathic Systemic Sclerosis are the absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon, normal nail fold capillaries, negative autoantibodies (ANA/Scl-70), and lack of visceral sclerosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old male taking Vemurafenib (a BRAF inhibitor) for metastatic melanoma develops several fast-growing, dome-shaped nodules with central keratinous craters on their arms within 3 weeks of starting the drug. Biopsy of one lesion shows a well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma/keratoacanthoma (SCC/KA). What is the mechanism driving these secondary skin malignancies?', '["A metastatic spread of the primary melanoma to the skin", "Systemic immunosuppression leading to explosive reactivation of HPV-16", "Paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do NOT have the BRAF mutation", "Upregulation of the Hedgehog pathway in follicular basal cells", "Direct mutagenic damage to epidermal stem cell DNA by the drug molecule"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Vemurafenib and other BRAF inhibitors cause paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal cells that do not harbor the BRAF V600E mutation. The drug binds to wild-type BRAF, causing it to homodimerize and hyper-activate downstream MEK/ERK signaling, driving rapid keratinocyte proliferation and leading to eruptive Keratoacanthomas and low-grade Squamous Cell Carcinomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old man on Sorafenib (a multi-kinase inhibitor) for renal cell carcinoma develops thick, painful, yellow calluses surrounded by a bright red halo on the heels and lateral aspects of their feet. How does this Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) clinically differ from Capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS)?', '["Classic HFS is caused by direct exposure to sunlight", "There is no clinical difference between the two reactions", "HFSR strictly affects the fingernails and spares the skin", "HFSR presents as localized hyperkeratosis and thick calluses on pressure points, whereas classic HFS is characterized by diffuse, symmetric erythema and peeling", "HFSR is completely painless and resolves within 24 hours of starting the drug"]'::jsonb, 3, 'While both affect the hands/feet, Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) is clinically distinct from capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS). HFSR is characterized by localized, hyperkeratotic, thick yellow plaques and blisters on high-friction pressure points (heels/lateral sole), while classic HFS (capecitabine) presents as diffuse, confluent erythema, edema, and desquamation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old woman with chronic renal insufficiency receives intravenous iodinated contrast for a CT scan. Two days later, they develop painful, rapidly expanding, pustular, vegetating plaques on their face and lower extremities. Pustule culture is negative. Biopsy shows massive epidermal hyperplasia (pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia) and dense neutrophilic microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Blastomycosis", "Iododerma (Halogenoderma)", "Sweet''s syndrome", "Pyoderma Gangrenosum", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Halogenodermas (Iododerma and Bromoderma) are rare cutaneous toxic accumulations of halogens, most commonly occurring in patients with impaired renal clearance. Clinically, they present as highly inflammatory, pustular, vegetating plaques that can closely mimic Pyoderma Gangrenosum, deep fungal infections (like Blastomycosis), or Squamous Cell Carcinoma. Histology shows marked pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old man is brought to the emergency department with a painful rash, high fever, and severe eye soreness that began 2 weeks after starting Allopurinol. Physical exam reveals confluent, atypical targetoid macules with flaccid blisters and sloughing covering approximately 8% of the total body surface area (BSA). Two mucosal sites are severely involved. Skin biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis with minimal dermal inflammation. What is the diagnosis?', '["SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS), defined by fever, mucosal involvement of $\ge 2$ sites, targetoid macules, and epidermal detachment affecting <10% of the body surface area (BSA). Biopsy characteristically shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis (apoptosis of keratinocytes) driven by cytotoxic T-cells and granulysin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old boy on Phenytoin for seizures presents with fever and widespread skin tenderness. Within 24 hours, the skin begins to peel off in large sheets. Physical examination shows dusky erythema progressing to confluent blisters. Gentle lateral pressure on the erythematous skin causes the epidermis to slide off the dermis (positive Nikolsky sign). The epidermal detachment is estimated at 35% of the total body surface area. What is the primary cause of mortality in this condition?', '["Acute kidney injury from severe myoglobinuria", "Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "High-output cardiac failure and hypothermia", "Hepatocellular failure and acute encephalopathy", "Infection, sepsis, and multi-organ failure"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN), characterized by epidermal detachment of >30% BSA and a positive Nikolsky sign. The loss of the skin barrier leads to massive fluid shifts, hypothermia, and high-output heart failure, but the leading cause of death in these patients is infection (sepsis) and subsequent multi-organ failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old female presents with a painful, blistering rash and fever. They recently started Sulfasalazine for inflammatory bowel disease. Physical examination reveals atypical targetoid plaques on the trunk and extremities, with flaccid bullae that slide off under lateral pressure. The surface area of epidermal detachment is measured at exactly 18% of the total BSA. What is the clinical category of this patient''s drug reaction?', '["Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "DRESS syndrome", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The SJS/TEN spectrum is categorized by the percentage of body surface area showing epidermal detachment: SJS involves <10% BSA detachment; SJS/TEN Overlap involves 10% to 30% BSA detachment; and TEN involves >30% BSA detachment. At 18% detachment, this patient falls into the SJS/TEN Overlap category.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old woman of East Asian descent is about to start Carbamazepine for neuropathic pain. The clinician orders genetic screening for the HLA-B*1502 allele before prescribing. What is the clinical significance of this allele?', '["It correlates with an increased risk of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia", "It indicates an increased risk of severe, irreversible agranulocytosis", "It carries an extremely high risk of Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in Asian populations", "It is a marker for drug-induced phototoxicity and exaggerated sunburns", "It is associated with rapid metabolic clearance of Carbamazepine, making it ineffective"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The HLA-B*1502 allele is strongly associated with an increased risk of developing Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in individuals of East Asian and South-East Asian descent. Screening is mandatory or highly recommended prior to starting Carbamazepine in these high-risk populations.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old man with Han Chinese ancestry is diagnosed with gout and is about to start Allopurinol therapy. Which HLA allele is strongly associated with Allopurinol-induced Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) in this population?', '["HLA-B*2705", "HLA-B*5701", "HLA-B*1502", "HLA-B*5801", "HLA-DRB1*0401"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The HLA-B*5801 allele is a potent genetic risk factor for Allopurinol-induced severe cutaneous adverse reactions, including SJS and TEN, particularly in Han Chinese, Thai, and Korean populations. HLA-B*1502 is associated with carbamazepine, and HLA-B*5701 is linked to abacavir hypersensitivity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old man with Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) involving 45% BSA is admitted to the hospital. Which of the following clinical settings is the most appropriate for the management of this patient?', '["A specialized Burn Unit or Intensive Care Unit (ICU)", "An infectious disease isolation room", "An outpatient clinic with daily nurse home visits", "A standard dermatology inpatient ward", "A clean private room in a general medical ward"]'::jsonb, 0, 'TEN is a life-threatening dermatologic emergency. Due to extensive skin loss, patients require meticulous sterile wound care, specialized non-adherent dressings, tight fluid resuscitation, and temperature regulation identical to severe burn victims. Management in a specialized Burn Unit or intensive care setting significantly reduces mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old boy began taking Carbamazepine 4 weeks ago. They now present with a high fever (39.0 C), a widespread red skin eruption, and severe facial and periorbital swelling. Physical exam reveals confluent morbilliform plaques on the trunk, palpable lymphadenopathy in the neck and axillae, and moderate tender hepatomegaly. Lab work shows a WBC of 16,500/mm3 with 18% eosinophils (absolute eosinophil count 2,970/mm3) and AST/ALT elevated at 4 times the upper limit of normal. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS) syndrome", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Exanthematous (Morbilliform) Drug Eruption", "Serum Sickness", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'DRESS syndrome (also known as Drug-Induced Hypersensitivity Syndrome) is a severe, life-threatening drug reaction characterized by a long latency period (2-8 weeks), high fever, a diffuse erythematous eruption, facial/periorbital edema, lymphadenopathy, hematologic abnormalities (eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytosis), and visceral organ involvement—most commonly hepatitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is diagnosed with DRESS syndrome after starting Phenytoin 6 weeks ago. Aside from stopping the offending drug, what virus reactivation is frequently associated with the pathogenesis and flare-ups of this systemic drug hypersensitivity?', '["Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Hepatitis C Virus (HCV)", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'DRESS syndrome is strongly associated with the sequential reactivation of latent herpesviruses, most notably Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6), as well as HHV-7, EBV, and CMV. This viral reactivation is thought to drive the prolonged, recurrent course of the disease even after the causative drug has been completely cleared.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old male is started on Cephalexin for a suspected soft tissue infection. Forty-eight hours later, they develop a high fever (39.2 C) and a burning rash that started in the groin and axillae. Physical exam reveals diffuse, bright erythema studded with hundreds of non-follicular, sterile, pinpoint pustules. Lab work shows a marked leukocytosis with neutrophilia (WBC 19,000/mm3). Pustule cultures are sterile. What is the diagnosis?', '["Subcorneal Pustular Dermatosis", "DRESS syndrome", "Disseminated candidiasis", "Generalized Pustular Psoriasis (Von Zumbusch)", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'AGEP is a rare, severe cutaneous reaction characterized by the rapid onset (typically <48 hours after drug exposure) of high fever and hundreds of small, sterile, non-follicular pustules arising on a background of edematous erythema, classically starting in flexural folds (groin/axillae). Beta-lactams and macrolides are the most common triggers. Leukocytosis with marked neutrophilia is characteristic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old female presents with a dark, round spot on their left forearm. They note that every time they take Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection, the exact same spot becomes red, swollen, and itchy, occasionally forming a small central blister. Once it heals, a persistent dusky brown patch remains. What is the mechanism of this ''fixing'' to the same anatomic site?', '["Localized accumulation of IgE molecules on dermal mast cells", "Deposition of circulating IgG immune complexes in the local capillary bed", "Slow lymphatic clearance of the drug from that specific site", "Persistence of resident memory CD8+ T-cells in the affected epidermis", "Localized somatic mutation in the keratinocytes of that area"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Fixed Drug Eruption (FDE) is characterized by recurrent erythematous plaques that appear in the exact same localized site (skin or mucosa) upon re-exposure to the offending drug. This site-specific memory is mediated by resident memory CD8+ T-cells that remain quiescent in the basal epidermis of the affected plaque and rapidly degranulate upon systemic re-exposure to the drug.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male presents with a single, highly circumscribed, dusky-red, itchy patch on their glans penis that developed 24 hours after taking a medication. Which of the following drugs is the most common trigger for a fixed drug eruption (FDE) affecting the genitalia?', '["Aspirin", "Metformin", "Amlodipine", "Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (Sulfonamides)", "Atorvastatin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'While FDE can be triggered by many drugs (including NSAIDs and tetracyclines), sulfonamides—most notably Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole—are the most common triggers for fixed drug eruptions located on the genitalia (glans penis or labia).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old man is started on Warfarin for a new deep vein thrombosis. Due to an oversight, no Heparin bridge is administered. On day 3 of therapy, the patient develops severe pain and dark purple, well-demarcated patches on their breasts and thighs. Within 24 hours, these lesions progress to necrotic black eschars. What is the primary molecular cause of this reaction?', '["Direct toxic necrosis of adipocytes caused by accumulation of the warfarin molecule", "An IgE-mediated anaphylactoid reaction causing localized vasculitis", "Localized vasoconstriction from paradoxical alpha-adrenergic stimulation", "A rapid drop in Protein C levels (due to its short half-life), causing a transient hypercoagulable state and microthrombosis", "A drop in antithrombin III levels leading to arterial spasm"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Warfarin-induced skin necrosis occurs early in therapy (typically days 3-5) when warfarin is initiated without a heparin bridge, especially in patients with hereditary Protein C or S deficiency. Protein C has a very short half-life (approx 8 hours) and falls rapidly, while procoagulant factors II, IX, and X fall slowly. This creates a transient, severe hypercoagulable state leading to microthrombosis in the capillaries supplying fat-rich areas (breasts, thighs, buttocks).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient on subcutaneous Unfractionated Heparin therapy for a pulmonary embolism develops painful, red-to-purple patches at their abdominal injection sites 7 days into therapy, which quickly progress to localized skin necrosis. Lab work reveals a platelet count that has dropped from 280,000/mm3 to 90,000/mm3. What is the target of the autoantibodies driving this clinical presentation?', '["Type XVII Collagen (BP180) in the basement membrane", "The G-protein coupled thrombin receptor", "Desmoglein 1 and Desmoglein 3 in the epidermis", "Protein C and Protein S", "Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT) presenting with localized heparin-induced skin necrosis at the injection sites. HIT is mediated by IgG autoantibodies targeting Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes, which bind and activate platelets, causing both thrombocytopenia (clearance of platelets) and a severe, life-threatening prothrombotic state.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient develops heparin-induced skin necrosis and thrombocytopenia (HIT). Which of the following is the most critical and immediate next step in management?', '["Stop heparin, administer Vitamin K, and start oral warfarin immediately", "Apply high-potency topical steroids to the necrotic injection sites", "Discontinue all heparin products immediately and initiate a non-heparin anticoagulant (e.g., Argatroban)", "Transfuse 2 units of platelets and continue subcutaneous heparin", "Increase the heparin dose to overcome the antibody-mediated clearance"]'::jsonb, 2, 'When HIT is suspected or confirmed (due to a platelet drop >50% and/or skin necrosis), the immediate action must be to stop all heparin products (including low-molecular-weight heparin and heparin flushes) and start an alternative, non-heparin rapid anticoagulant (e.g., a direct thrombin inhibitor like Argatroban or Bivalirudin) to prevent catastrophic thromboembolism. Warfarin is contraindicated in active HIT as it can precipitate gangrene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old female being treated with Hydralazine for severe hypertension presents with joint pain, fever, and a mild malar-like rash on their face. Lab results show a positive ANA (1:640) and highly elevated Anti-Histone antibodies. Anti-dsDNA and Anti-Smith antibodies are negative. Urinalysis and serum creatinine are normal. What is the clinical significance of anti-histone antibodies in this patient?', '["They indicate severe, silent lupus nephritis requiring renal biopsy", "They are caused by a cross-reaction with the hydralazine molecule itself", "They are highly specific for idiopathic systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)", "They are highly sensitive (approx 95%) for drug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE)", "They represent a high risk of transition to Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Anti-histone antibodies are highly sensitive (present in 90-95% of cases) for Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE). Common triggers include Hydralazine, Procainamide, and Isoniazid. Unlike idiopathic SLE, DILE rarely involves the kidneys or central nervous system, and anti-dsDNA or anti-Smith antibodies are usually absent.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old girl being treated with D-Penicillamine for Wilson''s disease develops flaccid blisters on their chest and upper back, along with painful sores in their mouth. Physical exam shows a positive Nikolsky sign. A skin biopsy reveals intraepidermal acantholysis. Direct Immunofluorescence (DIF) demonstrates intercellular IgG deposition in a ''fishnet'' pattern throughout the epidermis. What is the target of the autoantibodies in this drug-induced condition?', '["Integrin alpha-6 beta-4 in the hemidesmosomes", "Desmogleins (Dsg1 and Dsg3)", "Type VII Collagen in the anchoring fibrils", "BP180 (Type XVII Collagen) and BP230", "Laminin-332 in the lamina lucida"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient has Drug-Induced Pemphigus, characteristically triggered by thiol (sulfhydryl) group-containing drugs like D-Penicillamine, Captopril, and Gold salts. Thiol groups can biochemically disrupt desmosomes or act as haptens. The autoantibodies target desmoglein 1 and/or 3, leading to flaccid blisters, mucosal lesions, acantholysis, and a ''fishnet'' DIF pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'An 82-year-old man on Linagliptin (a DPP-4 inhibitor) for type 2 diabetes presents with an intensely pruritic rash and several large, firm, tense blisters on their abdomen and lower extremities. Nikolsky sign is negative. A biopsy shows a subepidermal split with numerous eosinophils. DIF demonstrates a linear band of IgG and C3 deposition along the basement membrane zone. What is the diagnosis?', '["Pseudoporphyria", "Drug-induced Pemphigus Vulgaris", "Drug-induced Bullous Pemphigoid", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita", "Linear IgA Bullous Dermatosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Drug-induced Bullous Pemphigoid (BP), characteristically presenting with tense subepidermal blisters and severe pruritus. In recent years, Dipeptidyl peptidase-4 (DPP-4) inhibitors (gliptins) have been recognized as a major drug trigger for BP, particularly in elderly patients. Loop diuretics (furosemide) are another classic trigger.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old female being treated with a Beta-blocker for hypertension presents with an extremely itchy rash on their forearms and shins. Physical examination reveals symmetric, flat-topped, polygonal, purple papules. Fine white lines (Wickham striae) are visible on the surface under magnification. How does this drug-induced lichenoid eruption typically differ from classic, idiopathic Lichen Planus?', '["It presents with large, flaccid blisters and a positive Nikolsky sign", "It is completely non-pruritic and does not show interface dermatitis on biopsy", "It selectively targets the face and spares the extremities", "It is often more generalized, symmetric, and frequently lacks mucosal involvement or classic Wickham striae", "It is highly infectious and spreads to close contacts"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Lichenoid drug eruptions (triggered by beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, thiazides, and gold) clinically mimic Lichen Planus. However, they typically differ by being more generalized, symmetric, and having a eczematous or psoriasiform appearance, while frequently sparing the oral mucosa and showing a slower resolution after drug withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female presents with an explosive onset of acne-like bumps on their chest and upper back. They deny having any acne as a teenager. Physical exam reveals dozens of monomorphic (all at the same stage of development) erythematous inflammatory papules and pustules. No blackheads or whiteheads (comedones) are present. The patient was recently treated with a high-dose oral prednisone taper. What is the diagnosis?', '["Acne vulgaris (papulopustular subtype)", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Hot tub folliculitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Steroid acne is an acneiform drug eruption caused by systemic or high-potency topical corticosteroids. Clinically, it is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules on the trunk and shoulders. Unlike acne vulgaris, steroid acne lacks comedones (blackheads/whiteheads) and the lesions are all at the identical stage of development.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient receiving a rapid intravenous infusion of Vancomycin suddenly develops severe flushing, pruritus, and a bright red rash over their face, neck, and upper torso. Their heart rate is 115/min and blood pressure is 98/60 mmHg. There is no wheezing, lip swelling, or hives. What is the primary mechanism of this reaction?', '["Type III hypersensitivity with deposition of IgA immune complexes in dermal vessels", "T-cell mediated cytotoxic destruction of endothelial cells", "Direct toxic effect of the vancomycin molecule on vascular smooth muscle", "IgE-mediated Type I hypersensitivity reaction causing systemic anaphylaxis", "Direct, non-immunologic histamine release from mast cells triggered by the rapid drug infusion"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This is Red Man Syndrome, a pseudo-allergic (anaphylactoid) reaction caused by a rapid infusion of intravenous Vancomycin. The drug directly degranulates mast cells and basophils in a non-immunologic fashion, releasing histamine. It is not a true IgE-mediated allergy. It can be prevented by slowing the infusion rate (to over 60 minutes) and pre-treating with antihistamines.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old child presents with a widespread, hive-like rash, severe joint pain and swelling of the ankles, and a low-grade fever that began 10 days after starting Cefaclor for an ear infection. Lab work shows a normal platelet count and no proteinuria. What is the diagnosis?', '["True Serum Sickness", "Acute Rheumatic Fever", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome", "Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR)", "Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura (IgA Vasculitis)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR) is a benign, transient reaction that classically occurs in children 1-3 weeks after starting certain drugs, most famously Cefaclor, as well as penicillins and sulfonamides. It mimics true serum sickness with fever, arthralgias, and urticarial plaques, but crucially LACKS circulating immune complexes, complement depletion, vasculitis, and renal damage (proteinuria).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old girl being treated with high-dose Capecitabine for metastatic colorectal cancer presents with painful redness, swelling, and tingling on their palms and soles. Physical examination reveals symmetric, bright erythema and edema of the palmar and plantar surfaces, with early desquamation. What is the clinical term for this condition?', '["Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (Hand-Foot Syndrome)", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Contact Dermatitis", "Hand-Foot-and-Mouth Disease", "Fixed Drug Eruption"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (also known as Hand-Foot Syndrome) is a common cutaneous side effect of several chemotherapy agents, most classically Capecitabine, 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU), and Cytarabine. It is caused by drug leaking from small capillaries into the thick skin of the palms and soles, leading to direct toxicity, pain, symmetric erythema, swelling, and peeling.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old male being treated with Cetuximab (an EGFR inhibitor) for lung cancer develops an itchy, acne-like rash consisting of papules and pustules on their face, neck, and upper chest. Comedones are completely absent. What is the clinical significance of this rash?', '["It is caused by secondary adrenal insufficiency from the chemotherapy", "It represents a deep follicular bacterial infection requiring intravenous antibiotics", "It is a positive prognostic indicator (the development and severity of the rash correlate with a favorable tumor response)", "It indicates a life-threatening drug allergy and the Cetuximab must be stopped permanently", "It is a sign of tumor progression and therapeutic failure"]'::jsonb, 2, 'EGFR inhibitor-induced acneiform (papulopustular) eruption is a highly common and expected class-effect of drugs like Cetuximab, Erlotinib, and Panitumumab. Counter-intuitively, the development and severity of this rash act as a positive surrogate marker for drug efficacy, correlating with improved tumor response and patient survival. It is managed with topical/oral antibiotics (doxycycline) and steroids without stopping the chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old girl who has been taking Minocycline for severe acne for 3 years presents with a new, muddy, blue-gray discoloration on their shins and the mucosal surface of their lower gums. A skin biopsy reveals iron-containing and melanin-containing pigment deposits within the dermis. What is the subtype and characteristic appearance of this minocycline-induced hyperpigmentation?', '["Slate-gray facial pigmentation induced by drug-induced lipofuscin", "Type III Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, presenting as a diffuse muddy-brown tan in sun-exposed areas", "Type II Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, affecting normal skin of the shins and forearms, as well as gums", "Argiria, caused by silver deposition in the eccrine glands", "Type I Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, limited strictly to sites of prior inflammation and scarring"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Minocycline can induce three distinct types of skin hyperpigmentation: Type I occurs strictly in scars and areas of prior inflammation (blue-black pigment); Type II occurs on normal skin, classically the shins, forearms, and gums (slate-blue/gray pigment containing iron and melanin); and Type III is a diffuse, muddy-brown hyperpigmentation in sun-exposed areas (pure melanin).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male with a history of refractory cardiac arrhythmias has been taking Amiodarone for 5 years. They present to the clinic complaining of a blue-gray discoloration of their face, nose, and ears. Biopsy of the affected skin reveals lipofuscin-like granules within dermal macrophages. What is the key environmental trigger required for the development of this specific pigmentation?', '["Exposure to cold weather (Raynaud-like)", "Ultraviolet (UV) light exposure (sunlight)", "Frequent hot water baths", "Chronic mechanical friction", "High dietary intake of vitamin C"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Amiodarone-induced hyperpigmentation is a classic side effect occurring in patients on long-term, high-dose amiodarone therapy. It presents as a slate-gray to blue-violet discoloration strictly in sun-exposed areas (face, nose, ears). Ultraviolet (UV) light is required to trigger the deposition of a drug-lipid complex (lipofuscin) inside dermal macrophages.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old man with systemic lupus erythematosus has been taking Hydroxychloroquine for 6 years. During a routine skin check, the dermatologist notes well-demarcated, slate-gray patches on the patient''s bilateral shins and a bluish-gray discoloration of the hard palate. What is the prognosis and clinical course of this pigmentation?', '["It resolves spontaneously within 2 weeks of stopping the drug", "It is slowly reversible over months to years after stopping the hydroxychloroquine, though some areas may persist", "It indicates an immediate risk of acute hepatic failure", "It is highly infectious and can be transmitted to others", "It is completely permanent and will continue to spread even if the drug is discontinued"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Skin and mucosal hyperpigmentation occurs in up to 25% of patients on long-term antimalarials (Chloroquine/Hydroxychloroquine). Classic sites are the shins (pretibial area), the hard palate, and the nail beds. The pigmentation is slowly reversible over many months or years after drug cessation, though some localized areas may remain permanently.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 32-year-old woman undergoing chemotherapy for Hodgkin''s lymphoma develops intensely pruritic, linear, criss-crossing red streaks on her back and chest that resemble ''whip marks''. She admits to scratching her skin due to dryness. Which of the following chemotherapy agents is the classic cause of this flagellate erythema?', '["Bleomycin", "Paclitaxel", "Doxorubicin", "5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)", "Cyclophosphamide"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Flagellate erythema is a virtually pathognomonic cutaneous toxicity associated with Bleomycin therapy. It presents as highly characteristic, linear, criss-crossing erythematous streaks that look like ''whip marks'' on the back and trunk. It represents a Koebner-like response where minor scratching or friction concentrates the drug in the local skin vessels.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old girl presents with progressive, painless, firm, fibrous swelling of their gums that has begun to cover the crowns of their teeth. They have a history of epilepsy. Which of the following triads of medications represents the classic causes of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia?', '["Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen", "Metformin, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Phenytoin, Cyclosporine, and Nifedipine", "Penicillin, Doxycycline, and Cephalexin", "Prednisone, Isotretinoin, and Spironolactone"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Drug-induced gingival hyperplasia is a classic side effect characterized by fibrous overgrowth of the gingival papillae. The three classic, high-yield culprits are Phenytoin (anti-epileptic), Cyclosporine (immunosuppressant), and Calcium Channel Blockers (especially Nifedipine, but also Amlodipine/Diltiazem).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old man taking Naproxen for osteoarthritic pain presents with skin fragility and multiple small, tense vesicles and erosions on the dorsum of their hands. Biopsy reveals a subepidermal blister with minimal inflammatory cell infiltrate. Laboratory workup demonstrates normal levels of urinary and fecal porphyrins, ruling out Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT). What is the diagnosis?', '["Pseudoporphyria", "Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT)", "Allergic Contact Dermatitis", "Bullous Pemphigoid", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pseudoporphyria is a drug-induced condition that clinically and histologically mimics Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT), presenting with skin fragility, blistering, and scarring on sun-exposed hands. Crucially, porphyrin levels in the blood, urine, and stool are completely normal in pseudoporphyria. NSAIDs (most famously Naproxen) are the leading cause.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 25-year-old female presents with multiple extremely painful, warm, red, deep-seated nodules on her bilateral shins that have been present for 2 weeks. She reports starting oral contraceptive pills (OCPs) 2 months ago. A biopsy reveals septal panniculitis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Erythema Nodosum (EN)", "Polyarteritis Nodosa (PAN)", "Weber-Christian disease", "Erythema Induratum (Bazin''s disease)", "Sweet''s syndrome"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Erythema Nodosum (EN) is a classic form of panniculitis (inflammation of subcutaneous fat) that presents as tender, warm, erythematous nodules on the pretibial shins. It represents a hypersensitivity reaction to various triggers, including infections (strep, TB) and medications—most commonly oral contraceptives (OCPs) and sulfonamides. Histology classically shows septal panniculitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old child presents with high fever, severe mucosal crusting and ulceration of the lips and mouth, and conjunctival redness with purulent discharge. Physical exam reveals less than 1% BSA of skin detachment with only a few targetoid macules on the limbs. The child was treated for ''walking pneumonia'' 1 week ago. What is the diagnosis?', '["Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS)", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Primary Herpes Simplex Gingivostomatitis", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS) is triggered by Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection, most commonly in children and young adults. It is characterized by severe mucositis involving $\ge 2$ mucosal sites (typically oral and ocular) with minimal or absent cutaneous lesions (<1% BSA skin involvement). It mimics SJS but has a distinct pathogenesis and carries a significantly better prognosis with lower mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old patient presents with painful, dark purple, necrotic, star-shaped (retiform) patches on their bilateral earlobes, cheeks, and lower extremities. Lab work reveals a severe neutropenia (WBC 1,200/mm3 with an absolute neutrophil count of 350/mm3). A urine drug screen is positive for Cocaine. What is the causative agent responsible for this patient''s necrotic ear skin and bone marrow suppression?', '["Direct vasoconstrictive toxicity of the cocaine molecule itself", "A contamination of cocaine with Streptococcus pyogenes", "An IgE-mediated drug allergy to benzoylecgonine", "Levamisole (a veterinary dewormer used as a cocaine cutting agent)", "Amiodarone-induced photo-vasculitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Levamisole-induced retiform purpura. Levamisole is a veterinary anthelmintic commonly used as an adulterant (cutting agent) in illicit cocaine. It triggers a unique, severe autoimmune vasculitis characterized by painful, retiform (star-shaped) purpura with central necrosis, characteristically involving the ears/earlobes and face. It is also a potent bone marrow suppressant, leading to severe neutropenia/agranulocytosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old woman being treated with Hydralazine for 8 years presents with fever, joint pain, weight loss, and palpable purple spots (purpura) on their lower legs. Lab results show a positive ANA, dual positivity for p-ANCA (MPO) and c-ANCA (PR3) antibodies, and urinalysis reveals proteinuria and hematuria with red blood cell casts. What is the diagnosis?', '["SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA)", "Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis", "Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura", "Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis is a severe necrotizing vasculitis classically triggered by long-term use of Hydralazine, Propylthiouracil (PTU), or Methimazole. Unlike simple drug-induced lupus (DILE), drug-induced ANCA vasculitis features high titers of p-ANCA (MPO) and PR3, palpable purpura, and significant internal organ involvement—most notably rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (renal failure).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old man on Captopril (an ACE inhibitor) develops a widespread scaly rash on their trunk. They report a large, oval, scaly ''herald patch'' appeared on their chest first, followed by smaller oval plaques along the skin tension lines on their back in a ''Christmas tree'' distribution. How does this drug-induced Pityriasis Rosea-like eruption typically differ from idiopathic Pityriasis Rosea?', '["It is highly contagious and spreads to children via respiratory droplets", "It is accompanied by severe kidney damage and proteinuria", "It strictly affects the face and spares the trunk", "It often lacks a distinct initial herald patch and takes much longer to resolve after stopping the drug", "It presents with deep, tense bullae and a positive Nikolsky sign"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pityriasis Rosea-like drug eruptions (triggered by ACE inhibitors, gold, beta-blockers, and metronidazole) closely mimic classic PR. However, the drug-induced form typically lacks the single large ''herald patch'' that precedes the main eruption, shows more confluent and inflammatory lesions, and is much more persistent, resolving slowly only after the offending drug is discontinued.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a history of stable, mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with bipolar disorder and started on Lithium. Two weeks later, they present with an explosive, severe flare of psoriasis, with thick, silvery plaques now covering 40% of their BSA. Which of the following lists represents the ''big four'' systemic drugs most famous for flaring or worsening psoriasis?', '["Statins, Fibrates, Metformin, and Lisinopril", "Isotretinoin, Spironolactone, Cyclosporine, and Prednisone", "Amlodipine, Warfarin, Heparin, and Aspirin", "Penicillins, Cephalosporins, Macrolides, and Sulfonamides", "Lithium, Beta-blockers, Antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), and NSAIDs"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Systemic drugs are common triggers for the onset or severe exacerbation of psoriasis. The classic, high-yield ''big four'' culprits are Lithium (bipolar therapy), Beta-blockers (cardiovascular therapy), Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine/chloroquine), and NSAIDs. Systemic steroid withdrawal can also precipitate a life-threatening rebound flare of generalized pustular psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Immediately after receiving an intramuscular (IM) injection of Penicillin G benzathine in the upper outer quadrant of the buttock, a 26-year-old man develops excruciating, burning pain and livedo-like, blue-gray discoloration around the injection site. Within 48 hours, this area develops deep, necrotic sloughing of the skin and underlying gluteal muscle. What is this clinical entity?', '["An IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction", "Warfarin-induced skin necrosis", "Necrotizing fasciitis from bacterial contamination", "Nicolau Syndrome (Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa)", "Arthus reaction from vaccine immune complex deposition"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nicolau Syndrome (also known as Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa) is a rare, devastating non-allergic vascular complication of intramuscular injections. It occurs due to accidental intra-arterial injection, peri-arterial injection, or drug-induced vasospasm/thrombosis. It presents with immediate severe pain, retiform cyanosis, and subsequent deep tissue necrosis of the skin, fat, and muscle.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing chemotherapy for leukemia is treated with G-CSF (Granulocyte Colony-Stimulating Factor) to manage neutropenia. Three days later, they develop a high fever (39.5 C) and several painful, bright red, ''succulent'' or ''juicy'' plaques on their neck and upper back. Biopsy reveals a massive, dense neutrophilic infiltrate in the upper dermis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["DRESS syndrome", "Sweet''s Syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis)", "Pyoderma Gangrenosum", "AGEP", "Erysipelas"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sweet''s syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis) is characterized by high fever, leukocytosis, and tender, erythematous plaques that are clinically described as ''juicy'' or ''succulent'' due to profound upper dermal edema. Histology shows a dense dermal neutrophilic infiltrate without vasculitis. G-CSF is a classic drug trigger because it directly stimulates neutrophil production.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old woman being treated with Paclitaxel for breast cancer develops bilateral, woody thickening and hardening of the skin of their lower legs and forearms. Physical exam shows marked induration, but there is an absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon or nail fold capillary changes, and systemic organ involvement is absent. ANA testing is negative. What is the diagnosis?', '["Stasis dermatitis", "Taxane-induced scleroderma-like changes", "Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis", "Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma)", "Eosinophilic Fasciitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Taxanes (Paclitaxel, Docetaxel) can induce scleroderma-like skin changes (induration, woody thickening) typically localized to the extremities. The key clinical clues to distinguish this drug-induced form from idiopathic Systemic Sclerosis are the absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon, normal nail fold capillaries, negative autoantibodies (ANA/Scl-70), and lack of visceral sclerosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 19-year-old female taking Vemurafenib (a BRAF inhibitor) for metastatic melanoma develops several fast-growing, dome-shaped nodules with central keratinous craters on their arms within 3 weeks of starting the drug. Biopsy of one lesion shows a well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma/keratoacanthoma (SCC/KA). What is the mechanism driving these secondary skin malignancies?', '["A metastatic spread of the primary melanoma to the skin", "Paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do NOT have the BRAF mutation", "Upregulation of the Hedgehog pathway in follicular basal cells", "Direct mutagenic damage to epidermal stem cell DNA by the drug molecule", "Systemic immunosuppression leading to explosive reactivation of HPV-16"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Vemurafenib and other BRAF inhibitors cause paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal cells that do not harbor the BRAF V600E mutation. The drug binds to wild-type BRAF, causing it to homodimerize and hyper-activate downstream MEK/ERK signaling, driving rapid keratinocyte proliferation and leading to eruptive Keratoacanthomas and low-grade Squamous Cell Carcinomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old boy on Sorafenib (a multi-kinase inhibitor) for renal cell carcinoma develops thick, painful, yellow calluses surrounded by a bright red halo on the heels and lateral aspects of their feet. How does this Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) clinically differ from Capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS)?', '["HFSR presents as localized hyperkeratosis and thick calluses on pressure points, whereas classic HFS is characterized by diffuse, symmetric erythema and peeling", "HFSR strictly affects the fingernails and spares the skin", "HFSR is completely painless and resolves within 24 hours of starting the drug", "There is no clinical difference between the two reactions", "Classic HFS is caused by direct exposure to sunlight"]'::jsonb, 0, 'While both affect the hands/feet, Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) is clinically distinct from capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS). HFSR is characterized by localized, hyperkeratotic, thick yellow plaques and blisters on high-friction pressure points (heels/lateral sole), while classic HFS (capecitabine) presents as diffuse, confluent erythema, edema, and desquamation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old female with chronic renal insufficiency receives intravenous iodinated contrast for a CT scan. Two days later, they develop painful, rapidly expanding, pustular, vegetating plaques on their face and lower extremities. Pustule culture is negative. Biopsy shows massive epidermal hyperplasia (pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia) and dense neutrophilic microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Blastomycosis", "Pyoderma Gangrenosum", "Iododerma (Halogenoderma)", "Sweet''s syndrome", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Halogenodermas (Iododerma and Bromoderma) are rare cutaneous toxic accumulations of halogens, most commonly occurring in patients with impaired renal clearance. Clinically, they present as highly inflammatory, pustular, vegetating plaques that can closely mimic Pyoderma Gangrenosum, deep fungal infections (like Blastomycosis), or Squamous Cell Carcinoma. Histology shows marked pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department with a painful rash, high fever, and severe eye soreness that began 2 weeks after starting Allopurinol. Physical exam reveals confluent, atypical targetoid macules with flaccid blisters and sloughing covering approximately 8% of the total body surface area (BSA). Two mucosal sites are severely involved. Skin biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis with minimal dermal inflammation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "Erythema Multiforme Major"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS), defined by fever, mucosal involvement of $\ge 2$ sites, targetoid macules, and epidermal detachment affecting <10% of the body surface area (BSA). Biopsy characteristically shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis (apoptosis of keratinocytes) driven by cytotoxic T-cells and granulysin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old boy on Phenytoin for seizures presents with fever and widespread skin tenderness. Within 24 hours, the skin begins to peel off in large sheets. Physical examination shows dusky erythema progressing to confluent blisters. Gentle lateral pressure on the erythematous skin causes the epidermis to slide off the dermis (positive Nikolsky sign). The epidermal detachment is estimated at 35% of the total body surface area. What is the primary cause of mortality in this condition?', '["Hepatocellular failure and acute encephalopathy", "Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis", "Infection, sepsis, and multi-organ failure", "High-output cardiac failure and hypothermia", "Acute kidney injury from severe myoglobinuria"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN), characterized by epidermal detachment of >30% BSA and a positive Nikolsky sign. The loss of the skin barrier leads to massive fluid shifts, hypothermia, and high-output heart failure, but the leading cause of death in these patients is infection (sepsis) and subsequent multi-organ failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old man presents with a painful, blistering rash and fever. They recently started Sulfasalazine for inflammatory bowel disease. Physical examination reveals atypical targetoid plaques on the trunk and extremities, with flaccid bullae that slide off under lateral pressure. The surface area of epidermal detachment is measured at exactly 18% of the total BSA. What is the clinical category of this patient''s drug reaction?', '["Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "DRESS syndrome", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The SJS/TEN spectrum is categorized by the percentage of body surface area showing epidermal detachment: SJS involves <10% BSA detachment; SJS/TEN Overlap involves 10% to 30% BSA detachment; and TEN involves >30% BSA detachment. At 18% detachment, this patient falls into the SJS/TEN Overlap category.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old girl of East Asian descent is about to start Carbamazepine for neuropathic pain. The clinician orders genetic screening for the HLA-B*1502 allele before prescribing. What is the clinical significance of this allele?', '["It is a marker for drug-induced phototoxicity and exaggerated sunburns", "It correlates with an increased risk of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia", "It carries an extremely high risk of Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in Asian populations", "It indicates an increased risk of severe, irreversible agranulocytosis", "It is associated with rapid metabolic clearance of Carbamazepine, making it ineffective"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The HLA-B*1502 allele is strongly associated with an increased risk of developing Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in individuals of East Asian and South-East Asian descent. Screening is mandatory or highly recommended prior to starting Carbamazepine in these high-risk populations.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old boy with Han Chinese ancestry is diagnosed with gout and is about to start Allopurinol therapy. Which HLA allele is strongly associated with Allopurinol-induced Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) in this population?', '["HLA-B*5701", "HLA-B*5801", "HLA-DRB1*0401", "HLA-B*2705", "HLA-B*1502"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The HLA-B*5801 allele is a potent genetic risk factor for Allopurinol-induced severe cutaneous adverse reactions, including SJS and TEN, particularly in Han Chinese, Thai, and Korean populations. HLA-B*1502 is associated with carbamazepine, and HLA-B*5701 is linked to abacavir hypersensitivity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old girl with Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) involving 45% BSA is admitted to the hospital. Which of the following clinical settings is the most appropriate for the management of this patient?', '["An infectious disease isolation room", "A specialized Burn Unit or Intensive Care Unit (ICU)", "A standard dermatology inpatient ward", "A clean private room in a general medical ward", "An outpatient clinic with daily nurse home visits"]'::jsonb, 1, 'TEN is a life-threatening dermatologic emergency. Due to extensive skin loss, patients require meticulous sterile wound care, specialized non-adherent dressings, tight fluid resuscitation, and temperature regulation identical to severe burn victims. Management in a specialized Burn Unit or intensive care setting significantly reduces mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old man began taking Carbamazepine 4 weeks ago. They now present with a high fever (39.0 C), a widespread red skin eruption, and severe facial and periorbital swelling. Physical exam reveals confluent morbilliform plaques on the trunk, palpable lymphadenopathy in the neck and axillae, and moderate tender hepatomegaly. Lab work shows a WBC of 16,500/mm3 with 18% eosinophils (absolute eosinophil count 2,970/mm3) and AST/ALT elevated at 4 times the upper limit of normal. What is the diagnosis?', '["Serum Sickness", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Exanthematous (Morbilliform) Drug Eruption", "Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS) syndrome", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'DRESS syndrome (also known as Drug-Induced Hypersensitivity Syndrome) is a severe, life-threatening drug reaction characterized by a long latency period (2-8 weeks), high fever, a diffuse erythematous eruption, facial/periorbital edema, lymphadenopathy, hematologic abnormalities (eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytosis), and visceral organ involvement—most commonly hepatitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is diagnosed with DRESS syndrome after starting Phenytoin 6 weeks ago. Aside from stopping the offending drug, what virus reactivation is frequently associated with the pathogenesis and flare-ups of this systemic drug hypersensitivity?', '["Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)", "Hepatitis C Virus (HCV)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'DRESS syndrome is strongly associated with the sequential reactivation of latent herpesviruses, most notably Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6), as well as HHV-7, EBV, and CMV. This viral reactivation is thought to drive the prolonged, recurrent course of the disease even after the causative drug has been completely cleared.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old man is started on Cephalexin for a suspected soft tissue infection. Forty-eight hours later, they develop a high fever (39.2 C) and a burning rash that started in the groin and axillae. Physical exam reveals diffuse, bright erythema studded with hundreds of non-follicular, sterile, pinpoint pustules. Lab work shows a marked leukocytosis with neutrophilia (WBC 19,000/mm3). Pustule cultures are sterile. What is the diagnosis?', '["Subcorneal Pustular Dermatosis", "Disseminated candidiasis", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "Generalized Pustular Psoriasis (Von Zumbusch)", "DRESS syndrome"]'::jsonb, 2, 'AGEP is a rare, severe cutaneous reaction characterized by the rapid onset (typically <48 hours after drug exposure) of high fever and hundreds of small, sterile, non-follicular pustules arising on a background of edematous erythema, classically starting in flexural folds (groin/axillae). Beta-lactams and macrolides are the most common triggers. Leukocytosis with marked neutrophilia is characteristic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old female presents with a dark, round spot on their left forearm. They note that every time they take Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection, the exact same spot becomes red, swollen, and itchy, occasionally forming a small central blister. Once it heals, a persistent dusky brown patch remains. What is the mechanism of this ''fixing'' to the same anatomic site?', '["Persistence of resident memory CD8+ T-cells in the affected epidermis", "Slow lymphatic clearance of the drug from that specific site", "Deposition of circulating IgG immune complexes in the local capillary bed", "Localized accumulation of IgE molecules on dermal mast cells", "Localized somatic mutation in the keratinocytes of that area"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Fixed Drug Eruption (FDE) is characterized by recurrent erythematous plaques that appear in the exact same localized site (skin or mucosa) upon re-exposure to the offending drug. This site-specific memory is mediated by resident memory CD8+ T-cells that remain quiescent in the basal epidermis of the affected plaque and rapidly degranulate upon systemic re-exposure to the drug.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 26-year-old boy presents with a single, highly circumscribed, dusky-red, itchy patch on their glans penis that developed 24 hours after taking a medication. Which of the following drugs is the most common trigger for a fixed drug eruption (FDE) affecting the genitalia?', '["Metformin", "Aspirin", "Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (Sulfonamides)", "Atorvastatin", "Amlodipine"]'::jsonb, 2, 'While FDE can be triggered by many drugs (including NSAIDs and tetracyclines), sulfonamides—most notably Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole—are the most common triggers for fixed drug eruptions located on the genitalia (glans penis or labia).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old man is started on Warfarin for a new deep vein thrombosis. Due to an oversight, no Heparin bridge is administered. On day 3 of therapy, the patient develops severe pain and dark purple, well-demarcated patches on their breasts and thighs. Within 24 hours, these lesions progress to necrotic black eschars. What is the primary molecular cause of this reaction?', '["An IgE-mediated anaphylactoid reaction causing localized vasculitis", "A drop in antithrombin III levels leading to arterial spasm", "Direct toxic necrosis of adipocytes caused by accumulation of the warfarin molecule", "A rapid drop in Protein C levels (due to its short half-life), causing a transient hypercoagulable state and microthrombosis", "Localized vasoconstriction from paradoxical alpha-adrenergic stimulation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Warfarin-induced skin necrosis occurs early in therapy (typically days 3-5) when warfarin is initiated without a heparin bridge, especially in patients with hereditary Protein C or S deficiency. Protein C has a very short half-life (approx 8 hours) and falls rapidly, while procoagulant factors II, IX, and X fall slowly. This creates a transient, severe hypercoagulable state leading to microthrombosis in the capillaries supplying fat-rich areas (breasts, thighs, buttocks).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient on subcutaneous Unfractionated Heparin therapy for a pulmonary embolism develops painful, red-to-purple patches at their abdominal injection sites 7 days into therapy, which quickly progress to localized skin necrosis. Lab work reveals a platelet count that has dropped from 280,000/mm3 to 90,000/mm3. What is the target of the autoantibodies driving this clinical presentation?', '["Desmoglein 1 and Desmoglein 3 in the epidermis", "Protein C and Protein S", "Type XVII Collagen (BP180) in the basement membrane", "Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes", "The G-protein coupled thrombin receptor"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT) presenting with localized heparin-induced skin necrosis at the injection sites. HIT is mediated by IgG autoantibodies targeting Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes, which bind and activate platelets, causing both thrombocytopenia (clearance of platelets) and a severe, life-threatening prothrombotic state.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient develops heparin-induced skin necrosis and thrombocytopenia (HIT). Which of the following is the most critical and immediate next step in management?', '["Increase the heparin dose to overcome the antibody-mediated clearance", "Apply high-potency topical steroids to the necrotic injection sites", "Stop heparin, administer Vitamin K, and start oral warfarin immediately", "Discontinue all heparin products immediately and initiate a non-heparin anticoagulant (e.g., Argatroban)", "Transfuse 2 units of platelets and continue subcutaneous heparin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'When HIT is suspected or confirmed (due to a platelet drop >50% and/or skin necrosis), the immediate action must be to stop all heparin products (including low-molecular-weight heparin and heparin flushes) and start an alternative, non-heparin rapid anticoagulant (e.g., a direct thrombin inhibitor like Argatroban or Bivalirudin) to prevent catastrophic thromboembolism. Warfarin is contraindicated in active HIT as it can precipitate gangrene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old male being treated with Hydralazine for severe hypertension presents with joint pain, fever, and a mild malar-like rash on their face. Lab results show a positive ANA (1:640) and highly elevated Anti-Histone antibodies. Anti-dsDNA and Anti-Smith antibodies are negative. Urinalysis and serum creatinine are normal. What is the clinical significance of anti-histone antibodies in this patient?', '["They indicate severe, silent lupus nephritis requiring renal biopsy", "They are highly sensitive (approx 95%) for drug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE)", "They are caused by a cross-reaction with the hydralazine molecule itself", "They represent a high risk of transition to Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis", "They are highly specific for idiopathic systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Anti-histone antibodies are highly sensitive (present in 90-95% of cases) for Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE). Common triggers include Hydralazine, Procainamide, and Isoniazid. Unlike idiopathic SLE, DILE rarely involves the kidneys or central nervous system, and anti-dsDNA or anti-Smith antibodies are usually absent.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old girl being treated with D-Penicillamine for Wilson''s disease develops flaccid blisters on their chest and upper back, along with painful sores in their mouth. Physical exam shows a positive Nikolsky sign. A skin biopsy reveals intraepidermal acantholysis. Direct Immunofluorescence (DIF) demonstrates intercellular IgG deposition in a ''fishnet'' pattern throughout the epidermis. What is the target of the autoantibodies in this drug-induced condition?', '["Desmogleins (Dsg1 and Dsg3)", "Integrin alpha-6 beta-4 in the hemidesmosomes", "Type VII Collagen in the anchoring fibrils", "Laminin-332 in the lamina lucida", "BP180 (Type XVII Collagen) and BP230"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient has Drug-Induced Pemphigus, characteristically triggered by thiol (sulfhydryl) group-containing drugs like D-Penicillamine, Captopril, and Gold salts. Thiol groups can biochemically disrupt desmosomes or act as haptens. The autoantibodies target desmoglein 1 and/or 3, leading to flaccid blisters, mucosal lesions, acantholysis, and a ''fishnet'' DIF pattern.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 26-year-old female being treated with a Beta-blocker for hypertension presents with an extremely itchy rash on their forearms and shins. Physical examination reveals symmetric, flat-topped, polygonal, purple papules. Fine white lines (Wickham striae) are visible on the surface under magnification. How does this drug-induced lichenoid eruption typically differ from classic, idiopathic Lichen Planus?', '["It is highly infectious and spreads to close contacts", "It is completely non-pruritic and does not show interface dermatitis on biopsy", "It presents with large, flaccid blisters and a positive Nikolsky sign", "It is often more generalized, symmetric, and frequently lacks mucosal involvement or classic Wickham striae", "It selectively targets the face and spares the extremities"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Lichenoid drug eruptions (triggered by beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors, thiazides, and gold) clinically mimic Lichen Planus. However, they typically differ by being more generalized, symmetric, and having a eczematous or psoriasiform appearance, while frequently sparing the oral mucosa and showing a slower resolution after drug withdrawal.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old man presents with an explosive onset of acne-like bumps on their chest and upper back. They deny having any acne as a teenager. Physical exam reveals dozens of monomorphic (all at the same stage of development) erythematous inflammatory papules and pustules. No blackheads or whiteheads (comedones) are present. The patient was recently treated with a high-dose oral prednisone taper. What is the diagnosis?', '["Steroid-induced acne (acneiform eruption)", "Pityrosporum folliculitis", "Papulopustular rosacea", "Acne vulgaris (papulopustular subtype)", "Hot tub folliculitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Steroid acne is an acneiform drug eruption caused by systemic or high-potency topical corticosteroids. Clinically, it is characterized by a sudden, monomorphic crop of erythematous papules and pustules on the trunk and shoulders. Unlike acne vulgaris, steroid acne lacks comedones (blackheads/whiteheads) and the lesions are all at the identical stage of development.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient receiving a rapid intravenous infusion of Vancomycin suddenly develops severe flushing, pruritus, and a bright red rash over their face, neck, and upper torso. Their heart rate is 115/min and blood pressure is 98/60 mmHg. There is no wheezing, lip swelling, or hives. What is the primary mechanism of this reaction?', '["Type III hypersensitivity with deposition of IgA immune complexes in dermal vessels", "Direct, non-immunologic histamine release from mast cells triggered by the rapid drug infusion", "T-cell mediated cytotoxic destruction of endothelial cells", "Direct toxic effect of the vancomycin molecule on vascular smooth muscle", "IgE-mediated Type I hypersensitivity reaction causing systemic anaphylaxis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is Red Man Syndrome, a pseudo-allergic (anaphylactoid) reaction caused by a rapid infusion of intravenous Vancomycin. The drug directly degranulates mast cells and basophils in a non-immunologic fashion, releasing histamine. It is not a true IgE-mediated allergy. It can be prevented by slowing the infusion rate (to over 60 minutes) and pre-treating with antihistamines.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 6-year-old child presents with a widespread, hive-like rash, severe joint pain and swelling of the ankles, and a low-grade fever that began 10 days after starting Cefaclor for an ear infection. Lab work shows a normal platelet count and no proteinuria. What is the diagnosis?', '["True Serum Sickness", "Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR)", "Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura (IgA Vasculitis)", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome", "Acute Rheumatic Fever"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Serum Sickness-Like Reaction (SSLR) is a benign, transient reaction that classically occurs in children 1-3 weeks after starting certain drugs, most famously Cefaclor, as well as penicillins and sulfonamides. It mimics true serum sickness with fever, arthralgias, and urticarial plaques, but crucially LACKS circulating immune complexes, complement depletion, vasculitis, and renal damage (proteinuria).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old female being treated with high-dose Capecitabine for metastatic colorectal cancer presents with painful redness, swelling, and tingling on their palms and soles. Physical examination reveals symmetric, bright erythema and edema of the palmar and plantar surfaces, with early desquamation. What is the clinical term for this condition?', '["Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (Hand-Foot Syndrome)", "Hand-Foot-and-Mouth Disease", "Contact Dermatitis", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Fixed Drug Eruption"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Palmar-Plantar Erythrodysesthesia (also known as Hand-Foot Syndrome) is a common cutaneous side effect of several chemotherapy agents, most classically Capecitabine, 5-Fluorouracil (5-FU), and Cytarabine. It is caused by drug leaking from small capillaries into the thick skin of the palms and soles, leading to direct toxicity, pain, symmetric erythema, swelling, and peeling.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old man being treated with Cetuximab (an EGFR inhibitor) for lung cancer develops an itchy, acne-like rash consisting of papules and pustules on their face, neck, and upper chest. Comedones are completely absent. What is the clinical significance of this rash?', '["It represents a deep follicular bacterial infection requiring intravenous antibiotics", "It is a sign of tumor progression and therapeutic failure", "It is caused by secondary adrenal insufficiency from the chemotherapy", "It is a positive prognostic indicator (the development and severity of the rash correlate with a favorable tumor response)", "It indicates a life-threatening drug allergy and the Cetuximab must be stopped permanently"]'::jsonb, 3, 'EGFR inhibitor-induced acneiform (papulopustular) eruption is a highly common and expected class-effect of drugs like Cetuximab, Erlotinib, and Panitumumab. Counter-intuitively, the development and severity of this rash act as a positive surrogate marker for drug efficacy, correlating with improved tumor response and patient survival. It is managed with topical/oral antibiotics (doxycycline) and steroids without stopping the chemotherapy.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old man who has been taking Minocycline for severe acne for 3 years presents with a new, muddy, blue-gray discoloration on their shins and the mucosal surface of their lower gums. A skin biopsy reveals iron-containing and melanin-containing pigment deposits within the dermis. What is the subtype and characteristic appearance of this minocycline-induced hyperpigmentation?', '["Type I Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, limited strictly to sites of prior inflammation and scarring", "Type III Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, presenting as a diffuse muddy-brown tan in sun-exposed areas", "Argiria, caused by silver deposition in the eccrine glands", "Type II Minocycline Hyperpigmentation, affecting normal skin of the shins and forearms, as well as gums", "Slate-gray facial pigmentation induced by drug-induced lipofuscin"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Minocycline can induce three distinct types of skin hyperpigmentation: Type I occurs strictly in scars and areas of prior inflammation (blue-black pigment); Type II occurs on normal skin, classically the shins, forearms, and gums (slate-blue/gray pigment containing iron and melanin); and Type III is a diffuse, muddy-brown hyperpigmentation in sun-exposed areas (pure melanin).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old boy with a history of refractory cardiac arrhythmias has been taking Amiodarone for 5 years. They present to the clinic complaining of a blue-gray discoloration of their face, nose, and ears. Biopsy of the affected skin reveals lipofuscin-like granules within dermal macrophages. What is the key environmental trigger required for the development of this specific pigmentation?', '["High dietary intake of vitamin C", "Chronic mechanical friction", "Exposure to cold weather (Raynaud-like)", "Frequent hot water baths", "Ultraviolet (UV) light exposure (sunlight)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Amiodarone-induced hyperpigmentation is a classic side effect occurring in patients on long-term, high-dose amiodarone therapy. It presents as a slate-gray to blue-violet discoloration strictly in sun-exposed areas (face, nose, ears). Ultraviolet (UV) light is required to trigger the deposition of a drug-lipid complex (lipofuscin) inside dermal macrophages.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male with systemic lupus erythematosus has been taking Hydroxychloroquine for 6 years. During a routine skin check, the dermatologist notes well-demarcated, slate-gray patches on the patient''s bilateral shins and a bluish-gray discoloration of the hard palate. What is the prognosis and clinical course of this pigmentation?', '["It is slowly reversible over months to years after stopping the hydroxychloroquine, though some areas may persist", "It is highly infectious and can be transmitted to others", "It is completely permanent and will continue to spread even if the drug is discontinued", "It indicates an immediate risk of acute hepatic failure", "It resolves spontaneously within 2 weeks of stopping the drug"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Skin and mucosal hyperpigmentation occurs in up to 25% of patients on long-term antimalarials (Chloroquine/Hydroxychloroquine). Classic sites are the shins (pretibial area), the hard palate, and the nail beds. The pigmentation is slowly reversible over many months or years after drug cessation, though some localized areas may remain permanently.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 32-year-old woman undergoing chemotherapy for Hodgkin''s lymphoma develops intensely pruritic, linear, criss-crossing red streaks on her back and chest that resemble ''whip marks''. She admits to scratching her skin due to dryness. Which of the following chemotherapy agents is the classic cause of this flagellate erythema?', '["Bleomycin", "Cyclophosphamide", "Paclitaxel", "5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)", "Doxorubicin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Flagellate erythema is a virtually pathognomonic cutaneous toxicity associated with Bleomycin therapy. It presents as highly characteristic, linear, criss-crossing erythematous streaks that look like ''whip marks'' on the back and trunk. It represents a Koebner-like response where minor scratching or friction concentrates the drug in the local skin vessels.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male presents with progressive, painless, firm, fibrous swelling of their gums that has begun to cover the crowns of their teeth. They have a history of epilepsy. Which of the following triads of medications represents the classic causes of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia?', '["Metformin, Lisinopril, and Atorvastatin", "Penicillin, Doxycycline, and Cephalexin", "Prednisone, Isotretinoin, and Spironolactone", "Phenytoin, Cyclosporine, and Nifedipine", "Aspirin, Acetaminophen, and Ibuprofen"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Drug-induced gingival hyperplasia is a classic side effect characterized by fibrous overgrowth of the gingival papillae. The three classic, high-yield culprits are Phenytoin (anti-epileptic), Cyclosporine (immunosuppressant), and Calcium Channel Blockers (especially Nifedipine, but also Amlodipine/Diltiazem).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 45-year-old girl taking Naproxen for osteoarthritic pain presents with skin fragility and multiple small, tense vesicles and erosions on the dorsum of their hands. Biopsy reveals a subepidermal blister with minimal inflammatory cell infiltrate. Laboratory workup demonstrates normal levels of urinary and fecal porphyrins, ruling out Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT). What is the diagnosis?', '["Pseudoporphyria", "Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT)", "Bullous Pemphigoid", "Allergic Contact Dermatitis", "Epidermolysis Bullosa Acquisita"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Pseudoporphyria is a drug-induced condition that clinically and histologically mimics Porphyria Cutanea Tarda (PCT), presenting with skin fragility, blistering, and scarring on sun-exposed hands. Crucially, porphyrin levels in the blood, urine, and stool are completely normal in pseudoporphyria. NSAIDs (most famously Naproxen) are the leading cause.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old child presents with high fever, severe mucosal crusting and ulceration of the lips and mouth, and conjunctival redness with purulent discharge. Physical exam reveals less than 1% BSA of skin detachment with only a few targetoid macules on the limbs. The child was treated for ''walking pneumonia'' 1 week ago. What is the diagnosis?', '["Erythema Multiforme Major", "Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS)", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "Primary Herpes Simplex Gingivostomatitis", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Mycoplasma-Induced Rash and Mucositis (MIRAS) is triggered by Mycoplasma pneumoniae infection, most commonly in children and young adults. It is characterized by severe mucositis involving $\ge 2$ mucosal sites (typically oral and ocular) with minimal or absent cutaneous lesions (<1% BSA skin involvement). It mimics SJS but has a distinct pathogenesis and carries a significantly better prognosis with lower mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old patient presents with painful, dark purple, necrotic, star-shaped (retiform) patches on their bilateral earlobes, cheeks, and lower extremities. Lab work reveals a severe neutropenia (WBC 1,200/mm3 with an absolute neutrophil count of 350/mm3). A urine drug screen is positive for Cocaine. What is the causative agent responsible for this patient''s necrotic ear skin and bone marrow suppression?', '["Amiodarone-induced photo-vasculitis", "A contamination of cocaine with Streptococcus pyogenes", "An IgE-mediated drug allergy to benzoylecgonine", "Levamisole (a veterinary dewormer used as a cocaine cutting agent)", "Direct vasoconstrictive toxicity of the cocaine molecule itself"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Levamisole-induced retiform purpura. Levamisole is a veterinary anthelmintic commonly used as an adulterant (cutting agent) in illicit cocaine. It triggers a unique, severe autoimmune vasculitis characterized by painful, retiform (star-shaped) purpura with central necrosis, characteristically involving the ears/earlobes and face. It is also a potent bone marrow suppressant, leading to severe neutropenia/agranulocytosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 26-year-old man being treated with Hydralazine for 8 years presents with fever, joint pain, weight loss, and palpable purple spots (purpura) on their lower legs. Lab results show a positive ANA, dual positivity for p-ANCA (MPO) and c-ANCA (PR3) antibodies, and urinalysis reveals proteinuria and hematuria with red blood cell casts. What is the diagnosis?', '["Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE)", "Henoch-Sch\u00f6nlein Purpura", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis", "Granulomatosis with Polyangiitis (GPA)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Drug-induced ANCA-associated vasculitis is a severe necrotizing vasculitis classically triggered by long-term use of Hydralazine, Propylthiouracil (PTU), or Methimazole. Unlike simple drug-induced lupus (DILE), drug-induced ANCA vasculitis features high titers of p-ANCA (MPO) and PR3, palpable purpura, and significant internal organ involvement—most notably rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (renal failure).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old man on Captopril (an ACE inhibitor) develops a widespread scaly rash on their trunk. They report a large, oval, scaly ''herald patch'' appeared on their chest first, followed by smaller oval plaques along the skin tension lines on their back in a ''Christmas tree'' distribution. How does this drug-induced Pityriasis Rosea-like eruption typically differ from idiopathic Pityriasis Rosea?', '["It is accompanied by severe kidney damage and proteinuria", "It often lacks a distinct initial herald patch and takes much longer to resolve after stopping the drug", "It strictly affects the face and spares the trunk", "It is highly contagious and spreads to children via respiratory droplets", "It presents with deep, tense bullae and a positive Nikolsky sign"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Pityriasis Rosea-like drug eruptions (triggered by ACE inhibitors, gold, beta-blockers, and metronidazole) closely mimic classic PR. However, the drug-induced form typically lacks the single large ''herald patch'' that precedes the main eruption, shows more confluent and inflammatory lesions, and is much more persistent, resolving slowly only after the offending drug is discontinued.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient with a history of stable, mild plaque psoriasis is diagnosed with bipolar disorder and started on Lithium. Two weeks later, they present with an explosive, severe flare of psoriasis, with thick, silvery plaques now covering 40% of their BSA. Which of the following lists represents the ''big four'' systemic drugs most famous for flaring or worsening psoriasis?', '["Amlodipine, Warfarin, Heparin, and Aspirin", "Statins, Fibrates, Metformin, and Lisinopril", "Isotretinoin, Spironolactone, Cyclosporine, and Prednisone", "Lithium, Beta-blockers, Antimalarials (hydroxychloroquine), and NSAIDs", "Penicillins, Cephalosporins, Macrolides, and Sulfonamides"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Systemic drugs are common triggers for the onset or severe exacerbation of psoriasis. The classic, high-yield ''big four'' culprits are Lithium (bipolar therapy), Beta-blockers (cardiovascular therapy), Antimalarials (e.g., hydroxychloroquine/chloroquine), and NSAIDs. Systemic steroid withdrawal can also precipitate a life-threatening rebound flare of generalized pustular psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Immediately after receiving an intramuscular (IM) injection of Penicillin G benzathine in the upper outer quadrant of the buttock, a 28-year-old girl develops excruciating, burning pain and livedo-like, blue-gray discoloration around the injection site. Within 48 hours, this area develops deep, necrotic sloughing of the skin and underlying gluteal muscle. What is this clinical entity?', '["Arthus reaction from vaccine immune complex deposition", "Nicolau Syndrome (Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa)", "An IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction", "Necrotizing fasciitis from bacterial contamination", "Warfarin-induced skin necrosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nicolau Syndrome (also known as Embolia Cutis Medicamentosa) is a rare, devastating non-allergic vascular complication of intramuscular injections. It occurs due to accidental intra-arterial injection, peri-arterial injection, or drug-induced vasospasm/thrombosis. It presents with immediate severe pain, retiform cyanosis, and subsequent deep tissue necrosis of the skin, fat, and muscle.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient undergoing chemotherapy for leukemia is treated with G-CSF (Granulocyte Colony-Stimulating Factor) to manage neutropenia. Three days later, they develop a high fever (39.5 C) and several painful, bright red, ''succulent'' or ''juicy'' plaques on their neck and upper back. Biopsy reveals a massive, dense neutrophilic infiltrate in the upper dermis without vasculitis. What is the diagnosis?', '["Erysipelas", "AGEP", "Sweet''s Syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis)", "Pyoderma Gangrenosum", "DRESS syndrome"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sweet''s syndrome (Acute Febrile Neutrophilic Dermatosis) is characterized by high fever, leukocytosis, and tender, erythematous plaques that are clinically described as ''juicy'' or ''succulent'' due to profound upper dermal edema. Histology shows a dense dermal neutrophilic infiltrate without vasculitis. G-CSF is a classic drug trigger because it directly stimulates neutrophil production.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old female being treated with Paclitaxel for breast cancer develops bilateral, woody thickening and hardening of the skin of their lower legs and forearms. Physical exam shows marked induration, but there is an absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon or nail fold capillary changes, and systemic organ involvement is absent. ANA testing is negative. What is the diagnosis?', '["Taxane-induced scleroderma-like changes", "Systemic Sclerosis (Scleroderma)", "Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis", "Eosinophilic Fasciitis", "Stasis dermatitis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Taxanes (Paclitaxel, Docetaxel) can induce scleroderma-like skin changes (induration, woody thickening) typically localized to the extremities. The key clinical clues to distinguish this drug-induced form from idiopathic Systemic Sclerosis are the absence of Raynaud''s phenomenon, normal nail fold capillaries, negative autoantibodies (ANA/Scl-70), and lack of visceral sclerosis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old girl taking Vemurafenib (a BRAF inhibitor) for metastatic melanoma develops several fast-growing, dome-shaped nodules with central keratinous craters on their arms within 3 weeks of starting the drug. Biopsy of one lesion shows a well-differentiated squamous cell carcinoma/keratoacanthoma (SCC/KA). What is the mechanism driving these secondary skin malignancies?', '["Direct mutagenic damage to epidermal stem cell DNA by the drug molecule", "A metastatic spread of the primary melanoma to the skin", "Systemic immunosuppression leading to explosive reactivation of HPV-16", "Upregulation of the Hedgehog pathway in follicular basal cells", "Paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal keratinocytes that do NOT have the BRAF mutation"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Vemurafenib and other BRAF inhibitors cause paradoxical activation of the MAP kinase pathway in normal cells that do not harbor the BRAF V600E mutation. The drug binds to wild-type BRAF, causing it to homodimerize and hyper-activate downstream MEK/ERK signaling, driving rapid keratinocyte proliferation and leading to eruptive Keratoacanthomas and low-grade Squamous Cell Carcinomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old female on Sorafenib (a multi-kinase inhibitor) for renal cell carcinoma develops thick, painful, yellow calluses surrounded by a bright red halo on the heels and lateral aspects of their feet. How does this Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) clinically differ from Capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS)?', '["HFSR strictly affects the fingernails and spares the skin", "HFSR is completely painless and resolves within 24 hours of starting the drug", "HFSR presents as localized hyperkeratosis and thick calluses on pressure points, whereas classic HFS is characterized by diffuse, symmetric erythema and peeling", "There is no clinical difference between the two reactions", "Classic HFS is caused by direct exposure to sunlight"]'::jsonb, 2, 'While both affect the hands/feet, Sorafenib-induced Hand-Foot Skin Reaction (HFSR) is clinically distinct from capecitabine-induced Hand-Foot Syndrome (HFS). HFSR is characterized by localized, hyperkeratotic, thick yellow plaques and blisters on high-friction pressure points (heels/lateral sole), while classic HFS (capecitabine) presents as diffuse, confluent erythema, edema, and desquamation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old boy with chronic renal insufficiency receives intravenous iodinated contrast for a CT scan. Two days later, they develop painful, rapidly expanding, pustular, vegetating plaques on their face and lower extremities. Pustule culture is negative. Biopsy shows massive epidermal hyperplasia (pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia) and dense neutrophilic microabscesses. What is the diagnosis?', '["Sweet''s syndrome", "Blastomycosis", "Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma", "Iododerma (Halogenoderma)", "Pyoderma Gangrenosum"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Halogenodermas (Iododerma and Bromoderma) are rare cutaneous toxic accumulations of halogens, most commonly occurring in patients with impaired renal clearance. Clinically, they present as highly inflammatory, pustular, vegetating plaques that can closely mimic Pyoderma Gangrenosum, deep fungal infections (like Blastomycosis), or Squamous Cell Carcinoma. Histology shows marked pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 26-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department with a painful rash, high fever, and severe eye soreness that began 2 weeks after starting Allopurinol. Physical exam reveals confluent, atypical targetoid macules with flaccid blisters and sloughing covering approximately 8% of the total body surface area (BSA). Two mucosal sites are severely involved. Skin biopsy shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis with minimal dermal inflammation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Erythema Multiforme Major", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This patient has Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS), defined by fever, mucosal involvement of $\ge 2$ sites, targetoid macules, and epidermal detachment affecting <10% of the body surface area (BSA). Biopsy characteristically shows full-thickness epidermal necrosis (apoptosis of keratinocytes) driven by cytotoxic T-cells and granulysin.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 20-year-old boy on Phenytoin for seizures presents with fever and widespread skin tenderness. Within 24 hours, the skin begins to peel off in large sheets. Physical examination shows dusky erythema progressing to confluent blisters. Gentle lateral pressure on the erythematous skin causes the epidermis to slide off the dermis (positive Nikolsky sign). The epidermal detachment is estimated at 35% of the total body surface area. What is the primary cause of mortality in this condition?', '["Acute kidney injury from severe myoglobinuria", "High-output cardiac failure and hypothermia", "Hepatocellular failure and acute encephalopathy", "Infection, sepsis, and multi-organ failure", "Pulmonary embolism from deep vein thrombosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN), characterized by epidermal detachment of >30% BSA and a positive Nikolsky sign. The loss of the skin barrier leads to massive fluid shifts, hypothermia, and high-output heart failure, but the leading cause of death in these patients is infection (sepsis) and subsequent multi-organ failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 50-year-old male presents with a painful, blistering rash and fever. They recently started Sulfasalazine for inflammatory bowel disease. Physical examination reveals atypical targetoid plaques on the trunk and extremities, with flaccid bullae that slide off under lateral pressure. The surface area of epidermal detachment is measured at exactly 18% of the total BSA. What is the clinical category of this patient''s drug reaction?', '["DRESS syndrome", "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN)", "SJS/TEN Overlap Syndrome", "Erythema Multiforme Major", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The SJS/TEN spectrum is categorized by the percentage of body surface area showing epidermal detachment: SJS involves <10% BSA detachment; SJS/TEN Overlap involves 10% to 30% BSA detachment; and TEN involves >30% BSA detachment. At 18% detachment, this patient falls into the SJS/TEN Overlap category.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old female of East Asian descent is about to start Carbamazepine for neuropathic pain. The clinician orders genetic screening for the HLA-B*1502 allele before prescribing. What is the clinical significance of this allele?', '["It is associated with rapid metabolic clearance of Carbamazepine, making it ineffective", "It correlates with an increased risk of drug-induced gingival hyperplasia", "It indicates an increased risk of severe, irreversible agranulocytosis", "It carries an extremely high risk of Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in Asian populations", "It is a marker for drug-induced phototoxicity and exaggerated sunburns"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The HLA-B*1502 allele is strongly associated with an increased risk of developing Carbamazepine-induced SJS and TEN in individuals of East Asian and South-East Asian descent. Screening is mandatory or highly recommended prior to starting Carbamazepine in these high-risk populations.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old woman with Han Chinese ancestry is diagnosed with gout and is about to start Allopurinol therapy. Which HLA allele is strongly associated with Allopurinol-induced Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) and Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) in this population?', '["HLA-B*1502", "HLA-B*5801", "HLA-B*5701", "HLA-DRB1*0401", "HLA-B*2705"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The HLA-B*5801 allele is a potent genetic risk factor for Allopurinol-induced severe cutaneous adverse reactions, including SJS and TEN, particularly in Han Chinese, Thai, and Korean populations. HLA-B*1502 is associated with carbamazepine, and HLA-B*5701 is linked to abacavir hypersensitivity.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old female with Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) involving 45% BSA is admitted to the hospital. Which of the following clinical settings is the most appropriate for the management of this patient?', '["A standard dermatology inpatient ward", "A clean private room in a general medical ward", "An infectious disease isolation room", "A specialized Burn Unit or Intensive Care Unit (ICU)", "An outpatient clinic with daily nurse home visits"]'::jsonb, 3, 'TEN is a life-threatening dermatologic emergency. Due to extensive skin loss, patients require meticulous sterile wound care, specialized non-adherent dressings, tight fluid resuscitation, and temperature regulation identical to severe burn victims. Management in a specialized Burn Unit or intensive care setting significantly reduces mortality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 26-year-old man began taking Carbamazepine 4 weeks ago. They now present with a high fever (39.0 C), a widespread red skin eruption, and severe facial and periorbital swelling. Physical exam reveals confluent morbilliform plaques on the trunk, palpable lymphadenopathy in the neck and axillae, and moderate tender hepatomegaly. Lab work shows a WBC of 16,500/mm3 with 18% eosinophils (absolute eosinophil count 2,970/mm3) and AST/ALT elevated at 4 times the upper limit of normal. What is the diagnosis?', '["Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "Exanthematous (Morbilliform) Drug Eruption", "Drug Reaction with Eosinophilia and Systemic Symptoms (DRESS) syndrome", "Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)", "Serum Sickness"]'::jsonb, 2, 'DRESS syndrome (also known as Drug-Induced Hypersensitivity Syndrome) is a severe, life-threatening drug reaction characterized by a long latency period (2-8 weeks), high fever, a diffuse erythematous eruption, facial/periorbital edema, lymphadenopathy, hematologic abnormalities (eosinophilia, atypical lymphocytosis), and visceral organ involvement—most commonly hepatitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient is diagnosed with DRESS syndrome after starting Phenytoin 6 weeks ago. Aside from stopping the offending drug, what virus reactivation is frequently associated with the pathogenesis and flare-ups of this systemic drug hypersensitivity?', '["Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6)", "Human Papillomavirus Type 16 (HPV-16)", "Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)", "Hepatitis C Virus (HCV)", "Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'DRESS syndrome is strongly associated with the sequential reactivation of latent herpesviruses, most notably Human Herpesvirus 6 (HHV-6), as well as HHV-7, EBV, and CMV. This viral reactivation is thought to drive the prolonged, recurrent course of the disease even after the causative drug has been completely cleared.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old woman is started on Cephalexin for a suspected soft tissue infection. Forty-eight hours later, they develop a high fever (39.2 C) and a burning rash that started in the groin and axillae. Physical exam reveals diffuse, bright erythema studded with hundreds of non-follicular, sterile, pinpoint pustules. Lab work shows a marked leukocytosis with neutrophilia (WBC 19,000/mm3). Pustule cultures are sterile. What is the diagnosis?', '["Generalized Pustular Psoriasis (Von Zumbusch)", "Acute Generalized Exanthematous Pustulosis (AGEP)", "DRESS syndrome", "Disseminated candidiasis", "Subcorneal Pustular Dermatosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'AGEP is a rare, severe cutaneous reaction characterized by the rapid onset (typically <48 hours after drug exposure) of high fever and hundreds of small, sterile, non-follicular pustules arising on a background of edematous erythema, classically starting in flexural folds (groin/axillae). Beta-lactams and macrolides are the most common triggers. Leukocytosis with marked neutrophilia is characteristic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 28-year-old female presents with a dark, round spot on their left forearm. They note that every time they take Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole for a urinary tract infection, the exact same spot becomes red, swollen, and itchy, occasionally forming a small central blister. Once it heals, a persistent dusky brown patch remains. What is the mechanism of this ''fixing'' to the same anatomic site?', '["Slow lymphatic clearance of the drug from that specific site", "Localized accumulation of IgE molecules on dermal mast cells", "Deposition of circulating IgG immune complexes in the local capillary bed", "Persistence of resident memory CD8+ T-cells in the affected epidermis", "Localized somatic mutation in the keratinocytes of that area"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Fixed Drug Eruption (FDE) is characterized by recurrent erythematous plaques that appear in the exact same localized site (skin or mucosa) upon re-exposure to the offending drug. This site-specific memory is mediated by resident memory CD8+ T-cells that remain quiescent in the basal epidermis of the affected plaque and rapidly degranulate upon systemic re-exposure to the drug.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male presents with a single, highly circumscribed, dusky-red, itchy patch on their glans penis that developed 24 hours after taking a medication. Which of the following drugs is the most common trigger for a fixed drug eruption (FDE) affecting the genitalia?', '["Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (Sulfonamides)", "Atorvastatin", "Aspirin", "Metformin", "Amlodipine"]'::jsonb, 0, 'While FDE can be triggered by many drugs (including NSAIDs and tetracyclines), sulfonamides—most notably Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole—are the most common triggers for fixed drug eruptions located on the genitalia (glans penis or labia).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 55-year-old male is started on Warfarin for a new deep vein thrombosis. Due to an oversight, no Heparin bridge is administered. On day 3 of therapy, the patient develops severe pain and dark purple, well-demarcated patches on their breasts and thighs. Within 24 hours, these lesions progress to necrotic black eschars. What is the primary molecular cause of this reaction?', '["A drop in antithrombin III levels leading to arterial spasm", "An IgE-mediated anaphylactoid reaction causing localized vasculitis", "Localized vasoconstriction from paradoxical alpha-adrenergic stimulation", "A rapid drop in Protein C levels (due to its short half-life), causing a transient hypercoagulable state and microthrombosis", "Direct toxic necrosis of adipocytes caused by accumulation of the warfarin molecule"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Warfarin-induced skin necrosis occurs early in therapy (typically days 3-5) when warfarin is initiated without a heparin bridge, especially in patients with hereditary Protein C or S deficiency. Protein C has a very short half-life (approx 8 hours) and falls rapidly, while procoagulant factors II, IX, and X fall slowly. This creates a transient, severe hypercoagulable state leading to microthrombosis in the capillaries supplying fat-rich areas (breasts, thighs, buttocks).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient on subcutaneous Unfractionated Heparin therapy for a pulmonary embolism develops painful, red-to-purple patches at their abdominal injection sites 7 days into therapy, which quickly progress to localized skin necrosis. Lab work reveals a platelet count that has dropped from 280,000/mm3 to 90,000/mm3. What is the target of the autoantibodies driving this clinical presentation?', '["Protein C and Protein S", "Desmoglein 1 and Desmoglein 3 in the epidermis", "Type XVII Collagen (BP180) in the basement membrane", "The G-protein coupled thrombin receptor", "Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Heparin-Induced Thrombocytopenia (HIT) presenting with localized heparin-induced skin necrosis at the injection sites. HIT is mediated by IgG autoantibodies targeting Platelet Factor 4 (PF4)-Heparin complexes, which bind and activate platelets, causing both thrombocytopenia (clearance of platelets) and a severe, life-threatening prothrombotic state.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A patient develops heparin-induced skin necrosis and thrombocytopenia (HIT). Which of the following is the most critical and immediate next step in management?', '["Transfuse 2 units of platelets and continue subcutaneous heparin", "Apply high-potency topical steroids to the necrotic injection sites", "Discontinue all heparin products immediately and initiate a non-heparin anticoagulant (e.g., Argatroban)", "Increase the heparin dose to overcome the antibody-mediated clearance", "Stop heparin, administer Vitamin K, and start oral warfarin immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'When HIT is suspected or confirmed (due to a platelet drop >50% and/or skin necrosis), the immediate action must be to stop all heparin products (including low-molecular-weight heparin and heparin flushes) and start an alternative, non-heparin rapid anticoagulant (e.g., a direct thrombin inhibitor like Argatroban or Bivalirudin) to prevent catastrophic thromboembolism. Warfarin is contraindicated in active HIT as it can precipitate gangrene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old woman being treated with Hydralazine for severe hypertension presents with joint pain, fever, and a mild malar-like rash on their face. Lab results show a positive ANA (1:640) and highly elevated Anti-Histone antibodies. Anti-dsDNA and Anti-Smith antibodies are negative. Urinalysis and serum creatinine are normal. What is the clinical significance of anti-histone antibodies in this patient?', '["They are caused by a cross-reaction with the hydralazine molecule itself", "They represent a high risk of transition to Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis", "They are highly sensitive (approx 95%) for drug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE)", "They are highly specific for idiopathic systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)", "They indicate severe, silent lupus nephritis requiring renal biopsy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Anti-histone antibodies are highly sensitive (present in 90-95% of cases) for Drug-Induced Lupus Erythematosus (DILE). Common triggers include Hydralazine, Procainamide, and Isoniazid. Unlike idiopathic SLE, DILE rarely involves the kidneys or central nervous system, and anti-dsDNA or anti-Smith antibodies are usually absent.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old man being treated with D-Penicillamine for Wilson''s disease develops flaccid blisters on their chest and upper back, along with painful sores in their mouth. Physical exam shows a positive Nikolsky sign. A skin biopsy reveals intraepidermal acantholysis. Direct Immunofluorescence (DIF) demonstrates intercellular IgG deposition in a ''fishnet'' pattern throughout the epidermis. What is the target of the autoantibodies in this drug-induced condition?', '["BP180 (Type XVII Collagen) and BP230", "Type VII Collagen in the anchoring fibrils", "Laminin-332 in the lamina lucida", "Integrin alpha-6 beta-4 in the hemidesmosomes", "Desmogleins (Dsg1 and Dsg3)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'This patient has Drug-Induced Pemphigus, characteristically triggered by thiol (sulfhydryl) group-containing drugs like D-Penicillamine, Captopril, and Gold salts. Thiol groups can biochemically disrupt desmosomes or act as haptens. The autoantibodies target desmoglein 1 and/or 3, leading to flaccid blisters, mucosal lesions, acantholysis, and a ''fishnet'' DIF pattern.');
